const productCaseLocales={"vanta":{"uk":{"name":"Monday Balance","industry":"Фінансові технології","platform":"iOS / Android","location":"New York, USA","role":"Дизайнер UX/UI · Дизайнер продукту","scope":"Дослідження продукту · Інформаційна архітектура · Дизайн взаємодії · Дизайн інтерфейсу користувача · Прототипування","strapline":"Зробіть особисті фінанси зрозумілими","sections":[["Огляд проекту","Monday Balance – це програма для управління особистими фінансами, розроблена, щоб допомогти людям зрозуміти звички витрачати кошти, організувати бюджет і приймати більш обґрунтовані фінансові рішення. Продукт зосереджений на розриві між записом транзакцій і розумінням їх значення.","Робота охоплювала дослідження продуктів, стратегію UX, інформаційну архітектуру, дизайн взаємодії, дизайн інтерфейсу користувача та створення прототипів."],["Контекст і визначення проблеми","Багато фінансових програм наражають користувачів на складні інформаційні панелі, надмірну категоризацію та облікову термінологію. Користувачі могли бачити окремі транзакції, але їм було важко розпізнавати ширші закономірності, пов’язувати щоденні витрати з довгостроковими цілями або підтримувати систему бюджетування, яка вимагала постійних зусиль."],["Користувачі та їхні потреби","Продукт розглядав молодих професіоналів із постійним доходом, фрілансерів із непостійним доходом та цілеспрямованих вкладників. Їх практичні запитання були простими: чи можу я собі це дозволити? Куди поділися мої гроші? Який прогрес я досягаю? Ці питання сформували ієрархію більше, ніж фінансова термінологія."],["Дослідження та відкриття","Discovery зосереджувався на особистих фінансових буднях і на моментах, коли людям дійсно потрібна інформація. Ключовим розумінням дизайну були когнітивні зусилля: продукт мав спочатку відповісти на миттєве запитання, а потім запропонувати глибший аналіз, коли користувач вирішив дослідити його."],["Стратегія UX та інформаційна архітектура","Додаток було організовано навколо п’яти напрямків: Огляд, Діяльність, Бюджети, Цілі та Статистика. Огляд став основною точкою взаємодії, показуючи поточний баланс, щомісячні витрати, залишок бюджету та одну відповідну інформацію замість усіх доступних показників."],["Ключові дизайнерські рішення","Прогресивне розкриття перемістило детальну аналітику на вторинні екрани. Візуальне відстеження бюджету замінило щільні таблиці з позначеними індикаторами прогресу. Гнучке управління доходами підтримувало як фіксовані зарплати, так і нерегулярні доходи. Швидке додавання транзакцій, запропоновані категорії та багаторазові шаблони зменшили кількість повторюваних вводів."],["Інтерфейс користувача та дизайн взаємодії","Темні поверхні, стримані акценти, великий шрифт і великі інтервали створили більш спокійну альтернативу інтерфейсам торгової платформи. Важливі значення отримали чіткий типографічний пріоритет. Порожні стани, незавершені транзакції, попередження та некольорові підказки стану розглядалися як частина основного досвіду."],["Ітерація та еволюція дизайну","На першій інформаційній панелі забагато аналітичних карток змагалися за увагу. Структуру було спрощено: другорядні показники перемістилися в розширювані подання, тоді як на головному екрані зберігалася інформація, безпосередньо пов’язана з повсякденними рішеннями. Досвід заощаджень також був переосмислений навколо досягнення цілей, а не історії транзакцій."],["Кінцевий продукт і результати","Кінцевий мобільний продукт поєднує щоденне управління витратами, планування бюджету, цілі заощаджень і фінансову аналітику. Його дизайн поєднує короткострокові дії з довгостроковими цілями, не вимагаючи знань бухгалтерського обліку."],["Рефлексія та компетенції","Визначальним завданням було визначити, яка інформація заслуговує на увагу, а не складати фінансові діаграми. Monday Balance демонструє мобільний UX, візуалізацію фінансових даних, інформаційну архітектуру, прогресивне розкриття інформації, доступний інтерфейс користувача та продуктове мислення."]]},"pl":{"name":"Monday Balance","industry":"Technologia finansowa","platform":"iOS/Androida","location":"New York, USA","role":"Projektant UX/UI · Projektant produktu","scope":"Badania produktu · Architektura informacji · Projektowanie interakcji · Projektowanie interfejsu użytkownika · Prototypowanie","strapline":"Sprawienie, że finanse osobiste będą zrozumiałe","sections":[["Przegląd projektu","Monday Balance to aplikacja do zarządzania finansami osobistymi, zaprojektowana, aby pomóc ludziom zrozumieć nawyki związane z wydatkami, organizować budżety i podejmować bardziej świadome decyzje finansowe. Produkt koncentruje się na luce pomiędzy rejestrowaniem transakcji a zrozumieniem, co te transakcje oznaczają.","Prace obejmowały badania produktu, strategię UX, architekturę informacji, projektowanie interakcji, projektowanie UI i prototypowanie."],["Kontekst i definicja problemu","Wiele aplikacji finansowych naraża użytkowników na skomplikowane dashboardy, nadmierną kategoryzację i terminologię księgową. Użytkownicy mogli zobaczyć pojedyncze transakcje, ale mieli trudności z rozpoznaniem szerszych wzorców, powiązaniem codziennych wydatków z celami długoterminowymi lub utrzymaniem systemu budżetowania wymagającego ciągłego wysiłku."],["Użytkownicy i ich potrzeby","W produkcie wzięli udział młodzi profesjonaliści o regularnych dochodach, freelancerzy osiągający nieregularne dochody oraz oszczędzający zorientowani na cel. Ich praktyczne pytania były proste: Czy mnie na to stać? Gdzie poszły moje pieniądze? Jakie robię postępy? Te pytania ukształtowały hierarchię w większym stopniu niż terminologia finansowa."],["Badania i odkrycia","Odkrycie skupiło się na osobistych rutynach finansowych i momentach, w których ludzie rzeczywiście potrzebują informacji. Kluczowym założeniem projektu był wysiłek poznawczy: produkt musiał najpierw odpowiedzieć na bezpośrednie pytanie, a następnie zaoferować głębszą analizę, gdy użytkownik zdecydował się z nim zapoznać."],["Strategia UX i architektura informacji","Aplikacja została zorganizowana wokół pięciu miejsc docelowych: Przegląd, Aktywność, Budżety, Cele i Wnioski. Przegląd stał się głównym punktem interakcji, pokazując bieżące saldo, miesięczne wydatki, pozostały budżet i jeden istotny wgląd w zamiast wszystkich dostępnych wskaźników."],["Kluczowe decyzje projektowe","Stopniowe ujawnianie przeniosło szczegółowe analizy na ekrany dodatkowe. Wizualne śledzenie budżetu zastąpiło gęste tabele oznaczonymi wskaźnikami postępu. Elastyczne zarządzanie dochodami wspierało zarówno pensje stałe, jak i dochody nieregularne. Szybkie dodawanie transakcji, sugerowane kategorie i szablony do wielokrotnego użytku zmniejszają powtarzalne tarcia związane z wprowadzaniem danych."],["Projekt interfejsu użytkownika i interakcji","Ciemne powierzchnie, powściągliwe akcenty, duża czcionka i duże odstępy stworzyły spokojniejszą alternatywę dla interfejsów platform handlowych. Ważne wartości otrzymały wyraźny priorytet typograficzny. Puste stany, niekompletne transakcje, ostrzeżenia i niekolorowe wskaźniki statusu uznano za część podstawowego doświadczenia."],["Iteracja i ewolucja projektu","W pierwszym dashboardzie zbyt wiele kart analitycznych rywalizowało o uwagę. Struktura została uproszczona: drugorzędne metryki przeniesiono do rozwijalnych widoków, natomiast na głównym ekranie przechowywano informacje bezpośrednio związane z codziennymi decyzjami. Doświadczenie związane z oszczędzaniem zostało również przeformułowane w oparciu o postęp celów, a nie historię transakcji."],["Produkt końcowy i elementy dostawy","Ostateczny produkt mobilny łączy w sobie codzienne zarządzanie wydatkami, planowanie budżetu, cele oszczędnościowe i analitykę finansową. Jego konstrukcja łączy krótkoterminowe działania z celami długoterminowymi bez konieczności posiadania wiedzy księgowej."],["Refleksja i kompetencje","Najważniejszym zadaniem było podjęcie decyzji, które informacje zasługują na uwagę, a nie rysowanie wykresów finansowych. Monday Balance demonstruje mobilny UX, wizualizację danych finansowych, architekturę informacji, stopniowe ujawnianie informacji, dostępny interfejs użytkownika i myślenie o produkcie."]]},"ru":{"name":"Monday Balance","industry":"Финансовые технологии","platform":"iOS/Андроид","location":"New York, USA","role":"UX/UI дизайнер · Дизайнер продуктов","scope":"Исследование продукта · Информационная архитектура · Дизайн взаимодействия · Дизайн пользовательского интерфейса · Прототипирование","strapline":"Делаем личные финансы понятными","sections":[["Обзор проекта","Monday Balance — это приложение для управления личными финансами, призванное помочь людям понять привычки расходования средств, организовать бюджеты и принимать более обоснованные финансовые решения. Продукт фокусируется на разрыве между записью транзакций и пониманием того, что означают эти транзакции.","Работа охватывала исследование продуктов, UX-стратегию, информационную архитектуру, дизайн взаимодействия, дизайн пользовательского интерфейса и прототипирование."],["Контекст и определение проблемы","Многие финансовые приложения предоставляют пользователям сложные информационные панели, чрезмерную категоризацию и учетную терминологию. Пользователи могли видеть отдельные транзакции, но с трудом могли распознать более широкие закономерности, связать повседневные расходы с долгосрочными целями или поддерживать систему бюджетирования, которая требовала постоянных усилий."],["Пользователи и их потребности","В продукте рассматривались молодые специалисты с регулярным доходом, фрилансеры с нерегулярным доходом и целеустремленные вкладчики. Их практические вопросы были простыми: могу ли я себе это позволить? Куда пошли мои деньги? Какого прогресса я добиваюсь? Эти вопросы сформировали иерархию больше, чем финансовая терминология."],["Исследования и открытия","Discovery сосредоточился на личных финансовых делах и на моментах, когда людям действительно нужна информация. Ключевой идеей дизайна было когнитивное усилие: продукт должен был сначала ответить на непосредственный вопрос, а затем предложить более глубокий анализ, когда пользователь решил его изучить."],["UX-стратегия и информационная архитектура","Приложение было организовано по пяти направлениям: «Обзор», «Действия», «Бюджеты», «Цели» и «Аналитика». Обзор стал основной точкой взаимодействия, показывая текущий баланс, ежемесячные расходы, оставшийся бюджет и одну важную информацию вместо всех доступных показателей."],["Ключевые проектные решения","Постепенное раскрытие информации переместило подробную аналитику на второстепенные экраны. Визуальное отслеживание бюджета заменило плотные таблицы с маркированными индикаторами прогресса. Гибкое управление доходами поддерживало как фиксированную заработную плату, так и нерегулярный доход. Быстрое добавление транзакций, предлагаемые категории и шаблоны многократного использования уменьшили необходимость повторяющегося ввода."],["Дизайн пользовательского интерфейса и взаимодействия","Темные поверхности, сдержанные акценты, крупный шрифт и большие интервалы создали более спокойную альтернативу интерфейсам торговых платформ. Важные ценности получили четкий типографский приоритет. Пустые состояния, незавершенные транзакции, предупреждения и нецветные сигналы статуса рассматривались как часть основного опыта."],["Итерация и эволюция дизайна","На первой информационной панели слишком много аналитических карточек конкурировали за внимание. Структура была упрощена: второстепенные метрики переместились в расширяемые представления, а на главном экране хранилась информация, непосредственно связанная с повседневными решениями. Опыт сбережений также был переосмыслен вокруг достижения цели, а не истории транзакций."],["Конечный продукт и результаты","Конечный мобильный продукт объединяет ежедневное управление расходами, планирование бюджета, цели экономии и финансовую аналитику. Его конструкция соединяет краткосрочные действия с долгосрочными целями, не требуя знаний бухгалтерского учета."],["Рефлексия и компетенции","Определяющей задачей было решить, какая информация заслуживает внимания, а не рисовать финансовые графики. Monday Balance демонстрирует мобильный UX, визуализацию финансовых данных, информационную архитектуру, прогрессивное раскрытие информации, доступный пользовательский интерфейс и продуктовое мышление."]]},"de":{"name":"Monday Balance","industry":"Finanztechnologie","platform":"iOS / Android","location":"New York, USA","role":"UX/UI-Designer · Produktdesigner","scope":"Produktforschung · Informationsarchitektur · Interaktionsdesign · UI-Design · Prototyping","strapline":"Persönliche Finanzen verständlich machen","sections":[["Projektübersicht","Monday Balance ist eine persönliche Finanzverwaltungsanwendung, die Menschen dabei helfen soll, ihre Ausgabegewohnheiten zu verstehen, Budgets zu organisieren und fundiertere Finanzentscheidungen zu treffen. Das Produkt konzentriert sich auf die Lücke zwischen der Aufzeichnung von Transaktionen und dem Verständnis, was diese Transaktionen bedeuten.","Die Arbeit umfasste Produktforschung, UX-Strategie, Informationsarchitektur, Interaktionsdesign, UI-Design und Prototyping."],["Kontext- und Problemdefinition","Bei vielen Finanzanwendungen sind Benutzer mit komplexen Dashboards, übermäßiger Kategorisierung und Buchhaltungsterminologie konfrontiert. Benutzer konnten einzelne Transaktionen sehen, hatten jedoch Schwierigkeiten, umfassendere Muster zu erkennen, alltägliche Ausgaben mit langfristigen Zielen zu verknüpfen oder ein Budgetierungssystem aufrechtzuerhalten, das ständigen Aufwand erforderte."],["Benutzer und ihre Bedürfnisse","Das Produkt berücksichtigt Berufseinsteiger mit regelmäßigem Einkommen, Freiberufler mit unregelmäßigem Einkommen und zielorientierte Sparer. Ihre praktischen Fragen waren einfach: Kann ich mir das leisten? Wo ist mein Geld geblieben? Welche Fortschritte mache ich? Diese Fragen prägten die Hierarchie stärker als die Finanzterminologie."],["Forschung und Entdeckung","Discovery konzentrierte sich auf persönliche Finanzroutinen und auf die Momente, in denen Menschen tatsächlich Informationen benötigen. Die wichtigste Designerkenntnis war der kognitive Aufwand: Das Produkt musste zunächst eine unmittelbare Frage beantworten und dann eine tiefergehende Analyse bieten, wenn der Benutzer es erkunden wollte."],["UX-Strategie und Informationsarchitektur","Die Anwendung war nach fünf Zielen organisiert: Übersicht, Aktivität, Budgets, Ziele und Einblicke. Die Übersicht wurde zum primären Interaktionspunkt und zeigt den aktuellen Saldo, die monatlichen Ausgaben, das verbleibende Budget und eine relevante Erkenntnis anstelle jeder verfügbaren Metrik."],["Wichtige Designentscheidungen","Durch die fortschreitende Offenlegung wurden detaillierte Analysen auf sekundäre Bildschirme verlagert. Die visuelle Budgetverfolgung ersetzte dichte Tabellen durch beschriftete Fortschrittsindikatoren. Das flexible Einkommensmanagement unterstützte sowohl Festgehälter als auch unregelmäßige Einkünfte. Das schnelle Hinzufügen von Transaktionen, vorgeschlagene Kategorien und wiederverwendbare Vorlagen reduzierten die Reibung bei wiederholten Eingaben."],["UI- und Interaktionsdesign","Dunkle Oberflächen, zurückhaltende Akzente, große Schrift und großzügige Abstände schufen eine ruhigere Alternative zu den Schnittstellen der Handelsplattform. Wichtige Werte erhielten klare typografische Priorität. Leere Zustände, unvollständige Transaktionen, Warnungen und nicht farbige Statushinweise wurden als Teil des Kernerlebnisses berücksichtigt."],["Iteration und Designentwicklung","Beim ersten Dashboard konkurrierten zu viele Analysekarten um Aufmerksamkeit. Die Struktur wurde vereinfacht: Sekundärmetriken wurden in erweiterbare Ansichten verschoben, während auf dem Hauptbildschirm Informationen angezeigt wurden, die in direktem Zusammenhang mit alltäglichen Entscheidungen standen. Das Sparerlebnis wurde außerdem auf den Zielfortschritt und nicht auf die Transaktionshistorie ausgerichtet."],["Endprodukt und Lieferungen","Das endgültige mobile Produkt vereint tägliches Ausgabenmanagement, Budgetplanung, Sparziele und Finanzanalysen. Sein Design verbindet kurzfristige Maßnahmen mit längerfristigen Zielen, ohne dass Buchhaltungskenntnisse erforderlich sind."],["Reflexion & Kompetenzen","Die entscheidende Aufgabe bestand darin, zu entscheiden, welche Informationen Aufmerksamkeit verdienen, und nicht darin, Finanzdiagramme zu erstellen. Monday Balance demonstriert mobile UX, Finanzdatenvisualisierung, Informationsarchitektur, progressive Offenlegung, barrierefreie Benutzeroberfläche und Produktdenken."]]}},"vetra":{"uk":{"name":"Willow Practice","industry":"Ветеринарна охорона здоров'я / SaaS","platform":"Чуйний веб-додаток","location":"Utrecht, Netherlands","role":"Дизайнер UX/UI · Дизайнер продукту","scope":"Аналіз робочих процесів · Інформаційна архітектура · Дизайн панелі керування · Потоки користувачів · Прототипування","strapline":"Розробка більш ефективного ветеринарного робочого процесу","sections":[["Огляд проекту","Willow Practice — це платформа для управління ветеринарною практикою, яка централізує запис на прийом, записи тварин, історії лікування та внутрішні операції клініки. Він підтримує невеликі клініки, де обов’язки розподілені між адміністраторами, ветеринарами та адміністраторами."],["Контекст і визначення проблеми","Невеликі практики часто поєднують паперові записи, електронні таблиці, обмін повідомленнями та відключені календарі. Інформацію про пацієнтів стає важко отримати, подальше лікування відстежується вручну, а власникам бракує консолідованого перегляду щоденних операцій."],["Користувачі та їхні потреби","Адміністратори призначають зустрічі, ведуть календарі, реєструють пацієнтів і спілкуються з власниками. Ветеринари переглядають історію, документують обстеження, записують діагнози та керують планами лікування. Адміністратори клініки керують графіками, послугами, звітністю та дозволами."],["Дослідження та відкриття","Розробка почалася з відображення візиту до ветеринара від реєстрації та планування до прибуття, консультації, документації лікування, реєстрації платежів і планування подальшого спостереження. Аналіз відокремив швидку адміністративну взаємодію від комплексного медичного контексту, необхідного під час консультацій."],["Інформаційна архітектура","Шість модулів організовують платформу: інформаційна панель, календар, пацієнти, клієнти, лікування та адміністрування. Записи пацієнтів є центральним об’єктом, що поєднує призначення, власників, історію лікування та подальші дії."],["Ключові дизайнерські рішення","Уніфікований профіль пацієнта групує ідентифікацію, дані власника, історію хвороби та майбутні зустрічі в хронологічному порядку. Календар із розділеним переглядом забезпечує видимість контексту зустрічі. Рольова навігація зменшує нерелевантні дії, тоді як структурована консультація записує окремі симптоми, знахідки, діагнози та рекомендації."],["Інтерфейс і візуальне спрямування","Нейтральні поверхні, стримані блакитні акценти, узгоджені індикатори стану та чітка типографіка забезпечують спокійну та читабельну роботу. Медична інформація залишається візуально відмінною від адміністративної, звертаючи увагу на попередження, неповні записи, конфлікти та використання планшетів у кабінетах для консультацій."],["Ітерація та еволюція дизайну","Перший профіль пацієнта надавав інформацію про власника та медичні записи однакового візуального пріоритету. Його було переглянуто, щоб на передньому плані була історія хвороби під час консультацій, зберігаючи доступ до інформації про власника. Створення записів на зустріч було спрощено завдяки зменшенню обов’язкових полів на першому кроці та дозволу заповнення деталей пізніше."],["Кінцевий продукт і результати","Willow Practice об’єднує записи пацієнтів, планування, робочі процеси персоналу та документацію лікування в одній системі. Результатом є більш передбачуваний шлях між пов’язаними записами та менше відключених адміністративних кроків."],["Рефлексія та компетенції","Willow Practice вимагала надійності та інформаційної щільності, а не візуальної новизни. Він демонструє дизайн B2B SaaS, UX для охорони здоров’я, складні інформаційні панелі, рольові інтерфейси, архітектуру робочого процесу та оперативне мислення продукту."]]},"pl":{"name":"Willow Practice","industry":"Weterynaryjna opieka zdrowotna / SaaS","platform":"Responsywna aplikacja internetowa","location":"Utrecht, Netherlands","role":"Projektant UX/UI · Projektant produktu","scope":"Analiza przepływu pracy · Architektura informacji · Projektowanie dashboardów · Przepływy użytkowników · Prototypowanie","strapline":"Projektowanie bardziej wydajnego przepływu pracy w weterynarii","sections":[["Przegląd projektu","Willow Practice to platforma do zarządzania praktyką weterynaryjną, która centralizuje wizyty, dokumentację zwierząt, historię leczenia i wewnętrzne operacje kliniki. Wspiera małe kliniki, w których obowiązki dzielone są pomiędzy recepcjonistki, lekarzy weterynarii i administratorów."],["Kontekst i definicja problemu","Małe praktyki często łączą dokumentację papierową, arkusze kalkulacyjne, wiadomości i niepowiązane kalendarze. Odzyskanie informacji o pacjencie staje się trudne, dalsze leczenie jest śledzone ręcznie, a właścicielom brakuje skonsolidowanego obrazu codziennych operacji."],["Użytkownicy i ich potrzeby","Recepcjoniści tworzą spotkania, zarządzają kalendarzami, rejestrują pacjentów i komunikują się z właścicielami. Weterynarze przeglądają historie choroby, badają dokumenty, rejestrują diagnozy i zarządzają planami leczenia. Administratorzy kliniki zarządzają harmonogramami, usługami, raportami i uprawnieniami."],["Badania i odkrycia","Projekt rozpoczął się od mapowania wizyt weterynaryjnych od rejestracji i planowania, poprzez przybycie, konsultacje, dokumentację leczenia, rejestrację płatności i planowanie dalszych działań. W analizie oddzielono szybkie interakcje administracyjne od kompleksowego kontekstu medycznego potrzebnego podczas konsultacji."],["Architektura Informacji","Platformę organizuje sześć modułów: Panel, Kalendarz, Pacjenci, Klienci, Zabiegi i Administracja. Rejestry pacjentów pełnią rolę centralnego obiektu łączącego wizyty, właścicieli, historię leczenia i działania następcze."],["Kluczowe decyzje projektowe","Ujednolicony profil pacjenta pozwala na chronologiczną identyfikację grup pacjentów, dane właściciela, historię medyczną i nadchodzące wizyty. Kalendarz z podzielonym widokiem zapewnia widoczność kontekstu spotkań. Nawigacja oparta na rolach ogranicza niepotrzebne działania, podczas gdy ustrukturyzowane konsultacje rejestrują oddzielne objawy, ustalenia, diagnozę i zalecenia."],["Interfejs użytkownika i kierunek wizualny","Neutralne powierzchnie, powściągliwe akcenty turkusu, spójne wskaźniki statusu i wyraźna typografia sprzyjają spokojnej, czytelnej pracy. Informacje medyczne wizualnie różnią się od informacji administracyjnych, ze szczególnym uwzględnieniem ostrzeżeń, niekompletnych zapisów, konfliktów i używania tabletów w gabinetach lekarskich."],["Iteracja i ewolucja projektu","W przypadku pierwszego profilu pacjenta informacje o właścicielu i dokumentacja medyczna miały równy priorytet wizualny. Poprawiono go tak, aby podczas konsultacji na pierwszym planie znajdowała się historia medyczna, przy jednoczesnym zapewnieniu dostępności danych właściciela. Tworzenie spotkań zostało uproszczone poprzez zmniejszenie wymaganych pól w pierwszym kroku i umożliwienie uzupełnienia szczegółów w późniejszym terminie."],["Produkt końcowy i elementy dostawy","Willow Practice łączy dokumentację pacjentów, harmonogramy, przepływ pracy personelu i dokumentację leczenia w jednym systemie. Rezultatem jest bardziej przewidywalna ścieżka między powiązanymi rekordami i mniej niepołączonych kroków administracyjnych."],["Refleksja i kompetencje","Projekt Willow Practice wymagał raczej niezawodności i gęstości informacji niż nowości wizualnej. Demonstruje projektowanie B2B SaaS, UX opieki zdrowotnej, złożone dashboardy, interfejsy oparte na rolach, architekturę przepływu pracy i operacyjne myślenie o produkcie."]]},"ru":{"name":"Willow Practice","industry":"Ветеринарное здравоохранение / SaaS","platform":"Адаптивное веб-приложение","location":"Utrecht, Netherlands","role":"UX/UI дизайнер · Дизайнер продуктов","scope":"Анализ рабочих процессов · Информационная архитектура · Разработка информационной панели · Потоки пользователей · Создание прототипов","strapline":"Разработка более эффективного ветеринарного рабочего процесса","sections":[["Обзор проекта","Willow Practice — это платформа управления ветеринарной практикой, которая централизует записи на прием, учет животных, истории лечения и внутренние операции клиники. Он поддерживает небольшие клиники, где обязанности разделены между регистраторами, ветеринарами и администраторами."],["Контекст и определение проблемы","Небольшие практики часто сочетают в себе бумажные записи, электронные таблицы, обмен сообщениями и автономные календари. Информацию о пациентах становится трудно получить, последующий уход отслеживается вручную, а владельцам не хватает консолидированного представления о ежедневных операциях."],["Пользователи и их потребности","Администраторы создают записи на прием, управляют календарями, регистрируют пациентов и общаются с владельцами. Ветеринары просматривают истории болезни, документируют обследования, записывают диагнозы и составляют планы лечения. Администраторы клиники управляют расписаниями, услугами, отчетами и разрешениями."],["Исследования и открытия","Разработка началась с составления карты визита к ветеринару, от регистрации и планирования до прибытия, консультации, оформления документации по лечению, регистрации платежей и планирования последующего наблюдения. Анализ отделил быстрое административное взаимодействие от комплексного медицинского контекста, необходимого во время консультаций."],["Информационная архитектура","Платформу организуют шесть модулей: «Панель управления», «Календарь», «Пациенты», «Клиенты», «Лечение» и «Администрирование». Записи пациентов выступают в качестве центрального объекта, связывающего назначения, владельцев, историю лечения и последующие действия."],["Ключевые проектные решения","Единый профиль пациента группирует идентификацию, данные владельца, историю болезни и предстоящие встречи в хронологическом порядке. Календарь с разделенным представлением позволяет видеть контекст встречи. Ролевая навигация сокращает количество ненужных действий, а структурированные консультации фиксируют отдельные симптомы, выводы, диагнозы и рекомендации."],["Пользовательский интерфейс и визуальное направление","Нейтральные поверхности, сдержанные бирюзовые акценты, последовательные индикаторы статуса и четкая типографика обеспечивают спокойную и удобочитаемую работу. Медицинская информация визуально отличается от административной информации, при этом уделяется внимание предупреждениям, неполным записям, конфликтам и использованию планшетов в консультационных кабинетах."],["Итерация и эволюция дизайна","Первый профиль пациента предоставил информации о владельце и медицинским записям равный визуальный приоритет. Во время консультаций история болезни была переведена на передний план, сохраняя при этом доступ к данным владельца. Создание встреч было упрощено за счет сокращения обязательных полей на первом этапе и возможности заполнения деталей позже."],["Конечный продукт и результаты","Willow Practice объединяет записи пациентов, расписание, рабочие процессы персонала и документацию по лечению в одной системе. Результатом является более предсказуемый путь между связанными записями и меньшее количество несвязанных административных шагов."],["Рефлексия и компетенции","Willow Practice требовала надежности и информативности, а не визуальной новизны. Он демонстрирует дизайн B2B SaaS, UX в сфере здравоохранения, сложные информационные панели, ролевые интерфейсы, архитектуру рабочих процессов и операционное мышление продукта."]]},"de":{"name":"Willow Practice","industry":"Veterinärgesundheit / SaaS","platform":"Responsive Webanwendung","location":"Utrecht, Netherlands","role":"UX/UI-Designer · Produktdesigner","scope":"Workflow-Analyse · Informationsarchitektur · Dashboard-Design · Benutzerflüsse · Prototyping","strapline":"Gestaltung eines effizienteren veterinärmedizinischen Arbeitsablaufs","sections":[["Projektübersicht","Willow Practice ist eine Managementplattform für Tierarztpraxen, die Termine, Tierakten, Behandlungsverläufe und interne Klinikabläufe zentralisiert. Es unterstützt kleine Kliniken, in denen sich die Verantwortlichkeiten zwischen Rezeptionisten, Tierärzten und Administratoren teilen."],["Kontext- und Problemdefinition","Kleine Praxen kombinieren oft Papierakten, Tabellenkalkulationen, Nachrichten und getrennte Kalender. Patienteninformationen sind nur schwer abzurufen, die Nachsorge wird manuell nachverfolgt und den Eigentümern fehlt ein konsolidierter Überblick über die täglichen Abläufe."],["Benutzer und ihre Bedürfnisse","Rezeptionisten erstellen Termine, verwalten Kalender, registrieren Patienten und kommunizieren mit Eigentümern. Tierärzte überprüfen Anamnesen, dokumentieren Untersuchungen, erfassen Diagnosen und verwalten Behandlungspläne. Klinikadministratoren verwalten Zeitpläne, Dienste, Berichte und Berechtigungen."],["Forschung und Entdeckung","Der Entwurf begann mit der Abbildung eines Tierarzttermins von der Registrierung und Terminplanung bis hin zum Eintreffen, der Beratung, der Behandlungsdokumentation, der Zahlungserfassung und der Nachsorgeplanung. Die Analyse trennte schnelle administrative Interaktionen vom umfassenden medizinischen Kontext, der während der Konsultationen erforderlich war."],["Informationsarchitektur","Sechs Module organisieren die Plattform: Dashboard, Kalender, Patienten, Kunden, Behandlungen und Verwaltung. Patientenakten fungieren als zentrales Objekt, das Termine, Eigentümer, Behandlungsverlauf und Folgemaßnahmen miteinander verbindet."],["Wichtige Designentscheidungen","Ein einheitliches Patientenprofil gruppiert Identifikation, Besitzerdetails, Krankengeschichte und bevorstehende Termine chronologisch. Ein Kalender mit geteilter Ansicht sorgt dafür, dass der Terminkontext sichtbar bleibt. Die rollenbasierte Navigation reduziert irrelevante Aktionen, während die strukturierte Beratung einzelne Symptome, Befunde, Diagnosen und Empfehlungen erfasst."],["Benutzeroberfläche und visuelle Ausrichtung","Neutrale Oberflächen, zurückhaltende blaugrüne Akzente, einheitliche Statusanzeigen und eine klare Typografie unterstützen ein ruhiges, lesbares Arbeiten. Medizinische Informationen bleiben optisch von administrativen Informationen abgegrenzt, wobei auf Warnungen, unvollständige Aufzeichnungen, Konflikte und Tablet-Nutzung in Sprechzimmern geachtet wird."],["Iteration und Designentwicklung","Das erste Patientenprofil gab den Eigentümerinformationen und den Krankenakten die gleiche visuelle Priorität. Es wurde überarbeitet, um die Krankengeschichte bei Konsultationen in den Vordergrund zu stellen und gleichzeitig die Daten des Eigentümers zugänglich zu halten. Die Terminerstellung wurde vereinfacht, indem die erforderlichen Felder im ersten Schritt reduziert wurden und die Details später vervollständigt werden konnten."],["Endprodukt und Lieferungen","Willow Practice verbindet Patientenakten, Terminplanung, Personalabläufe und Behandlungsdokumentation in einem System. Das Ergebnis ist ein vorhersehbarerer Pfad zwischen verwandten Datensätzen und weniger unzusammenhängende Verwaltungsschritte."],["Reflexion & Kompetenzen","Willow Practice erforderte eher Zuverlässigkeit und Informationsdichte als visuelle Neuheit. Es demonstriert B2B-SaaS-Design, UX im Gesundheitswesen, komplexe Dashboards, rollenbasierte Schnittstellen, Workflow-Architektur und operatives Produktdenken."]]}},"moveo":{"uk":{"name":"Parcel District","industry":"Логістика та доставка","platform":"Веб-панель + мобільний додаток","location":"Singapore","role":"Дизайнер продукту · Дизайнер UX/UI","scope":"Дизайн сервісу · Мультиплатформний UX · Операційні робочі процеси · Дизайн інформаційної панелі · Mobile UX","strapline":"Підключення диспетчерів, кур'єрів і доставки","sections":[["Огляд проекту","Parcel District – це платформа доставки для місцевої кур’єрської служби, яка обслуговує доставку в той же день для малого бізнесу. Він поєднує в собі інформаційну панель диспетчера на робочому столі та програму мобільного кур’єра для роботи в умовах постійного дефіциту часу."],["Контекст і визначення проблеми","Диспетчери можуть координувати багато замовлень за допомогою телефонних дзвінків і обміну повідомленнями, тоді як кур’єрам потрібен негайний доступ до відповідної інформації про доставку. Продукт, необхідний для координації людей, пунктів призначення, статусів і мінливих умов, не піддаючи жодну роль непотрібній складності."],["Користувачі та їхні потреби","Диспетчерам потрібен оперативний огляд, засоби керування призначеннями, статуси доставки та обробка винятків. Кур’єрам потрібна коротка послідовність для перегляду наступної доставки, підтвердження прогресу та повідомлення про проблему під час переміщення між місцями."],["Дослідження та відкриття","У роботі проаналізовано життєвий цикл доставки від створення та призначення замовлення до самовивозу, доставки, підтвердження доставки та вирішення винятків. Це виявило потребу в моделі спільного статусу з ролевими діями, а не в двох відключених продуктах."],["Інформаційна архітектура","Робоча область диспетчера зосереджена на активних доставках, призначеннях, доступності кур’єрів, місцях, винятках і звітності. Досвід кур’єра визначає пріоритетність призначених завдань, контекст маршруту, деталі доставки, оновлення статусу та контактні дії."],["Ключові дизайнерські рішення","Спільна система статусу доставки підтримує узгодженість обох інтерфейсів. Інформаційна панель використовує компактні таблиці, контекстні панелі та інформацію на основі карти для координації. Додаток Courier використовує більші сенсорні цілі, спрощену навігацію та висококонтрастні основні дії для зміни середовища."],["Інтерфейс користувача та дизайн взаємодії","Продукт відокремлює актуальну інформацію від історичних звітів. Робочі стани впізнавані та послідовні, тоді як мобільні дії залишаються короткими та передбачуваними. Обробка особливих ситуацій розглядається як першокласний потік, а не помилка, що виникає пізніше."],["Ітерація та еволюція дизайну","Рання інформаційна панель відображала занадто багато операційних показників одночасно. Його було реорганізовано навколо актуальної інформації, а історичні звіти перенесено в окрему область. Робочий процес кур’єра було скорочено, тому стандартні оновлення статусу вимагали менше взаємодії."],["Кінцевий продукт і результати","Parcel District поєднує керування призначеннями, відстеження доставки, робочі статуси та обробку винятків через інтерфейси диспетчера та кур’єра. Досвід зменшує непотрібні накладні витрати на координацію, зберігаючи усвідомлення мінливих умов."],["Рефлексія та компетенції","Головною проблемою проектування була координація послуг, а не ізольовані екрани. Parcel District демонструє логістичний UX, дизайн послуг, інтерфейс користувача на основі карт, багатоплатформні системи, дизайн робочого процесу та складну інформаційну архітектуру."]]},"pl":{"name":"Parcel District","industry":"Logistyka i dostawa","platform":"Panel WWW + aplikacja mobilna","location":"Singapore","role":"Projektant produktu · Projektant UX/UI","scope":"Projektowanie usług · Wieloplatformowy UX · Operacyjne przepływy pracy · Projektowanie dashboardów · Mobilny UX","strapline":"Łączenie dyspozytorów, kurierów i dostaw","sections":[["Przegląd projektu","Parcel District to platforma operacji dostaw dla lokalnych firm kurierskich obsługujących dostawy tego samego dnia dla małych firm. Łączy pulpit wysyłkowy z mobilną aplikacją kurierską do pracy pod ciągłą presją czasu."],["Kontekst i definicja problemu","Dyspozytorzy mogą koordynować wiele zamówień poprzez rozmowy telefoniczne i wiadomości, podczas gdy kurierzy potrzebują natychmiastowego dostępu do odpowiednich informacji o dostawie. Produkt potrzebny do koordynowania ludzi, miejsc docelowych, statusów i zmieniających się warunków bez narażania żadnej z ról na niepotrzebną złożoność."],["Użytkownicy i ich potrzeby","Dyspozytorzy potrzebują przeglądu operacyjnego, kontroli przydziałów, statusów dostaw i obsługi wyjątków. Kurierzy potrzebują krótkiej sekwencji, aby obejrzeć kolejną dostawę, potwierdzić postęp i zgłosić problem podczas przemieszczania się pomiędzy lokalizacjami."],["Badania i odkrycia","W ramach pracy przeanalizowano cykl życia dostawy, od utworzenia i przypisania zamówienia, poprzez odbiór, transport, potwierdzenie dostawy i rozwiązanie wyjątku. Ujawniło to potrzebę wspólnego modelu statusu z działaniami dostosowanymi do konkretnych ról, a nie dwóch odłączonych produktów."],["Architektura Informacji","Obszar roboczy dyspozytora koncentruje się na aktywnych dostawach, przydziałach, dostępności kurierów, lokalizacjach, wyjątkach i raportowaniu. Doświadczenie kuriera nadaje priorytet przydzielonym zadaniom, kontekstowi trasy, szczegółom dostawy, aktualizacjom statusu i działaniom kontaktowym."],["Kluczowe decyzje projektowe","Wspólny system statusu dostawy zapewnia spójność obu interfejsów. Pulpit nawigacyjny wykorzystuje do koordynacji kompaktowe tabele, panele kontekstowe i informacje oparte na mapach. Aplikacja kurierska wykorzystuje większe cele dotykowe, uproszczoną nawigację i podstawowe działania o wysokim kontraście w przypadku zmieniających się środowisk."],["Projekt interfejsu użytkownika i interakcji","Produkt oddziela przydatne informacje od raportów historycznych. Stany operacyjne są rozpoznawalne i spójne, a działania mobilne pozostają krótkie i przewidywalne. Obsługa wyjątków jest traktowana jako przepływ pierwszej klasy, a nie jako błąd po namyśle."],["Iteracja i ewolucja projektu","Wczesny pulpit nawigacyjny wyświetlał jednocześnie zbyt wiele wskaźników operacyjnych. Został on zreorganizowany w oparciu o przydatne informacje, a raportowanie historyczne zostało przeniesione do osobnego obszaru. Przepływ pracy kuriera został skrócony, więc częste aktualizacje statusu wymagały mniejszej liczby interakcji."],["Produkt końcowy i elementy dostawy","Parcel District łączy zarządzanie zadaniami, śledzenie dostaw, statusy operacyjne i obsługę wyjątków w interfejsach dyspozytora i kuriera. To doświadczenie zmniejsza niepotrzebne obciążenie koordynacyjne, zachowując jednocześnie świadomość zmieniających się warunków."],["Refleksja i kompetencje","Głównym wyzwaniem projektowym była koordynacja usług, a nie izolowane ekrany. Parcel District demonstruje UX logistyki, projektowanie usług, interfejs użytkownika oparty na mapach, systemy wieloplatformowe, projektowanie przepływu pracy i złożoną architekturę informacji."]]},"ru":{"name":"Parcel District","industry":"Логистика и доставка","platform":"Веб-панель + мобильное приложение","location":"Singapore","role":"Дизайнер продуктов · UX/UI дизайнер","scope":"Дизайн услуг · Мультиплатформенный UX · Операционные рабочие процессы · Дизайн информационной панели · Мобильный UX","strapline":"Подключение диспетчеров, курьеров и доставок","sections":[["Обзор проекта","Parcel District — это платформа доставки для местной курьерской службы, осуществляющая доставку в тот же день для малого бизнеса. Он сочетает в себе настольную диспетчерскую панель с мобильным курьерским приложением для работы в условиях постоянного дефицита времени."],["Контекст и определение проблемы","Диспетчеры могут координировать многие заказы посредством телефонных звонков и сообщений, а курьерам необходим немедленный доступ к соответствующей информации о доставке. Продукт должен был координировать людей, пункты назначения, статусы и меняющиеся условия, не подвергая ни одну из ролей ненужной сложности."],["Пользователи и их потребности","Диспетчерам необходим оперативный обзор, контроль назначений, статусы доставки и обработка исключений. Курьерам нужна короткая последовательность действий для просмотра следующей доставки, подтверждения прогресса и сообщения о проблеме при перемещении между точками."],["Исследования и открытия","В ходе работы был проанализирован жизненный цикл доставки: от создания и назначения заказа до получения, транзита, подтверждения доставки и разрешения исключений. Это выявило необходимость в общей модели статуса с действиями для конкретных ролей, а не в двух разрозненных продуктах."],["Информационная архитектура","Рабочее пространство диспетчера сосредоточено на активных доставках, назначениях, доступности курьеров, местоположениях, исключениях и отчетности. В работе курьера приоритет отдается назначенным задачам, контексту маршрута, деталям доставки, обновлениям статуса и действиям с контактами."],["Ключевые проектные решения","Общая система статуса доставки обеспечивает синхронизацию обоих интерфейсов. На информационной панели для координации используются компактные таблицы, контекстные панели и информация на основе карт. Приложение курьера использует более крупные сенсорные объекты, упрощенную навигацию и высококонтрастные основные действия для меняющихся условий."],["Дизайн пользовательского интерфейса и взаимодействия","Продукт отделяет полезную информацию от исторической отчетности. Оперативные состояния узнаваемы и последовательны, а мобильные действия остаются короткими и предсказуемыми. Обработка исключений рассматривается как первоклассный поток, а не как второстепенная ошибка."],["Итерация и эволюция дизайна","Ранняя информационная панель отображала слишком много операционных показателей одновременно. Он был реорганизован вокруг практической информации, а исторические отчеты были выделены в отдельную область. Рабочий процесс курьера был сокращен, поэтому общие обновления статуса требовали меньшего количества взаимодействий."],["Конечный продукт и результаты","Parcel District объединяет управление назначениями, отслеживание доставки, операционные статусы и обработку исключений через интерфейсы диспетчера и курьера. Этот опыт снижает ненужные затраты на координацию, сохраняя при этом осведомленность об изменяющихся условиях."],["Рефлексия и компетенции","Центральной проблемой дизайна была координация услуг, а не изолированные экраны. Parcel District демонстрирует UX логистики, дизайн услуг, картографический пользовательский интерфейс, многоплатформенные системы, проектирование рабочих процессов и сложную информационную архитектуру."]]},"de":{"name":"Parcel District","industry":"Logistik & Lieferung","platform":"Web-Dashboard + mobile Anwendung","location":"Singapore","role":"Produktdesigner · UX/UI-Designer","scope":"Service-Design · Multiplattform-UX · Betriebsabläufe · Dashboard-Design · Mobile UX","strapline":"Verbindung von Disponenten, Kurieren und Lieferungen","sections":[["Projektübersicht","Parcel District ist eine Lieferplattform für einen lokalen Kurierdienst, der Kleinunternehmen noch am selben Tag beliefert. Es kombiniert ein Desktop-Versand-Dashboard mit einer mobilen Kurieranwendung für Arbeiten unter ständigem Zeitdruck."],["Kontext- und Problemdefinition","Disponenten können viele Bestellungen über Telefonanrufe und Nachrichten koordinieren, während Kuriere sofortigen Zugriff auf die relevanten Lieferinformationen benötigen. Das Produkt musste Personen, Ziele, Status und sich ändernde Bedingungen koordinieren, ohne eine der Rollen unnötiger Komplexität auszusetzen."],["Benutzer und ihre Bedürfnisse","Disponenten benötigen einen Betriebsüberblick, Zuweisungskontrollen, Lieferstatus und Ausnahmebehandlung. Kuriere benötigen eine kurze Sequenz, um sich die nächste Lieferung anzusehen, den Fortschritt zu bestätigen und ein Problem zu melden, während sie sich zwischen den Standorten bewegen."],["Forschung und Entdeckung","Die Arbeit analysierte den Lieferlebenszyklus von der Auftragserstellung und -zuweisung bis hin zur Abholung, dem Transport, der Lieferbestätigung und der Ausnahmelösung. Dies zeigte die Notwendigkeit eines gemeinsamen Statusmodells mit rollenspezifischen Aktionen anstelle von zwei getrennten Produkten."],["Informationsarchitektur","Der Dispatcher-Arbeitsbereich konzentriert sich auf aktive Lieferungen, Zuweisungen, Kurierverfügbarkeit, Standorte, Ausnahmen und Berichte. Das Kuriererlebnis priorisiert zugewiesene Aufgaben, Routenkontext, Lieferdetails, Statusaktualisierungen und Kontaktaktionen."],["Wichtige Designentscheidungen","Ein gemeinsames Lieferstatussystem sorgt dafür, dass beide Schnittstellen aufeinander abgestimmt sind. Das Dashboard nutzt kompakte Tabellen, kontextbezogene Panels und kartenbasierte Informationen zur Koordination. Die Kurier-App nutzt größere Touch-Ziele, vereinfachte Navigation und kontrastreiche Hauptaktionen für sich ändernde Umgebungen."],["UI- und Interaktionsdesign","Das Produkt trennt umsetzbare Informationen von historischen Berichten. Betriebszustände sind erkennbar und konsistent, während mobile Aktionen kurz und vorhersehbar bleiben. Die Ausnahmebehandlung wird als erstklassiger Ablauf und nicht als nachträglicher Fehler behandelt."],["Iteration und Designentwicklung","Ein frühes Dashboard zeigte zu viele Betriebskennzahlen auf einmal an. Es wurde um umsetzbare Informationen herum neu organisiert und die historische Berichterstattung in einen separaten Bereich verschoben. Der Kurier-Workflow wurde verkürzt, sodass häufige Statusaktualisierungen weniger Interaktionen erforderten."],["Endprodukt und Lieferungen","Parcel District verbindet Auftragsverwaltung, Lieferverfolgung, Betriebsstatus und Ausnahmebehandlung über Disponenten- und Kurierschnittstellen hinweg. Die Erfahrung reduziert unnötigen Koordinationsaufwand und bewahrt gleichzeitig das Bewusstsein für sich ändernde Bedingungen."],["Reflexion & Kompetenzen","Die zentrale Designherausforderung war die Servicekoordination und nicht isolierte Bildschirme. Parcel District demonstriert Logistik-UX, Service-Design, kartenbasierte Benutzeroberfläche, Multiplattform-Systeme, Workflow-Design und komplexe Informationsarchitektur."]]}},"modo":{"uk":{"name":"Atelier Learning","industry":"Освіта та творчі індустрії","platform":"Чуйний веб-додаток","location":"Melbourne, Australia","role":"Дизайнер UX/UI · Дизайнер цифрових продуктів","scope":"Стратегія UX · Дизайн досвіду навчання · Потоки користувачів · Дизайн взаємодії · Прототипування","strapline":"Переосмислення досвіду цифрового навчання","sections":[["Огляд проекту","Atelier Learning — це онлайн-навчальна платформа для творчого освітнього бізнесу, яка пропонує курси з ілюстрації, візуальної комунікації та цифрового дизайну. Він поєднує в собі навчальний контент, подання проектів, відгуки викладачів і відстеження прогресу."],["Контекст і визначення проблеми","Невеликі творчі школи часто поєднують розміщення відео, обмін файлами, обмін повідомленнями та ручне ведення записів про прогрес. Учні втрачають зв’язок між уроками, завданнями та зворотним зв’язком, а вчителям потрібен ефективний огляд кількох проектів і переглядів."],["Користувачі та їхні потреби","Початківцям потрібна чітка структура та впевненість у досягненні прогресу. Досвідченим студентам потрібна гнучкість і доступ до попередніх ресурсів. Викладачам потрібні історії подання, керування завданнями та ефективні робочі процеси зворотного зв’язку."],["Дослідження та відкриття","Стратегія продукту передбачала як структуроване навчання, так і самостійне дослідження. Аналіз зосереджувався на тому, де студентам потрібне керівництво, де їм потрібна свобода повторного перегляду матеріалу та як інструктор може переглянути практичну роботу, не втрачаючи її історію."],["Інформаційна архітектура","П’ять розділів організовують платформу: Моє навчання, Курси, Робочий простір, Відгуки та Профіль. Кожен курс пов’язує свої уроки із завданнями та історією відгуків, зменшуючи необхідність пошуку в непов’язаних розділах."],["Ключові дизайнерські рішення","Інформаційна панель навчання висвітлює активні курси, наступний рекомендований урок, незавершені завдання та останні відгуки. Завдання отримують спеціальну робочу область для файлів і пояснень. Зворотній зв’язок — це постійний процес перегляду, що підтримує коментарі, повторне надсилання та попередні версії."],["Інтерфейс і візуальне спрямування","Теплі нейтральні тони, редакційна типографіка, стримані акценти та щедрі області зображення підтримують творчу роботу, не перетворюючи інтерфейс на гру. Індикатори прогресу повідомляють про віхи, а не про конкуренцію, тоді як попередній перегляд проекту отримує більше уваги, ніж адміністративні метадані."],["Ітерація та еволюція дизайну","Інтерфейс першого курсу розміщував уроки та завдання в одному безперервному списку, що ускладнювало їхнє розуміння. Переглянута структура відокремила вміст від завдань, зберігаючи зв’язок через модулі. Історію відгуків також було перероблено, щоб полегшити пошук змін."],["Кінцевий продукт і результати","Atelier Learning об’єднує курси, творчі завдання, відгуки викладачів і прогрес в єдиний процес навчання. Він підтримує самостійне навчання, зберігаючи взаємодію між студентами та викладачами."],["Рефлексія та компетенції","Atelier Learning розглядає навчання як безперервний процес, а не як набір сторінок. Проект демонструє EdTech UX, дизайн досвіду навчання, архітектуру контенту, інформаційні панелі студентів, дизайн взаємодії та залучення без непотрібної гейміфікації."]]},"pl":{"name":"Atelier Learning","industry":"Edukacja i przemysł kreatywny","platform":"Responsywna aplikacja internetowa","location":"Melbourne, Australia","role":"Projektant UX/UI · Projektant produktów cyfrowych","scope":"Strategia UX · Projektowanie doświadczeń edukacyjnych · Przepływy użytkowników · Projektowanie interakcji · Prototypowanie","strapline":"Nowe podejście do cyfrowego uczenia się","sections":[["Przegląd projektu","Atelier Learning to internetowa platforma edukacyjna dla kreatywnej firmy edukacyjnej oferująca kursy z zakresu ilustracji, komunikacji wizualnej i projektowania cyfrowego. Łączy w sobie treści edukacyjne, przesłane projekty, opinie instruktorów i śledzenie postępów."],["Kontekst i definicja problemu","Małe szkoły kreatywne często łączą hosting wideo, udostępnianie plików, przesyłanie wiadomości i ręcznie prowadzone rejestry postępów. Uczniowie tracą wątek pomiędzy lekcjami, zadaniami i informacją zwrotną, podczas gdy nauczycielom potrzebny jest skuteczny przegląd wielu projektów i poprawek."],["Użytkownicy i ich potrzeby","Początkujący potrzebują jasnej struktury i pewności, że czynią postępy. Doświadczeni studenci potrzebują elastyczności i dostępu do wcześniejszych zasobów. Instruktorzy potrzebują historii zgłoszeń, zarządzania zadaniami i skutecznych przepływów informacji zwrotnych."],["Badania i odkrycia","Strategia produktu uwzględniała zarówno ustrukturyzowane uczenie się, jak i niezależną eksplorację. Analiza skupiała się na tym, gdzie uczniowie potrzebują wskazówek, gdzie potrzebują swobody w ponownym przeglądaniu materiału i w jaki sposób instruktor może przeglądać praktyczną pracę bez utraty jej historii."],["Architektura Informacji","Platformę organizuje pięć obszarów: Moja nauka, Kursy, Przestrzeń robocza, Opinia i Profil. Każdy kurs łączy lekcje z zadaniami i historią opinii, co ogranicza potrzebę przeszukiwania niepowiązanych sekcji."],["Kluczowe decyzje projektowe","Panel nauki wyświetla aktywne kursy, następną zalecaną lekcję, oczekujące zadania i najnowsze opinie. Zadania otrzymują dedykowaną przestrzeń roboczą na pliki i objaśnienia. Opinia to ciągły proces sprawdzania, obejmujący komentarze, ponowne przesyłanie i poprzednie wersje."],["Interfejs użytkownika i kierunek wizualny","Ciepłe neutralne odcienie, typografia redakcyjna, powściągliwe akcenty i obszerne obszary obrazu wspierają twórczą pracę bez przekształcania interfejsu w grę. Wskaźniki postępu informują o kamieniach milowych, a nie o konkurencji, podczas gdy zapowiedzi projektów kładzie się większy nacisk niż metadane administracyjne."],["Iteracja i ewolucja projektu","W pierwszym interfejsie kursu lekcje i zadania znajdowały się na jednej ciągłej liście, co utrudniało zrozumienie ich powiązań. Zmieniona struktura oddzieliła treść od zadań, zachowując jednocześnie połączenie poprzez moduły. Przeprojektowano także historię opinii, aby ułatwić wyszukiwanie poprawek."],["Produkt końcowy i elementy dostawy","Atelier Learning łączy kursy, kreatywne zadania, opinie instruktorów i postępy w jedno spójne doświadczenie edukacyjne. Wspiera niezależną naukę, zachowując jednocześnie interakcję między uczniami i nauczycielami."],["Refleksja i kompetencje","Atelier Learning traktuje naukę jako proces ciągły, a nie zbiór stron. Projekt demonstruje EdTech UX, projektowanie doświadczeń edukacyjnych, architekturę treści, pulpity nawigacyjne uczniów, projektowanie interakcji i zaangażowanie bez niepotrzebnej grywalizacji."]]},"ru":{"name":"Atelier Learning","industry":"Образование и творческие индустрии","platform":"Адаптивное веб-приложение","location":"Melbourne, Australia","role":"UX/UI дизайнер · Дизайнер цифровых продуктов","scope":"UX-стратегия · Проектирование опыта обучения · Потоки пользователей · Дизайн взаимодействия · Прототипирование","strapline":"Переосмысление опыта цифрового обучения","sections":[["Обзор проекта","Atelier Learning — это онлайн-платформа обучения для творческого образовательного бизнеса, предлагающая курсы по иллюстрации, визуальным коммуникациям и цифровому дизайну. Он сочетает в себе образовательный контент, представление проектов, отзывы инструкторов и отслеживание прогресса."],["Контекст и определение проблемы","Небольшие творческие школы часто сочетают в себе видеохостинг, обмен файлами, обмен сообщениями и ведение записей успеваемости вручную. Учащиеся теряют связь между уроками, заданиями и отзывами, а учителям нужен эффективный обзор множества проектов и изменений."],["Пользователи и их потребности","Новичкам нужна четкая структура и уверенность в том, что прогресс достигается. Опытным студентам необходима гибкость и доступ к предыдущим ресурсам. Преподавателям нужны истории отправки, управление заданиями и эффективные рабочие процессы обратной связи."],["Исследования и открытия","Стратегия продукта учитывала как структурированное обучение, так и независимое исследование. Анализ был сосредоточен на том, где студентам необходимо руководство, где им нужна свобода повторного просмотра материала и как преподаватель может просмотреть практическую работу, не теряя ее истории."],["Информационная архитектура","Платформу организуют пять разделов: «Мое обучение», «Курсы», «Рабочее пространство», «Обратная связь» и «Профиль». В каждом курсе уроки связаны с заданиями и историей отзывов, что снижает необходимость поиска по несвязанным разделам."],["Ключевые проектные решения","На информационной панели обучения отображаются активные курсы, следующий рекомендуемый урок, ожидающие задания и недавние отзывы. Задания получают выделенное рабочее пространство для файлов и пояснений. Обратная связь — это непрерывный процесс пересмотра, поддерживающий комментарии, повторные отправки и предыдущие версии."],["Пользовательский интерфейс и визуальное направление","Теплые нейтральные цвета, редакционная типографика, сдержанные акценты и просторные области изображений поддерживают творческую работу, не превращая интерфейс в игру. Индикаторы прогресса сообщают о вехах, а не о конкуренции, а предварительному просмотру проектов уделяется больше внимания, чем административным метаданным."],["Итерация и эволюция дизайна","Интерфейс первого курса помещал уроки и задания в один непрерывный список, что затрудняло понимание их взаимосвязи. Пересмотренная структура отделила контент от задач, сохраняя при этом связь через модули. История отзывов также была переработана, чтобы облегчить поиск изменений."],["Конечный продукт и результаты","Atelier Learning объединяет курсы, творческие задания, отзывы инструкторов и прогресс в единый процесс обучения. Он поддерживает независимое обучение, сохраняя при этом взаимодействие между учениками и преподавателями."],["Рефлексия и компетенции","Atelier Learning рассматривает обучение как непрерывный процесс, а не как набор страниц. Проект демонстрирует UX EdTech, дизайн опыта обучения, архитектуру контента, информационные панели учащихся, дизайн взаимодействия и взаимодействие без ненужной геймификации."]]},"de":{"name":"Atelier Learning","industry":"Bildung und Kreativwirtschaft","platform":"Responsive Webanwendung","location":"Melbourne, Australia","role":"UX/UI-Designer · Digitaler Produktdesigner","scope":"UX-Strategie · Lernerfahrungsdesign · Benutzerflüsse · Interaktionsdesign · Prototyping","strapline":"Das digitale Lernerlebnis neu denken","sections":[["Projektübersicht","Atelier Learning ist eine Online-Lernplattform für ein kreatives Bildungsunternehmen, das Kurse in Illustration, visueller Kommunikation und digitalem Design anbietet. Es kombiniert Bildungsinhalte, Projekteinreichungen, Dozenten-Feedback und Fortschrittsverfolgung."],["Kontext- und Problemdefinition","Kleine Kreativschulen kombinieren oft Video-Hosting, Dateifreigabe, Messaging und manuell gepflegte Fortschrittsaufzeichnungen. Schüler verlieren den Faden zwischen Unterricht, Aufgaben und Feedback, während Lehrer einen effizienten Überblick über mehrere Projekte und Überarbeitungen benötigen."],["Benutzer und ihre Bedürfnisse","Anfänger brauchen eine klare Struktur und die Gewissheit, dass Fortschritte gemacht werden. Erfahrene Studierende benötigen Flexibilität und Zugang zu früheren Ressourcen. Dozenten benötigen Einreichungshistorien, Aufgabenverwaltung und effiziente Feedback-Workflows."],["Forschung und Entdeckung","Die Produktstrategie berücksichtigte sowohl strukturiertes Lernen als auch unabhängige Erkundung. Die Analyse konzentrierte sich darauf, wo die Schüler Orientierung benötigen, wo sie die Freiheit brauchen, den Stoff noch einmal durchzugehen, und wie ein Dozent praktische Arbeiten wiederholen kann, ohne deren Geschichte zu verlieren."],["Informationsarchitektur","Fünf Bereiche organisieren die Plattform: Mein Lernen, Kurse, Arbeitsbereich, Feedback und Profil. Jeder Kurs verknüpft seine Lektionen mit Aufgaben und dem Feedback-Verlauf, wodurch die Notwendigkeit einer Suche in nicht zusammenhängenden Abschnitten verringert wird."],["Wichtige Designentscheidungen","Ein Lern-Dashboard hebt aktive Kurse, die nächste empfohlene Lektion, ausstehende Aufgaben und aktuelles Feedback hervor. Aufgaben erhalten einen eigenen Arbeitsbereich für Dateien und Erläuterungen. Feedback ist ein fortlaufender Überarbeitungsprozess, der Kommentare, Wiedereinreichungen und frühere Versionen unterstützt."],["Benutzeroberfläche und visuelle Ausrichtung","Warme Neutraltöne, redaktionelle Typografie, zurückhaltende Akzente und großzügige Bildflächen unterstützen kreatives Arbeiten, ohne die Benutzeroberfläche in ein Spiel zu verwandeln. Fortschrittsindikatoren vermitteln eher Meilensteine ​​als Wettbewerb, während Projektvorschauen mehr Gewicht erhalten als administrative Metadaten."],["Iteration und Designentwicklung","Die erste Kursoberfläche platzierte Lektionen und Aufgaben in einer fortlaufenden Liste, was es schwierig machte, ihre Beziehung zu verstehen. Die überarbeitete Struktur trennte Inhalte von Aufgaben, während die Verbindung durch Module erhalten blieb. Der Feedback-Verlauf wurde ebenfalls neu gestaltet, um das Auffinden von Überarbeitungen zu erleichtern."],["Endprodukt und Lieferungen","Atelier Learning vereint Kurse, kreative Aufgaben, Lehrer-Feedback und Fortschritte in einem zusammenhängenden Lernerlebnis. Es unterstützt das unabhängige Lernen und bewahrt gleichzeitig die Interaktion zwischen Schülern und Lehrern."],["Reflexion & Kompetenzen","Atelier Learning betrachtet Lernen als einen kontinuierlichen Prozess und nicht als eine Ansammlung von Seiten. Das Projekt demonstriert EdTech UX, Lernerfahrungsdesign, Inhaltsarchitektur, Studenten-Dashboards, Interaktionsdesign und Engagement ohne unnötige Gamifizierung."]]}},"maria":{"uk":{"name":"MARIA APARTMENTS","industry":"Гостинність / управління нерухомістю","platform":"Сайт · платформа бронювання · автоматизація бізнесу","location":"Тернопіль, Україна","role":"UX/UI · вебдизайн · айдентика · операційні процеси","scope":"Website structure · Booking experience · Guest communication · Operations","strapline":"A digital product shaped by direct operational knowledge","links":[{"label":"MARIA APARTMENTS","href":"https://www.bymariaapartments.com"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>Maria Apartments is a short-term accommodation business operating a portfolio of 19 apartments across several locations in Ternopil, Ukraine.</p><p>The project began with an established rental business that had customers, properties and operational experience, but lacked a unified brand identity and an integrated digital infrastructure.</p><p>Reservations were managed through Excel spreadsheets. Guests relied on direct communication to check availability, confirm bookings and arrange their arrival. Payments involved bank transfers and cash, while key handovers required physical coordination. Instagram served as an important customer-facing channel, but there was no dedicated platform connecting apartment discovery, reservations, payments and internal operations.</p><p>My responsibility was to transform this fragmented experience into a coherent digital ecosystem.</p><p>I independently managed the project across creative direction, user experience, technical implementation and operational integration. The work included establishing a new brand, producing visual content, designing and developing a bilingual accommodation website, implementing a booking and payment system, creating an administrative management platform and developing a custom Telegram bot for guest assistance and contactless check-in.</p><p>The result was not simply a redesigned website.</p><p>It was a connected hospitality management system designed to support the relationship between guests, administrators, reservations, payments, physical properties and daily business operations.</p>"],["Understanding the Business","<h3>The original operating model</h3><p>Before the transformation, the business operated under the generic description &quot;Daily Apartment Rentals&quot; rather than a recognizable independent brand.</p><p>Individual apartments were presented primarily through existing listings and social media. Customers frequently needed to contact an administrator to obtain information that could otherwise have been available through a self-service platform.</p><p>The reservation process depended on several disconnected interactions.</p><p>A potential guest would inquire about an apartment, ask about available dates, receive pricing information and coordinate payment directly with the business.</p><p>A security deposit was typically transferred to a bank card, while the remaining accommodation cost was paid in cash upon arrival.</p><p>After booking, the administrator recorded reservation details in Excel, coordinated arrival arrangements, managed physical keys and later processed the deposit return.</p><p>Other activities, including cleaning schedules, property maintenance and financial tracking, required additional manual coordination.</p><p>These processes were functional individually, but their separation created unnecessary dependencies.</p><h3>The core business problems</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Fragmented information*</em></p><p>Reservation records, apartment availability, payments, guest communication and operational tasks were managed through separate channels.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Administrative dependency*</em></p><p>Routine guest questions and booking actions required direct staff involvement.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited self-service*</em></p><p>Customers could not complete the entire accommodation journey independently through an official website.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Payment friction*</em></p><p>Financial transactions were divided between different payment methods and stages of the stay.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Operational complexity*</em></p><p>Managing 19 properties required ongoing coordination of arrivals, departures, cleaning, maintenance and availability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Inconsistent brand presentation*</em></p><p>The absence of a unified identity made the business appear as a collection of rental listings rather than a recognizable accommodation provider.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited reporting*</em></p><p>Without integrated analytics, evaluating occupancy, reservation activity and property-level performance required manual work.</p><p>The challenge was to address these problems as parts of one system rather than introduce isolated solutions.</p>"],["Discovery & UX Research","<p>My research approach was strongly influenced by direct participation in the business's daily operations.</p><p>Rather than relying exclusively on a design brief, I gained firsthand insight into guest communication, booking coordination, arrival procedures and administrative responsibilities.</p><p>This allowed me to examine the complete service experience, including interactions that happened outside a conventional website.</p><p>I analyzed how guests obtained information, how availability was confirmed, how payments were coordinated and what administrators needed to prepare an apartment for the next arrival.</p><p>I also examined the relationships between reservations, cleaning schedules, security deposits and physical access.</p><p>This revealed an important distinction between the needs of different users.</p><h3>Guest needs</h3><p>Guests needed to:</p><ul><li>Find suitable accommodation without extensive messaging.</li><li>Compare apartments by location, price, capacity and amenities.</li><li>Understand availability and accommodation costs.</li><li>Complete a reservation with clear payment instructions.</li><li>Receive reliable confirmation.</li><li>Access practical arrival and accommodation information.</li><li>Obtain assistance without repeating their booking details.</li></ul><h3>Administrator needs</h3><p>Administrators needed to:</p><ul><li>View reservations across all 19 apartments.</li><li>Identify occupied and available dates.</li><li>Create and modify bookings.</li><li>Coordinate cleaning between departures and arrivals.</li><li>Track deposits and reservation payments.</li><li>Manage apartment information and operational issues.</li><li>Prepare guests for check-in.</li><li>Control physical keybox access.</li><li>Access structured business reporting.</li></ul><h3>Research conclusion</h3><p>The principal challenge was not a lack of individual digital tools.</p><p>It was the absence of a shared system connecting customer-facing experiences with operational data.</p><p>This conclusion shaped the architecture of the entire project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>Research methodology note: The discovery process was based on operational observation, firsthand experience and workflow analysis rather than a formal controlled user study.</em></p>"],["Product Strategy","<p>I defined the transformation around a connected set of digital and physical touchpoints.</p><p>The strategy had three major objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Establish a recognizable hospitality brand*</em></p><p>Create a consistent identity capable of representing all 19 apartments across digital channels and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Build an independent customer booking journey*</em></p><p>Allow guests to discover accommodation, check dates, understand pricing, book and pay without requiring continuous administrator assistance.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Centralize internal operations*</em></p><p>Provide administrators with a dedicated management environment connected to reservations, payment records, apartment availability, cleaning and guest communication.</p><p>A fourth consideration influenced the entire project: the connection between software and physical accommodation.</p><p>A successful online reservation still needed to result in a guest arriving at the correct property, receiving authorized access and finding an apartment prepared for occupancy.</p><p>This made service design and operational reliability just as important as visual interface design.</p>"],["Brand Identity & Creative Direction","<h3>From a generic rental service to Maria Apartments</h3><p>The transformation began with a new brand identity.</p><p>I developed the name <strong>Maria Apartments</strong>, replacing the original generic positioning with an identifiable hospitality brand.</p><p>The new identity needed to communicate comfort, reliability and professional management without feeling excessively corporate or disconnected from the nature of the accommodation service.</p><p>I designed the logo, established the visual direction and created a consistent system of typography, color and supporting visual elements.</p><p>A warm, light visual approach was selected to make the brand feel welcoming while maintaining clarity across functional interfaces.</p><p>The identity was designed to work in several environments: property listings, booking forms, Instagram content, printed materials and physical guest touchpoints.</p><h3>Photography &amp; property presentation</h3><p>Accommodation is a visually driven service.</p><p>Guests rely heavily on photographs to assess the condition, atmosphere and suitability of a property.</p><p>I photographed apartment interiors, selected and retouched images, and prepared visual assets for consistent presentation across the website and social media.</p><p>The objective was not to make different properties appear identical.</p><p>It was to establish a consistent standard of visual quality while preserving the individual characteristics of each apartment.</p><h3>Physical brand materials</h3><p>I also designed branded business cards and keychains, prepared them for production and coordinated ordering.</p><p>These materials extended the new identity beyond screens.</p><p>The keychains were particularly relevant because physical access was an essential part of the accommodation experience.</p><p>Connecting digital presentation with physical objects helped establish continuity throughout the guest journey.</p>"],["Instagram Rebranding & Digital Presence","<p>Instagram was already an important discovery and communication channel for the business.</p><p>However, the existing account lacked the coherence of a unified brand.</p><p>I redesigned the profile presentation, developed a consistent content direction and established a visual system aligned with the new Maria Apartments identity.</p><p>The work included updated profile information, visual content, post design, publication and organized navigation through Instagram Highlights.</p><p>I also connected the social media presence to the official website.</p><p>This reflected a deliberate strategic decision.</p><p>Instagram would remain a channel for discovery, brand communication and customer engagement, while the website would become the primary environment for property exploration, reservations and payments.</p><p>The objective was to reduce dependence on manual social media conversations as the main booking infrastructure.</p>"],["Website UX/UI Design","<h3>A website designed around the complete booking journey</h3><p>The official Maria Apartments website was developed as a responsive, bilingual platform supporting all 19 apartments.</p><p>Its primary purpose was to transform accommodation discovery into a structured and actionable experience.</p><p>The information architecture was organized around the guest's practical decision-making process:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover → Compare → Evaluate → Check Availability → Book → Pay → Receive Confirmation*</em></p><p>Each stage needed to provide enough information for the user to proceed confidently.</p><h3>Interactive building facade</h3><p>One of the distinctive visual features of the website is its interactive building facade.</p><p>Instead of functioning as a purely decorative homepage illustration, the building provides an alternative way to explore available accommodation.</p><p>The interactive windows connect the visual presentation to apartment discovery.</p><p>This approach gave the business a memorable digital identity while making the interface itself part of the experience.</p><p>However, visual originality was not allowed to replace usability.</p><p>The interactive building was supported by more conventional browsing tools, ensuring that customers could still explore properties through familiar catalog and filtering patterns.</p><p>The design decision balanced brand differentiation with practical navigation.</p><h3>Apartment catalog &amp; filtering</h3><p>The website includes a structured catalog of 19 apartments, allowing guests to explore different properties within a consistent interface.</p><p>Filtering supports practical accommodation decisions, including available dates, guest capacity, location and pricing considerations.</p><p>Because properties are distributed across different areas of Ternopil, location-based navigation was an important requirement.</p><p>The catalog was designed to help visitors narrow their options before committing to a detailed property review.</p><h3>Individual apartment pages</h3><p>Each apartment needed enough information to support an informed booking decision.</p><p>I organized property pages around consistent content categories, including:</p><ul><li>Apartment photography.</li><li>Descriptions and accommodation characteristics.</li><li>Guest capacity.</li><li>Relevant amenities.</li><li>Location information.</li><li>Pricing.</li><li>Availability.</li><li>Booking actions.</li></ul><p>Consistent structure was particularly important for a portfolio containing many different properties.</p><p>It allowed visitors to compare options without repeatedly learning a new page layout.</p><h3>Responsive design &amp; localization</h3><p>The website supports Ukrainian and English.</p><p>Both language versions were integrated into the customer-facing experience to make accommodation information accessible to local and international visitors.</p><p>The responsive design accounts for different screen sizes, including mobile browsing, where property discovery, date selection and booking actions require additional attention to hierarchy and touch interaction.</p>"],["Booking Experience & Payment Integration","<h3>Replacing a fragmented reservation process</h3><p>The original booking journey required multiple conversations, manual availability checks and separate payment arrangements.</p><p>I designed a connected booking experience that brought these actions into a structured digital flow.</p><p>The intended customer journey became:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Apartment Selection → Date Selection → Guest Details → Price Review → Online Payment → Booking Confirmation*</em></p><h3>Availability &amp; reservation validation</h3><p>Accommodation availability cannot be treated as a static piece of information.</p><p>A property may become unavailable between the moment a guest views the calendar and the moment they attempt to complete a reservation.</p><p>For this reason, availability is validated through server-side reservation logic.</p><p>The system includes safeguards against conflicting reservations and overlapping booking dates.</p><h3>Pricing logic</h3><p>The booking system was designed to account for accommodation-specific pricing conditions.</p><p>The checkout calculation can incorporate selected dates, length of stay, guest-related charges and security deposit requirements.</p><p>The aim was to present the relevant booking cost within the reservation process instead of relying on a separate conversation to establish the amount.</p><h3>Full online payment</h3><p>I integrated <strong>WayForPay</strong> to support online payment for the accommodation and security deposit.</p><p>Moving from a combination of bank transfers and cash to an integrated checkout experience was one of the project's most significant operational changes.</p><p>However, introducing digital payment also required careful handling of transaction states.</p><p>The implementation included signed payment requests, server-side verification, webhook processing, transaction status handling and reconciliation mechanisms.</p><p>A successful browser redirect alone was not treated as sufficient evidence of payment completion.</p><p>The system needed to verify the payment independently.</p><h3>Handling failure and uncertainty</h3><p>The reservation process also needed to account for situations where a payment was pending, failed, delayed or reported more than once.</p><p>These conditions informed the design of payment states and reservation safeguards.</p><p>The objective was to avoid both customer confusion and inconsistent booking records.</p><p>This work demonstrated that transactional UX involves more than a checkout interface.</p><p>It requires the visible customer journey and underlying system behavior to remain consistent under real-world conditions.</p>"],["Administrative Dashboard","<h3>Replacing Excel with an operational management system</h3><p>A major component of the project was the development of a dedicated administrative dashboard.</p><p>The original Excel-based workflow stored reservation information but did not provide a connected environment for coordinating the broader accommodation operation.</p><p>I designed and developed an interface organized around the tasks administrators actually needed to perform.</p><p>The dashboard consolidated information and actions that had previously been distributed across spreadsheets, messages and manual processes.</p><h3>Reservation management</h3><p>The system supports administrative reservation workflows, including:</p><ul><li>Reviewing existing reservations.</li><li>Creating bookings manually.</li><li>Modifying reservation details.</li><li>Extending stays.</li><li>Transferring reservations between apartments.</li><li>Managing reservation statuses.</li><li>Accessing guest and booking information.</li></ul><p>Manual booking creation was an important requirement because not every reservation originated through the public website.</p><p>The platform needed to accommodate existing business channels without forcing every customer through the same digital entry point.</p><h3>Visual reservation calendar</h3><p>I implemented a visual administrative calendar to provide a clearer view of apartment occupancy and upcoming bookings.</p><p>For a business managing 19 properties, seeing the relationships between apartments, dates and reservation statuses is more useful than examining isolated spreadsheet rows.</p><p>The calendar supports day-to-day planning and helps administrators understand the operational consequences of booking changes.</p><h3>Cleaning coordination</h3><p>Cleaning schedules are directly connected to check-outs and upcoming arrivals.</p><p>The administrative system introduced structured cleaning records and scheduling information based on reservation activity.</p><p>This helped connect apartment availability with the physical preparation required between guest stays.</p><p>Importantly, the software supports planning and coordination; the actual cleaning remains a human operational responsibility.</p><h3>Maintenance &amp; operational tasks</h3><p>The dashboard includes functionality for tracking maintenance issues and other property-related tasks.</p><p>This provides a more organized alternative to relying entirely on informal messages and separate records.</p><h3>Security deposits</h3><p>Deposit tracking was integrated into the administrative environment.</p><p>The system provides records and visibility around deposit-related responsibilities, while physical or financial actions that require administrator involvement remain controlled operational processes.</p><h3>Administrative UX principles</h3><p>The dashboard was designed around information clarity, operational context and task efficiency.</p><p>The goal was not to reproduce Excel inside a website.</p><p>It was to give administrators a purpose-built environment where reservation data could support meaningful actions.</p>"],["Business Analytics & Reporting","<p>Before the transformation, the business had limited centralized tools for examining operational performance.</p><p>Information relevant to occupancy, reservation value and individual properties existed, but evaluating it required manual calculation or reconciliation.</p><p>I introduced an analytics and reporting layer within the administrative platform.</p><p>The system organizes business information around reservation activity, occupancy, financial records and property-level performance.</p><p>This allows administrators to examine historical and upcoming activity through structured reporting rather than disconnected records.</p><p>The design also distinguishes operational information needed for immediate decisions from broader reporting used for planning.</p><p>The project established the infrastructure for better visibility into the business.</p><p>It did not, however, establish verified long-term improvements in revenue or occupancy attributable to the new platform.</p>"],["Telegram Bot & Guest Experience Automation","<h3>Designing the experience beyond checkout</h3><p>Completing a reservation is not the end of an accommodation journey.</p><p>Guests still need practical information about arrival, access, apartment rules and their stay.</p><p>Previously, much of this information was provided through direct communication with an administrator.</p><p>I developed a custom Telegram bot to support the guest experience after booking.</p><p>The bot was built with Python and aiogram and connected to the reservation ecosystem.</p><h3>Personalized booking access</h3><p>An early version of the concept involved reservation-specific MA codes.</p><p>During development, this approach evolved into personalized Telegram links.</p><p>Rather than remembering and manually entering a code, guests could open a reservation-linked Telegram experience.</p><p>The flow includes phone verification and account binding, allowing booking information to be associated with an authorized Telegram account.</p><p>This reduced friction in the intended guest journey while supporting controlled access to reservation details.</p><h3>Guest assistance</h3><p>The bot provides structured access to information relevant to the guest's stay.</p><p>This includes:</p><ul><li>Reservation information.</li><li>Pre-arrival guidance.</li><li>Check-in instructions.</li><li>Apartment-specific information.</li><li>FAQ navigation.</li><li>Help resources.</li><li>Administrator contact options.</li></ul><p>The interaction structure was designed around practical guest questions rather than an unnecessarily complex conversational interface.</p><h3>Contactless check-in</h3><p>A central feature was the connection between verified reservations and physical keybox access.</p><p>The system includes operational configuration for six physical keyboxes associated with the apartment portfolio.</p><p>Authorized guests can receive relevant access instructions according to their reservation and the applicable access conditions.</p><p>This required particular attention to the boundary between public information and restricted data.</p><p>An apartment description can be available to anyone.</p><p>Its physical access code cannot.</p><p>The bot therefore separates general guest assistance from information requiring current authorization.</p>"],["Security & Physical Access Control","<p>Because the platform connects digital reservations to physical accommodation, access security was an important design and engineering consideration.</p><p>Several safeguards were implemented across different stages of the guest journey.</p><h3>Reservation verification</h3><p>Restricted instructions are linked to verified booking information.</p><p>The bot checks relevant authorization conditions instead of relying solely on information recorded during a guest's first interaction.</p><p>This matters when a booking is cancelled, modified, completed or no longer authorized for access.</p><h3>Controlled keybox code activation</h3><p>One of the more important operational decisions concerned the relationship between digital records and physical keyboxes.</p><p>Updating a code in software does not automatically change the code installed on a physical lockbox.</p><p>The administrative workflow therefore separates entering a replacement code from confirming its physical activation.</p><p>A replacement code is not made available to guests until the administrator confirms that the physical keybox has been updated.</p><p>This prevents the system from distributing access information that may not yet work in the real world.</p><h3>Conflicting administrative changes</h3><p>The keybox management system also accounts for potential conflicts between administrative actions.</p><p>Version checks help prevent an outdated administrative view from overwriting more recent code information.</p><h3>Booking and payment integrity</h3><p>The reservation system incorporates server-side safeguards, including payment verification, duplicate-processing protection and database-level measures against overlapping bookings.</p><p>These safeguards are not primarily visual design features.</p><p>They are essential parts of making the customer-facing experience dependable.</p>"],["Technical Architecture & Development","<p>My work extended beyond product strategy and interface design into the implementation and integration of the underlying digital infrastructure.</p><p>The platform was developed as several connected components with distinct responsibilities.</p><h3>Public website</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Next.js · React · TypeScript*</em></p><p>The customer-facing web application supports apartment discovery, responsive interfaces, availability information and booking interactions.</p><h3>Database</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Neon PostgreSQL*</em></p><p>The database supports structured reservation data, apartment information and the relationships required by booking and administrative workflows.</p><h3>Payment system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*WayForPay*</em></p><p>The integration handles checkout requests, payment verification, status updates and transaction processing safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative platform</h3><p>The management interface connects reservation data with operational workflows, including property availability, cleaning schedules, guest records, financial tracking and reporting.</p><h3>Telegram automation</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Python · aiogram*</em></p><p>The separately developed bot provides authenticated guest assistance and controlled access to booking-related information.</p><h3>Hosting &amp; infrastructure</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Vercel · Render · SQLite · GitHub*</em></p><p>The website was deployed through Vercel.</p><p>The Telegram bot was migrated from Railway to Render, with persistent storage used for its operational state.</p><p>GitHub supported source control and the development workflow.</p><h3>My development responsibility</h3><p>I independently directed and implemented the connected system using modern development tools and AI-assisted workflows.</p><p>My responsibilities included translating business requirements into technical structures, designing interfaces, developing application functionality, coordinating integrations, evaluating system behavior, debugging and deployment.</p><p>The project required continuity between design decisions and engineering implementation.</p><p>A booking interaction could affect the database.</p><p>A payment confirmation could affect reservation status.</p><p>A change to a reservation could affect Telegram access permissions.</p><p>Understanding these dependencies was essential to delivering the project as a coherent product.</p>"],["Iteration & Problem-Solving","<p>The project was developed iteratively rather than through a completely fixed sequence of design and development phases.</p><p>Several important decisions changed as operational requirements became clearer.</p><h3>Iteration 01: MA codes to personalized links</h3><p><strong>Initial approach:</strong> Guests would access reservation information using manually entered booking codes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> This introduced an additional step and required guests to retain and enter a code.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Personalized Telegram links connected reservations to a verification and account-binding flow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Reduce unnecessary interaction while preserving controlled access to booking information.</p><h3>Iteration 02: Payment confirmation and reservation reliability</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Payment completion involved multiple systems and potentially asynchronous responses.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Browser redirects and payment notifications could not be treated as perfectly synchronized or independently authoritative.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Server-side verification, reconciliation and duplicate-processing safeguards became part of the transaction workflow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Protect reservation and financial records against delayed or repeated payment events.</p><h3>Iteration 03: Database-level booking protection</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Availability displayed in the interface could change while a customer was completing a reservation.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Interface validation alone could not reliably prevent conflicting bookings.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Additional server-side and database-level safeguards were incorporated.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Maintain reservation integrity even when multiple actions occur close together.</p><h3>Iteration 04: Physical keybox activation</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Digital code updates and physical lockbox changes happened through different processes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> A stored code could be distributed before the physical keybox was updated.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Separate code entry from administrator-confirmed physical activation.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Keep software permissions consistent with the real-world state of the property.</p><h3>Iteration 05: Infrastructure refinement</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> The Telegram service required reliable hosting and persistence of operational data.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> The bot was migrated from Railway to Render with attention to preserving stored information.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Support the continuity of a separately hosted service connected to the wider booking ecosystem.</p><p>These iterations illustrate an important aspect of the project: decisions were evaluated against operational requirements, not only against the appearance of individual interfaces.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The transformation changed how the business presented itself and how its core processes were organized.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Business Area</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>Generic apartment rental positioning</td><td>Maria Apartments brand identity</td></tr><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>Instagram and external listings</td><td>Branded social presence and official website</td></tr><tr><td>Apartment discovery</td><td>Direct inquiries and separate listings</td><td>Structured catalog of 19 apartments</td></tr><tr><td>Availability</td><td>Manual checks and spreadsheets</td><td>Integrated availability calendars</td></tr><tr><td>Booking</td><td>Administrator-coordinated process</td><td>Structured online reservation flow</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td>Bank transfer and cash</td><td>Integrated WayForPay checkout</td></tr><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Excel-based management</td><td>Dedicated management dashboard</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning</td><td>Manual scheduling</td><td>Reservation-connected cleaning records</td></tr><tr><td>Guest communication</td><td>Direct messaging and calls</td><td>Personalized Telegram assistance</td></tr><tr><td>Check-in</td><td>Physical coordination and key handover</td><td>Controlled contactless access workflow</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Manual calculations</td><td>Centralized operational analytics</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The value of these changes lies in their connection.</p><p>A redesigned website alone would not have resolved spreadsheet-based administration.</p><p>A booking form alone would not have solved payment verification.</p><p>A Telegram bot alone would not have provided reliable authorization.</p><p>The project required these elements to function as parts of one service.</p>"],["Results & Delivered Outcomes","<p>The project produced a complete set of interconnected creative, technical and operational deliverables.</p><h3>Brand transformation</h3><p>A new name, logo, visual identity, photography direction, social media presentation and printed brand materials established a consistent identity for the accommodation business.</p><h3>Customer-facing platform</h3><p>The business gained an official bilingual website presenting all 19 apartments through structured listings, individual property information, availability and booking functionality.</p><h3>Digital reservations &amp; payment</h3><p>A connected booking experience introduced online checkout, pricing calculations, payment-status handling and server-side reservation safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative management</h3><p>A centralized dashboard introduced structured reservation management, visual calendars, guest records, cleaning coordination, deposit tracking, maintenance records and business reporting.</p><h3>Automated guest assistance</h3><p>The Telegram system provided personalized booking access, verification, structured information and controlled check-in instructions.</p><h3>Connected infrastructure</h3><p>The website, database, payment gateway, administrative system and Telegram bot were integrated into a shared digital operating environment.</p><h3>Validation &amp; measurement</h3><p>The project documentation includes automated tests and deployment checks supporting the technical implementation.</p><p>However, the available evidence does not establish a controlled long-term comparison of revenue, conversion rates, customer satisfaction or operational cost savings.</p><p>Some real-world scenarios, including comprehensive production payment validation and physical check-in success rates, require further documented evaluation.</p><p>For this reason, I distinguish between delivered functionality, expected operational benefits and independently measured business outcomes.</p><p>The most defensible result is the transformation of previously fragmented workflows into a connected digital system.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>Maria Apartments was a multidisciplinary project in which I worked across responsibilities commonly divided between several specialist roles.</p><p>My involvement began with understanding the business and continued through creative direction, interface design, technical development and deployment.</p><p>I was responsible for translating operational problems into product requirements, determining the experience across customer and administrative interfaces, implementing connected functionality and refining decisions as technical and business constraints emerged.</p><p>The project combined several professional disciplines:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Product Strategy*</em></p><p>Understanding the rental operation, defining requirements and organizing the relationship between digital tools and business processes.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI &amp; Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Designing discovery, booking, payment, administration and guest assistance experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Identity &amp; Creative Direction*</em></p><p>Establishing a recognizable identity and applying it consistently across digital and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Photography &amp; Content Production*</em></p><p>Creating and preparing property photography, branded visuals and social media materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Web &amp; Application Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive interfaces, server-side functionality, database connections and administrative features.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Integrations &amp; Automation*</em></p><p>Connecting payment processing, reservation records, Telegram assistance and physical access workflows.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Deployment*</em></p><p>Validating functionality, addressing integration challenges and managing production-oriented technical delivery.</p><p>This breadth of responsibility required an understanding of the entire service rather than a narrow focus on one design deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>Maria Apartments fundamentally expanded the scope of what began as a branding and digital presence project.</p><p>The most important challenge was not creating a visually attractive website.</p><p>It was developing a reliable relationship between customer interactions, administrative processes, financial transactions, stored data and physical accommodation.</p><p>Working directly with the business revealed how easily a small operational detail can affect the wider user experience.</p><p>A confusing payment state can create uncertainty for a guest.</p><p>An overlapping reservation can create a serious administrative problem.</p><p>An incorrectly activated keybox code can prevent someone from entering an apartment.</p><p>A disconnected cleaning schedule can affect whether accommodation is ready for the next arrival.</p><p>These are not isolated interface problems.</p><p>They are service problems that require coordinated product, design and engineering decisions.</p><p>The experience reinforced the importance of understanding a business before selecting technologies or designing screens.</p><p>It also demonstrated the value of maintaining responsibility across the complete development process.</p><p>Maria Apartments represents my ability to work independently across brand identity, product strategy, UX/UI design, software development, technical integrations and operational automation.</p><p>The final result is more than a website.</p><p>It is a connected digital foundation for an accommodation business, built around the practical needs of guests, administrators and the service itself.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*A complete hospitality experience, designed from the first impression to the apartment door.*</em></p>"]]},"pl":{"name":"MARIA APARTMENTS","industry":"Hotelarstwo / zarządzanie nieruchomościami","platform":"Strona · platforma rezerwacji · automatyzacja biznesu","location":"Tarnopol, Ukraina","role":"UX/UI · web design · identyfikacja marki · operacje","scope":"Website structure · Booking experience · Guest communication · Operations","strapline":"A digital product shaped by direct operational knowledge","links":[{"label":"MARIA APARTMENTS","href":"https://www.bymariaapartments.com"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>Maria Apartments is a short-term accommodation business operating a portfolio of 19 apartments across several locations in Ternopil, Ukraine.</p><p>The project began with an established rental business that had customers, properties and operational experience, but lacked a unified brand identity and an integrated digital infrastructure.</p><p>Reservations were managed through Excel spreadsheets. Guests relied on direct communication to check availability, confirm bookings and arrange their arrival. Payments involved bank transfers and cash, while key handovers required physical coordination. Instagram served as an important customer-facing channel, but there was no dedicated platform connecting apartment discovery, reservations, payments and internal operations.</p><p>My responsibility was to transform this fragmented experience into a coherent digital ecosystem.</p><p>I independently managed the project across creative direction, user experience, technical implementation and operational integration. The work included establishing a new brand, producing visual content, designing and developing a bilingual accommodation website, implementing a booking and payment system, creating an administrative management platform and developing a custom Telegram bot for guest assistance and contactless check-in.</p><p>The result was not simply a redesigned website.</p><p>It was a connected hospitality management system designed to support the relationship between guests, administrators, reservations, payments, physical properties and daily business operations.</p>"],["Understanding the Business","<h3>The original operating model</h3><p>Before the transformation, the business operated under the generic description &quot;Daily Apartment Rentals&quot; rather than a recognizable independent brand.</p><p>Individual apartments were presented primarily through existing listings and social media. Customers frequently needed to contact an administrator to obtain information that could otherwise have been available through a self-service platform.</p><p>The reservation process depended on several disconnected interactions.</p><p>A potential guest would inquire about an apartment, ask about available dates, receive pricing information and coordinate payment directly with the business.</p><p>A security deposit was typically transferred to a bank card, while the remaining accommodation cost was paid in cash upon arrival.</p><p>After booking, the administrator recorded reservation details in Excel, coordinated arrival arrangements, managed physical keys and later processed the deposit return.</p><p>Other activities, including cleaning schedules, property maintenance and financial tracking, required additional manual coordination.</p><p>These processes were functional individually, but their separation created unnecessary dependencies.</p><h3>The core business problems</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Fragmented information*</em></p><p>Reservation records, apartment availability, payments, guest communication and operational tasks were managed through separate channels.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Administrative dependency*</em></p><p>Routine guest questions and booking actions required direct staff involvement.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited self-service*</em></p><p>Customers could not complete the entire accommodation journey independently through an official website.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Payment friction*</em></p><p>Financial transactions were divided between different payment methods and stages of the stay.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Operational complexity*</em></p><p>Managing 19 properties required ongoing coordination of arrivals, departures, cleaning, maintenance and availability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Inconsistent brand presentation*</em></p><p>The absence of a unified identity made the business appear as a collection of rental listings rather than a recognizable accommodation provider.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited reporting*</em></p><p>Without integrated analytics, evaluating occupancy, reservation activity and property-level performance required manual work.</p><p>The challenge was to address these problems as parts of one system rather than introduce isolated solutions.</p>"],["Discovery & UX Research","<p>My research approach was strongly influenced by direct participation in the business's daily operations.</p><p>Rather than relying exclusively on a design brief, I gained firsthand insight into guest communication, booking coordination, arrival procedures and administrative responsibilities.</p><p>This allowed me to examine the complete service experience, including interactions that happened outside a conventional website.</p><p>I analyzed how guests obtained information, how availability was confirmed, how payments were coordinated and what administrators needed to prepare an apartment for the next arrival.</p><p>I also examined the relationships between reservations, cleaning schedules, security deposits and physical access.</p><p>This revealed an important distinction between the needs of different users.</p><h3>Guest needs</h3><p>Guests needed to:</p><ul><li>Find suitable accommodation without extensive messaging.</li><li>Compare apartments by location, price, capacity and amenities.</li><li>Understand availability and accommodation costs.</li><li>Complete a reservation with clear payment instructions.</li><li>Receive reliable confirmation.</li><li>Access practical arrival and accommodation information.</li><li>Obtain assistance without repeating their booking details.</li></ul><h3>Administrator needs</h3><p>Administrators needed to:</p><ul><li>View reservations across all 19 apartments.</li><li>Identify occupied and available dates.</li><li>Create and modify bookings.</li><li>Coordinate cleaning between departures and arrivals.</li><li>Track deposits and reservation payments.</li><li>Manage apartment information and operational issues.</li><li>Prepare guests for check-in.</li><li>Control physical keybox access.</li><li>Access structured business reporting.</li></ul><h3>Research conclusion</h3><p>The principal challenge was not a lack of individual digital tools.</p><p>It was the absence of a shared system connecting customer-facing experiences with operational data.</p><p>This conclusion shaped the architecture of the entire project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>Research methodology note: The discovery process was based on operational observation, firsthand experience and workflow analysis rather than a formal controlled user study.</em></p>"],["Product Strategy","<p>I defined the transformation around a connected set of digital and physical touchpoints.</p><p>The strategy had three major objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Establish a recognizable hospitality brand*</em></p><p>Create a consistent identity capable of representing all 19 apartments across digital channels and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Build an independent customer booking journey*</em></p><p>Allow guests to discover accommodation, check dates, understand pricing, book and pay without requiring continuous administrator assistance.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Centralize internal operations*</em></p><p>Provide administrators with a dedicated management environment connected to reservations, payment records, apartment availability, cleaning and guest communication.</p><p>A fourth consideration influenced the entire project: the connection between software and physical accommodation.</p><p>A successful online reservation still needed to result in a guest arriving at the correct property, receiving authorized access and finding an apartment prepared for occupancy.</p><p>This made service design and operational reliability just as important as visual interface design.</p>"],["Brand Identity & Creative Direction","<h3>From a generic rental service to Maria Apartments</h3><p>The transformation began with a new brand identity.</p><p>I developed the name <strong>Maria Apartments</strong>, replacing the original generic positioning with an identifiable hospitality brand.</p><p>The new identity needed to communicate comfort, reliability and professional management without feeling excessively corporate or disconnected from the nature of the accommodation service.</p><p>I designed the logo, established the visual direction and created a consistent system of typography, color and supporting visual elements.</p><p>A warm, light visual approach was selected to make the brand feel welcoming while maintaining clarity across functional interfaces.</p><p>The identity was designed to work in several environments: property listings, booking forms, Instagram content, printed materials and physical guest touchpoints.</p><h3>Photography &amp; property presentation</h3><p>Accommodation is a visually driven service.</p><p>Guests rely heavily on photographs to assess the condition, atmosphere and suitability of a property.</p><p>I photographed apartment interiors, selected and retouched images, and prepared visual assets for consistent presentation across the website and social media.</p><p>The objective was not to make different properties appear identical.</p><p>It was to establish a consistent standard of visual quality while preserving the individual characteristics of each apartment.</p><h3>Physical brand materials</h3><p>I also designed branded business cards and keychains, prepared them for production and coordinated ordering.</p><p>These materials extended the new identity beyond screens.</p><p>The keychains were particularly relevant because physical access was an essential part of the accommodation experience.</p><p>Connecting digital presentation with physical objects helped establish continuity throughout the guest journey.</p>"],["Instagram Rebranding & Digital Presence","<p>Instagram was already an important discovery and communication channel for the business.</p><p>However, the existing account lacked the coherence of a unified brand.</p><p>I redesigned the profile presentation, developed a consistent content direction and established a visual system aligned with the new Maria Apartments identity.</p><p>The work included updated profile information, visual content, post design, publication and organized navigation through Instagram Highlights.</p><p>I also connected the social media presence to the official website.</p><p>This reflected a deliberate strategic decision.</p><p>Instagram would remain a channel for discovery, brand communication and customer engagement, while the website would become the primary environment for property exploration, reservations and payments.</p><p>The objective was to reduce dependence on manual social media conversations as the main booking infrastructure.</p>"],["Website UX/UI Design","<h3>A website designed around the complete booking journey</h3><p>The official Maria Apartments website was developed as a responsive, bilingual platform supporting all 19 apartments.</p><p>Its primary purpose was to transform accommodation discovery into a structured and actionable experience.</p><p>The information architecture was organized around the guest's practical decision-making process:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover → Compare → Evaluate → Check Availability → Book → Pay → Receive Confirmation*</em></p><p>Each stage needed to provide enough information for the user to proceed confidently.</p><h3>Interactive building facade</h3><p>One of the distinctive visual features of the website is its interactive building facade.</p><p>Instead of functioning as a purely decorative homepage illustration, the building provides an alternative way to explore available accommodation.</p><p>The interactive windows connect the visual presentation to apartment discovery.</p><p>This approach gave the business a memorable digital identity while making the interface itself part of the experience.</p><p>However, visual originality was not allowed to replace usability.</p><p>The interactive building was supported by more conventional browsing tools, ensuring that customers could still explore properties through familiar catalog and filtering patterns.</p><p>The design decision balanced brand differentiation with practical navigation.</p><h3>Apartment catalog &amp; filtering</h3><p>The website includes a structured catalog of 19 apartments, allowing guests to explore different properties within a consistent interface.</p><p>Filtering supports practical accommodation decisions, including available dates, guest capacity, location and pricing considerations.</p><p>Because properties are distributed across different areas of Ternopil, location-based navigation was an important requirement.</p><p>The catalog was designed to help visitors narrow their options before committing to a detailed property review.</p><h3>Individual apartment pages</h3><p>Each apartment needed enough information to support an informed booking decision.</p><p>I organized property pages around consistent content categories, including:</p><ul><li>Apartment photography.</li><li>Descriptions and accommodation characteristics.</li><li>Guest capacity.</li><li>Relevant amenities.</li><li>Location information.</li><li>Pricing.</li><li>Availability.</li><li>Booking actions.</li></ul><p>Consistent structure was particularly important for a portfolio containing many different properties.</p><p>It allowed visitors to compare options without repeatedly learning a new page layout.</p><h3>Responsive design &amp; localization</h3><p>The website supports Ukrainian and English.</p><p>Both language versions were integrated into the customer-facing experience to make accommodation information accessible to local and international visitors.</p><p>The responsive design accounts for different screen sizes, including mobile browsing, where property discovery, date selection and booking actions require additional attention to hierarchy and touch interaction.</p>"],["Booking Experience & Payment Integration","<h3>Replacing a fragmented reservation process</h3><p>The original booking journey required multiple conversations, manual availability checks and separate payment arrangements.</p><p>I designed a connected booking experience that brought these actions into a structured digital flow.</p><p>The intended customer journey became:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Apartment Selection → Date Selection → Guest Details → Price Review → Online Payment → Booking Confirmation*</em></p><h3>Availability &amp; reservation validation</h3><p>Accommodation availability cannot be treated as a static piece of information.</p><p>A property may become unavailable between the moment a guest views the calendar and the moment they attempt to complete a reservation.</p><p>For this reason, availability is validated through server-side reservation logic.</p><p>The system includes safeguards against conflicting reservations and overlapping booking dates.</p><h3>Pricing logic</h3><p>The booking system was designed to account for accommodation-specific pricing conditions.</p><p>The checkout calculation can incorporate selected dates, length of stay, guest-related charges and security deposit requirements.</p><p>The aim was to present the relevant booking cost within the reservation process instead of relying on a separate conversation to establish the amount.</p><h3>Full online payment</h3><p>I integrated <strong>WayForPay</strong> to support online payment for the accommodation and security deposit.</p><p>Moving from a combination of bank transfers and cash to an integrated checkout experience was one of the project's most significant operational changes.</p><p>However, introducing digital payment also required careful handling of transaction states.</p><p>The implementation included signed payment requests, server-side verification, webhook processing, transaction status handling and reconciliation mechanisms.</p><p>A successful browser redirect alone was not treated as sufficient evidence of payment completion.</p><p>The system needed to verify the payment independently.</p><h3>Handling failure and uncertainty</h3><p>The reservation process also needed to account for situations where a payment was pending, failed, delayed or reported more than once.</p><p>These conditions informed the design of payment states and reservation safeguards.</p><p>The objective was to avoid both customer confusion and inconsistent booking records.</p><p>This work demonstrated that transactional UX involves more than a checkout interface.</p><p>It requires the visible customer journey and underlying system behavior to remain consistent under real-world conditions.</p>"],["Administrative Dashboard","<h3>Replacing Excel with an operational management system</h3><p>A major component of the project was the development of a dedicated administrative dashboard.</p><p>The original Excel-based workflow stored reservation information but did not provide a connected environment for coordinating the broader accommodation operation.</p><p>I designed and developed an interface organized around the tasks administrators actually needed to perform.</p><p>The dashboard consolidated information and actions that had previously been distributed across spreadsheets, messages and manual processes.</p><h3>Reservation management</h3><p>The system supports administrative reservation workflows, including:</p><ul><li>Reviewing existing reservations.</li><li>Creating bookings manually.</li><li>Modifying reservation details.</li><li>Extending stays.</li><li>Transferring reservations between apartments.</li><li>Managing reservation statuses.</li><li>Accessing guest and booking information.</li></ul><p>Manual booking creation was an important requirement because not every reservation originated through the public website.</p><p>The platform needed to accommodate existing business channels without forcing every customer through the same digital entry point.</p><h3>Visual reservation calendar</h3><p>I implemented a visual administrative calendar to provide a clearer view of apartment occupancy and upcoming bookings.</p><p>For a business managing 19 properties, seeing the relationships between apartments, dates and reservation statuses is more useful than examining isolated spreadsheet rows.</p><p>The calendar supports day-to-day planning and helps administrators understand the operational consequences of booking changes.</p><h3>Cleaning coordination</h3><p>Cleaning schedules are directly connected to check-outs and upcoming arrivals.</p><p>The administrative system introduced structured cleaning records and scheduling information based on reservation activity.</p><p>This helped connect apartment availability with the physical preparation required between guest stays.</p><p>Importantly, the software supports planning and coordination; the actual cleaning remains a human operational responsibility.</p><h3>Maintenance &amp; operational tasks</h3><p>The dashboard includes functionality for tracking maintenance issues and other property-related tasks.</p><p>This provides a more organized alternative to relying entirely on informal messages and separate records.</p><h3>Security deposits</h3><p>Deposit tracking was integrated into the administrative environment.</p><p>The system provides records and visibility around deposit-related responsibilities, while physical or financial actions that require administrator involvement remain controlled operational processes.</p><h3>Administrative UX principles</h3><p>The dashboard was designed around information clarity, operational context and task efficiency.</p><p>The goal was not to reproduce Excel inside a website.</p><p>It was to give administrators a purpose-built environment where reservation data could support meaningful actions.</p>"],["Business Analytics & Reporting","<p>Before the transformation, the business had limited centralized tools for examining operational performance.</p><p>Information relevant to occupancy, reservation value and individual properties existed, but evaluating it required manual calculation or reconciliation.</p><p>I introduced an analytics and reporting layer within the administrative platform.</p><p>The system organizes business information around reservation activity, occupancy, financial records and property-level performance.</p><p>This allows administrators to examine historical and upcoming activity through structured reporting rather than disconnected records.</p><p>The design also distinguishes operational information needed for immediate decisions from broader reporting used for planning.</p><p>The project established the infrastructure for better visibility into the business.</p><p>It did not, however, establish verified long-term improvements in revenue or occupancy attributable to the new platform.</p>"],["Telegram Bot & Guest Experience Automation","<h3>Designing the experience beyond checkout</h3><p>Completing a reservation is not the end of an accommodation journey.</p><p>Guests still need practical information about arrival, access, apartment rules and their stay.</p><p>Previously, much of this information was provided through direct communication with an administrator.</p><p>I developed a custom Telegram bot to support the guest experience after booking.</p><p>The bot was built with Python and aiogram and connected to the reservation ecosystem.</p><h3>Personalized booking access</h3><p>An early version of the concept involved reservation-specific MA codes.</p><p>During development, this approach evolved into personalized Telegram links.</p><p>Rather than remembering and manually entering a code, guests could open a reservation-linked Telegram experience.</p><p>The flow includes phone verification and account binding, allowing booking information to be associated with an authorized Telegram account.</p><p>This reduced friction in the intended guest journey while supporting controlled access to reservation details.</p><h3>Guest assistance</h3><p>The bot provides structured access to information relevant to the guest's stay.</p><p>This includes:</p><ul><li>Reservation information.</li><li>Pre-arrival guidance.</li><li>Check-in instructions.</li><li>Apartment-specific information.</li><li>FAQ navigation.</li><li>Help resources.</li><li>Administrator contact options.</li></ul><p>The interaction structure was designed around practical guest questions rather than an unnecessarily complex conversational interface.</p><h3>Contactless check-in</h3><p>A central feature was the connection between verified reservations and physical keybox access.</p><p>The system includes operational configuration for six physical keyboxes associated with the apartment portfolio.</p><p>Authorized guests can receive relevant access instructions according to their reservation and the applicable access conditions.</p><p>This required particular attention to the boundary between public information and restricted data.</p><p>An apartment description can be available to anyone.</p><p>Its physical access code cannot.</p><p>The bot therefore separates general guest assistance from information requiring current authorization.</p>"],["Security & Physical Access Control","<p>Because the platform connects digital reservations to physical accommodation, access security was an important design and engineering consideration.</p><p>Several safeguards were implemented across different stages of the guest journey.</p><h3>Reservation verification</h3><p>Restricted instructions are linked to verified booking information.</p><p>The bot checks relevant authorization conditions instead of relying solely on information recorded during a guest's first interaction.</p><p>This matters when a booking is cancelled, modified, completed or no longer authorized for access.</p><h3>Controlled keybox code activation</h3><p>One of the more important operational decisions concerned the relationship between digital records and physical keyboxes.</p><p>Updating a code in software does not automatically change the code installed on a physical lockbox.</p><p>The administrative workflow therefore separates entering a replacement code from confirming its physical activation.</p><p>A replacement code is not made available to guests until the administrator confirms that the physical keybox has been updated.</p><p>This prevents the system from distributing access information that may not yet work in the real world.</p><h3>Conflicting administrative changes</h3><p>The keybox management system also accounts for potential conflicts between administrative actions.</p><p>Version checks help prevent an outdated administrative view from overwriting more recent code information.</p><h3>Booking and payment integrity</h3><p>The reservation system incorporates server-side safeguards, including payment verification, duplicate-processing protection and database-level measures against overlapping bookings.</p><p>These safeguards are not primarily visual design features.</p><p>They are essential parts of making the customer-facing experience dependable.</p>"],["Technical Architecture & Development","<p>My work extended beyond product strategy and interface design into the implementation and integration of the underlying digital infrastructure.</p><p>The platform was developed as several connected components with distinct responsibilities.</p><h3>Public website</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Next.js · React · TypeScript*</em></p><p>The customer-facing web application supports apartment discovery, responsive interfaces, availability information and booking interactions.</p><h3>Database</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Neon PostgreSQL*</em></p><p>The database supports structured reservation data, apartment information and the relationships required by booking and administrative workflows.</p><h3>Payment system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*WayForPay*</em></p><p>The integration handles checkout requests, payment verification, status updates and transaction processing safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative platform</h3><p>The management interface connects reservation data with operational workflows, including property availability, cleaning schedules, guest records, financial tracking and reporting.</p><h3>Telegram automation</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Python · aiogram*</em></p><p>The separately developed bot provides authenticated guest assistance and controlled access to booking-related information.</p><h3>Hosting &amp; infrastructure</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Vercel · Render · SQLite · GitHub*</em></p><p>The website was deployed through Vercel.</p><p>The Telegram bot was migrated from Railway to Render, with persistent storage used for its operational state.</p><p>GitHub supported source control and the development workflow.</p><h3>My development responsibility</h3><p>I independently directed and implemented the connected system using modern development tools and AI-assisted workflows.</p><p>My responsibilities included translating business requirements into technical structures, designing interfaces, developing application functionality, coordinating integrations, evaluating system behavior, debugging and deployment.</p><p>The project required continuity between design decisions and engineering implementation.</p><p>A booking interaction could affect the database.</p><p>A payment confirmation could affect reservation status.</p><p>A change to a reservation could affect Telegram access permissions.</p><p>Understanding these dependencies was essential to delivering the project as a coherent product.</p>"],["Iteration & Problem-Solving","<p>The project was developed iteratively rather than through a completely fixed sequence of design and development phases.</p><p>Several important decisions changed as operational requirements became clearer.</p><h3>Iteration 01: MA codes to personalized links</h3><p><strong>Initial approach:</strong> Guests would access reservation information using manually entered booking codes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> This introduced an additional step and required guests to retain and enter a code.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Personalized Telegram links connected reservations to a verification and account-binding flow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Reduce unnecessary interaction while preserving controlled access to booking information.</p><h3>Iteration 02: Payment confirmation and reservation reliability</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Payment completion involved multiple systems and potentially asynchronous responses.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Browser redirects and payment notifications could not be treated as perfectly synchronized or independently authoritative.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Server-side verification, reconciliation and duplicate-processing safeguards became part of the transaction workflow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Protect reservation and financial records against delayed or repeated payment events.</p><h3>Iteration 03: Database-level booking protection</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Availability displayed in the interface could change while a customer was completing a reservation.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Interface validation alone could not reliably prevent conflicting bookings.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Additional server-side and database-level safeguards were incorporated.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Maintain reservation integrity even when multiple actions occur close together.</p><h3>Iteration 04: Physical keybox activation</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Digital code updates and physical lockbox changes happened through different processes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> A stored code could be distributed before the physical keybox was updated.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Separate code entry from administrator-confirmed physical activation.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Keep software permissions consistent with the real-world state of the property.</p><h3>Iteration 05: Infrastructure refinement</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> The Telegram service required reliable hosting and persistence of operational data.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> The bot was migrated from Railway to Render with attention to preserving stored information.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Support the continuity of a separately hosted service connected to the wider booking ecosystem.</p><p>These iterations illustrate an important aspect of the project: decisions were evaluated against operational requirements, not only against the appearance of individual interfaces.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The transformation changed how the business presented itself and how its core processes were organized.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Business Area</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>Generic apartment rental positioning</td><td>Maria Apartments brand identity</td></tr><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>Instagram and external listings</td><td>Branded social presence and official website</td></tr><tr><td>Apartment discovery</td><td>Direct inquiries and separate listings</td><td>Structured catalog of 19 apartments</td></tr><tr><td>Availability</td><td>Manual checks and spreadsheets</td><td>Integrated availability calendars</td></tr><tr><td>Booking</td><td>Administrator-coordinated process</td><td>Structured online reservation flow</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td>Bank transfer and cash</td><td>Integrated WayForPay checkout</td></tr><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Excel-based management</td><td>Dedicated management dashboard</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning</td><td>Manual scheduling</td><td>Reservation-connected cleaning records</td></tr><tr><td>Guest communication</td><td>Direct messaging and calls</td><td>Personalized Telegram assistance</td></tr><tr><td>Check-in</td><td>Physical coordination and key handover</td><td>Controlled contactless access workflow</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Manual calculations</td><td>Centralized operational analytics</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The value of these changes lies in their connection.</p><p>A redesigned website alone would not have resolved spreadsheet-based administration.</p><p>A booking form alone would not have solved payment verification.</p><p>A Telegram bot alone would not have provided reliable authorization.</p><p>The project required these elements to function as parts of one service.</p>"],["Results & Delivered Outcomes","<p>The project produced a complete set of interconnected creative, technical and operational deliverables.</p><h3>Brand transformation</h3><p>A new name, logo, visual identity, photography direction, social media presentation and printed brand materials established a consistent identity for the accommodation business.</p><h3>Customer-facing platform</h3><p>The business gained an official bilingual website presenting all 19 apartments through structured listings, individual property information, availability and booking functionality.</p><h3>Digital reservations &amp; payment</h3><p>A connected booking experience introduced online checkout, pricing calculations, payment-status handling and server-side reservation safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative management</h3><p>A centralized dashboard introduced structured reservation management, visual calendars, guest records, cleaning coordination, deposit tracking, maintenance records and business reporting.</p><h3>Automated guest assistance</h3><p>The Telegram system provided personalized booking access, verification, structured information and controlled check-in instructions.</p><h3>Connected infrastructure</h3><p>The website, database, payment gateway, administrative system and Telegram bot were integrated into a shared digital operating environment.</p><h3>Validation &amp; measurement</h3><p>The project documentation includes automated tests and deployment checks supporting the technical implementation.</p><p>However, the available evidence does not establish a controlled long-term comparison of revenue, conversion rates, customer satisfaction or operational cost savings.</p><p>Some real-world scenarios, including comprehensive production payment validation and physical check-in success rates, require further documented evaluation.</p><p>For this reason, I distinguish between delivered functionality, expected operational benefits and independently measured business outcomes.</p><p>The most defensible result is the transformation of previously fragmented workflows into a connected digital system.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>Maria Apartments was a multidisciplinary project in which I worked across responsibilities commonly divided between several specialist roles.</p><p>My involvement began with understanding the business and continued through creative direction, interface design, technical development and deployment.</p><p>I was responsible for translating operational problems into product requirements, determining the experience across customer and administrative interfaces, implementing connected functionality and refining decisions as technical and business constraints emerged.</p><p>The project combined several professional disciplines:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Product Strategy*</em></p><p>Understanding the rental operation, defining requirements and organizing the relationship between digital tools and business processes.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI &amp; Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Designing discovery, booking, payment, administration and guest assistance experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Identity &amp; Creative Direction*</em></p><p>Establishing a recognizable identity and applying it consistently across digital and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Photography &amp; Content Production*</em></p><p>Creating and preparing property photography, branded visuals and social media materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Web &amp; Application Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive interfaces, server-side functionality, database connections and administrative features.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Integrations &amp; Automation*</em></p><p>Connecting payment processing, reservation records, Telegram assistance and physical access workflows.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Deployment*</em></p><p>Validating functionality, addressing integration challenges and managing production-oriented technical delivery.</p><p>This breadth of responsibility required an understanding of the entire service rather than a narrow focus on one design deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>Maria Apartments fundamentally expanded the scope of what began as a branding and digital presence project.</p><p>The most important challenge was not creating a visually attractive website.</p><p>It was developing a reliable relationship between customer interactions, administrative processes, financial transactions, stored data and physical accommodation.</p><p>Working directly with the business revealed how easily a small operational detail can affect the wider user experience.</p><p>A confusing payment state can create uncertainty for a guest.</p><p>An overlapping reservation can create a serious administrative problem.</p><p>An incorrectly activated keybox code can prevent someone from entering an apartment.</p><p>A disconnected cleaning schedule can affect whether accommodation is ready for the next arrival.</p><p>These are not isolated interface problems.</p><p>They are service problems that require coordinated product, design and engineering decisions.</p><p>The experience reinforced the importance of understanding a business before selecting technologies or designing screens.</p><p>It also demonstrated the value of maintaining responsibility across the complete development process.</p><p>Maria Apartments represents my ability to work independently across brand identity, product strategy, UX/UI design, software development, technical integrations and operational automation.</p><p>The final result is more than a website.</p><p>It is a connected digital foundation for an accommodation business, built around the practical needs of guests, administrators and the service itself.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*A complete hospitality experience, designed from the first impression to the apartment door.*</em></p>"]]},"ru":{"name":"MARIA APARTMENTS","industry":"Гостиничный бизнес / управление недвижимостью","platform":"Сайт · платформа бронирования · автоматизация бизнеса","location":"Тернополь, Украина","role":"UX/UI · веб-дизайн · айдентика · операционные процессы","scope":"Website structure · Booking experience · Guest communication · Operations","strapline":"A digital product shaped by direct operational knowledge","links":[{"label":"MARIA APARTMENTS","href":"https://www.bymariaapartments.com"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>Maria Apartments is a short-term accommodation business operating a portfolio of 19 apartments across several locations in Ternopil, Ukraine.</p><p>The project began with an established rental business that had customers, properties and operational experience, but lacked a unified brand identity and an integrated digital infrastructure.</p><p>Reservations were managed through Excel spreadsheets. Guests relied on direct communication to check availability, confirm bookings and arrange their arrival. Payments involved bank transfers and cash, while key handovers required physical coordination. Instagram served as an important customer-facing channel, but there was no dedicated platform connecting apartment discovery, reservations, payments and internal operations.</p><p>My responsibility was to transform this fragmented experience into a coherent digital ecosystem.</p><p>I independently managed the project across creative direction, user experience, technical implementation and operational integration. The work included establishing a new brand, producing visual content, designing and developing a bilingual accommodation website, implementing a booking and payment system, creating an administrative management platform and developing a custom Telegram bot for guest assistance and contactless check-in.</p><p>The result was not simply a redesigned website.</p><p>It was a connected hospitality management system designed to support the relationship between guests, administrators, reservations, payments, physical properties and daily business operations.</p>"],["Understanding the Business","<h3>The original operating model</h3><p>Before the transformation, the business operated under the generic description &quot;Daily Apartment Rentals&quot; rather than a recognizable independent brand.</p><p>Individual apartments were presented primarily through existing listings and social media. Customers frequently needed to contact an administrator to obtain information that could otherwise have been available through a self-service platform.</p><p>The reservation process depended on several disconnected interactions.</p><p>A potential guest would inquire about an apartment, ask about available dates, receive pricing information and coordinate payment directly with the business.</p><p>A security deposit was typically transferred to a bank card, while the remaining accommodation cost was paid in cash upon arrival.</p><p>After booking, the administrator recorded reservation details in Excel, coordinated arrival arrangements, managed physical keys and later processed the deposit return.</p><p>Other activities, including cleaning schedules, property maintenance and financial tracking, required additional manual coordination.</p><p>These processes were functional individually, but their separation created unnecessary dependencies.</p><h3>The core business problems</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Fragmented information*</em></p><p>Reservation records, apartment availability, payments, guest communication and operational tasks were managed through separate channels.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Administrative dependency*</em></p><p>Routine guest questions and booking actions required direct staff involvement.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited self-service*</em></p><p>Customers could not complete the entire accommodation journey independently through an official website.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Payment friction*</em></p><p>Financial transactions were divided between different payment methods and stages of the stay.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Operational complexity*</em></p><p>Managing 19 properties required ongoing coordination of arrivals, departures, cleaning, maintenance and availability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Inconsistent brand presentation*</em></p><p>The absence of a unified identity made the business appear as a collection of rental listings rather than a recognizable accommodation provider.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited reporting*</em></p><p>Without integrated analytics, evaluating occupancy, reservation activity and property-level performance required manual work.</p><p>The challenge was to address these problems as parts of one system rather than introduce isolated solutions.</p>"],["Discovery & UX Research","<p>My research approach was strongly influenced by direct participation in the business's daily operations.</p><p>Rather than relying exclusively on a design brief, I gained firsthand insight into guest communication, booking coordination, arrival procedures and administrative responsibilities.</p><p>This allowed me to examine the complete service experience, including interactions that happened outside a conventional website.</p><p>I analyzed how guests obtained information, how availability was confirmed, how payments were coordinated and what administrators needed to prepare an apartment for the next arrival.</p><p>I also examined the relationships between reservations, cleaning schedules, security deposits and physical access.</p><p>This revealed an important distinction between the needs of different users.</p><h3>Guest needs</h3><p>Guests needed to:</p><ul><li>Find suitable accommodation without extensive messaging.</li><li>Compare apartments by location, price, capacity and amenities.</li><li>Understand availability and accommodation costs.</li><li>Complete a reservation with clear payment instructions.</li><li>Receive reliable confirmation.</li><li>Access practical arrival and accommodation information.</li><li>Obtain assistance without repeating their booking details.</li></ul><h3>Administrator needs</h3><p>Administrators needed to:</p><ul><li>View reservations across all 19 apartments.</li><li>Identify occupied and available dates.</li><li>Create and modify bookings.</li><li>Coordinate cleaning between departures and arrivals.</li><li>Track deposits and reservation payments.</li><li>Manage apartment information and operational issues.</li><li>Prepare guests for check-in.</li><li>Control physical keybox access.</li><li>Access structured business reporting.</li></ul><h3>Research conclusion</h3><p>The principal challenge was not a lack of individual digital tools.</p><p>It was the absence of a shared system connecting customer-facing experiences with operational data.</p><p>This conclusion shaped the architecture of the entire project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>Research methodology note: The discovery process was based on operational observation, firsthand experience and workflow analysis rather than a formal controlled user study.</em></p>"],["Product Strategy","<p>I defined the transformation around a connected set of digital and physical touchpoints.</p><p>The strategy had three major objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Establish a recognizable hospitality brand*</em></p><p>Create a consistent identity capable of representing all 19 apartments across digital channels and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Build an independent customer booking journey*</em></p><p>Allow guests to discover accommodation, check dates, understand pricing, book and pay without requiring continuous administrator assistance.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Centralize internal operations*</em></p><p>Provide administrators with a dedicated management environment connected to reservations, payment records, apartment availability, cleaning and guest communication.</p><p>A fourth consideration influenced the entire project: the connection between software and physical accommodation.</p><p>A successful online reservation still needed to result in a guest arriving at the correct property, receiving authorized access and finding an apartment prepared for occupancy.</p><p>This made service design and operational reliability just as important as visual interface design.</p>"],["Brand Identity & Creative Direction","<h3>From a generic rental service to Maria Apartments</h3><p>The transformation began with a new brand identity.</p><p>I developed the name <strong>Maria Apartments</strong>, replacing the original generic positioning with an identifiable hospitality brand.</p><p>The new identity needed to communicate comfort, reliability and professional management without feeling excessively corporate or disconnected from the nature of the accommodation service.</p><p>I designed the logo, established the visual direction and created a consistent system of typography, color and supporting visual elements.</p><p>A warm, light visual approach was selected to make the brand feel welcoming while maintaining clarity across functional interfaces.</p><p>The identity was designed to work in several environments: property listings, booking forms, Instagram content, printed materials and physical guest touchpoints.</p><h3>Photography &amp; property presentation</h3><p>Accommodation is a visually driven service.</p><p>Guests rely heavily on photographs to assess the condition, atmosphere and suitability of a property.</p><p>I photographed apartment interiors, selected and retouched images, and prepared visual assets for consistent presentation across the website and social media.</p><p>The objective was not to make different properties appear identical.</p><p>It was to establish a consistent standard of visual quality while preserving the individual characteristics of each apartment.</p><h3>Physical brand materials</h3><p>I also designed branded business cards and keychains, prepared them for production and coordinated ordering.</p><p>These materials extended the new identity beyond screens.</p><p>The keychains were particularly relevant because physical access was an essential part of the accommodation experience.</p><p>Connecting digital presentation with physical objects helped establish continuity throughout the guest journey.</p>"],["Instagram Rebranding & Digital Presence","<p>Instagram was already an important discovery and communication channel for the business.</p><p>However, the existing account lacked the coherence of a unified brand.</p><p>I redesigned the profile presentation, developed a consistent content direction and established a visual system aligned with the new Maria Apartments identity.</p><p>The work included updated profile information, visual content, post design, publication and organized navigation through Instagram Highlights.</p><p>I also connected the social media presence to the official website.</p><p>This reflected a deliberate strategic decision.</p><p>Instagram would remain a channel for discovery, brand communication and customer engagement, while the website would become the primary environment for property exploration, reservations and payments.</p><p>The objective was to reduce dependence on manual social media conversations as the main booking infrastructure.</p>"],["Website UX/UI Design","<h3>A website designed around the complete booking journey</h3><p>The official Maria Apartments website was developed as a responsive, bilingual platform supporting all 19 apartments.</p><p>Its primary purpose was to transform accommodation discovery into a structured and actionable experience.</p><p>The information architecture was organized around the guest's practical decision-making process:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover → Compare → Evaluate → Check Availability → Book → Pay → Receive Confirmation*</em></p><p>Each stage needed to provide enough information for the user to proceed confidently.</p><h3>Interactive building facade</h3><p>One of the distinctive visual features of the website is its interactive building facade.</p><p>Instead of functioning as a purely decorative homepage illustration, the building provides an alternative way to explore available accommodation.</p><p>The interactive windows connect the visual presentation to apartment discovery.</p><p>This approach gave the business a memorable digital identity while making the interface itself part of the experience.</p><p>However, visual originality was not allowed to replace usability.</p><p>The interactive building was supported by more conventional browsing tools, ensuring that customers could still explore properties through familiar catalog and filtering patterns.</p><p>The design decision balanced brand differentiation with practical navigation.</p><h3>Apartment catalog &amp; filtering</h3><p>The website includes a structured catalog of 19 apartments, allowing guests to explore different properties within a consistent interface.</p><p>Filtering supports practical accommodation decisions, including available dates, guest capacity, location and pricing considerations.</p><p>Because properties are distributed across different areas of Ternopil, location-based navigation was an important requirement.</p><p>The catalog was designed to help visitors narrow their options before committing to a detailed property review.</p><h3>Individual apartment pages</h3><p>Each apartment needed enough information to support an informed booking decision.</p><p>I organized property pages around consistent content categories, including:</p><ul><li>Apartment photography.</li><li>Descriptions and accommodation characteristics.</li><li>Guest capacity.</li><li>Relevant amenities.</li><li>Location information.</li><li>Pricing.</li><li>Availability.</li><li>Booking actions.</li></ul><p>Consistent structure was particularly important for a portfolio containing many different properties.</p><p>It allowed visitors to compare options without repeatedly learning a new page layout.</p><h3>Responsive design &amp; localization</h3><p>The website supports Ukrainian and English.</p><p>Both language versions were integrated into the customer-facing experience to make accommodation information accessible to local and international visitors.</p><p>The responsive design accounts for different screen sizes, including mobile browsing, where property discovery, date selection and booking actions require additional attention to hierarchy and touch interaction.</p>"],["Booking Experience & Payment Integration","<h3>Replacing a fragmented reservation process</h3><p>The original booking journey required multiple conversations, manual availability checks and separate payment arrangements.</p><p>I designed a connected booking experience that brought these actions into a structured digital flow.</p><p>The intended customer journey became:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Apartment Selection → Date Selection → Guest Details → Price Review → Online Payment → Booking Confirmation*</em></p><h3>Availability &amp; reservation validation</h3><p>Accommodation availability cannot be treated as a static piece of information.</p><p>A property may become unavailable between the moment a guest views the calendar and the moment they attempt to complete a reservation.</p><p>For this reason, availability is validated through server-side reservation logic.</p><p>The system includes safeguards against conflicting reservations and overlapping booking dates.</p><h3>Pricing logic</h3><p>The booking system was designed to account for accommodation-specific pricing conditions.</p><p>The checkout calculation can incorporate selected dates, length of stay, guest-related charges and security deposit requirements.</p><p>The aim was to present the relevant booking cost within the reservation process instead of relying on a separate conversation to establish the amount.</p><h3>Full online payment</h3><p>I integrated <strong>WayForPay</strong> to support online payment for the accommodation and security deposit.</p><p>Moving from a combination of bank transfers and cash to an integrated checkout experience was one of the project's most significant operational changes.</p><p>However, introducing digital payment also required careful handling of transaction states.</p><p>The implementation included signed payment requests, server-side verification, webhook processing, transaction status handling and reconciliation mechanisms.</p><p>A successful browser redirect alone was not treated as sufficient evidence of payment completion.</p><p>The system needed to verify the payment independently.</p><h3>Handling failure and uncertainty</h3><p>The reservation process also needed to account for situations where a payment was pending, failed, delayed or reported more than once.</p><p>These conditions informed the design of payment states and reservation safeguards.</p><p>The objective was to avoid both customer confusion and inconsistent booking records.</p><p>This work demonstrated that transactional UX involves more than a checkout interface.</p><p>It requires the visible customer journey and underlying system behavior to remain consistent under real-world conditions.</p>"],["Administrative Dashboard","<h3>Replacing Excel with an operational management system</h3><p>A major component of the project was the development of a dedicated administrative dashboard.</p><p>The original Excel-based workflow stored reservation information but did not provide a connected environment for coordinating the broader accommodation operation.</p><p>I designed and developed an interface organized around the tasks administrators actually needed to perform.</p><p>The dashboard consolidated information and actions that had previously been distributed across spreadsheets, messages and manual processes.</p><h3>Reservation management</h3><p>The system supports administrative reservation workflows, including:</p><ul><li>Reviewing existing reservations.</li><li>Creating bookings manually.</li><li>Modifying reservation details.</li><li>Extending stays.</li><li>Transferring reservations between apartments.</li><li>Managing reservation statuses.</li><li>Accessing guest and booking information.</li></ul><p>Manual booking creation was an important requirement because not every reservation originated through the public website.</p><p>The platform needed to accommodate existing business channels without forcing every customer through the same digital entry point.</p><h3>Visual reservation calendar</h3><p>I implemented a visual administrative calendar to provide a clearer view of apartment occupancy and upcoming bookings.</p><p>For a business managing 19 properties, seeing the relationships between apartments, dates and reservation statuses is more useful than examining isolated spreadsheet rows.</p><p>The calendar supports day-to-day planning and helps administrators understand the operational consequences of booking changes.</p><h3>Cleaning coordination</h3><p>Cleaning schedules are directly connected to check-outs and upcoming arrivals.</p><p>The administrative system introduced structured cleaning records and scheduling information based on reservation activity.</p><p>This helped connect apartment availability with the physical preparation required between guest stays.</p><p>Importantly, the software supports planning and coordination; the actual cleaning remains a human operational responsibility.</p><h3>Maintenance &amp; operational tasks</h3><p>The dashboard includes functionality for tracking maintenance issues and other property-related tasks.</p><p>This provides a more organized alternative to relying entirely on informal messages and separate records.</p><h3>Security deposits</h3><p>Deposit tracking was integrated into the administrative environment.</p><p>The system provides records and visibility around deposit-related responsibilities, while physical or financial actions that require administrator involvement remain controlled operational processes.</p><h3>Administrative UX principles</h3><p>The dashboard was designed around information clarity, operational context and task efficiency.</p><p>The goal was not to reproduce Excel inside a website.</p><p>It was to give administrators a purpose-built environment where reservation data could support meaningful actions.</p>"],["Business Analytics & Reporting","<p>Before the transformation, the business had limited centralized tools for examining operational performance.</p><p>Information relevant to occupancy, reservation value and individual properties existed, but evaluating it required manual calculation or reconciliation.</p><p>I introduced an analytics and reporting layer within the administrative platform.</p><p>The system organizes business information around reservation activity, occupancy, financial records and property-level performance.</p><p>This allows administrators to examine historical and upcoming activity through structured reporting rather than disconnected records.</p><p>The design also distinguishes operational information needed for immediate decisions from broader reporting used for planning.</p><p>The project established the infrastructure for better visibility into the business.</p><p>It did not, however, establish verified long-term improvements in revenue or occupancy attributable to the new platform.</p>"],["Telegram Bot & Guest Experience Automation","<h3>Designing the experience beyond checkout</h3><p>Completing a reservation is not the end of an accommodation journey.</p><p>Guests still need practical information about arrival, access, apartment rules and their stay.</p><p>Previously, much of this information was provided through direct communication with an administrator.</p><p>I developed a custom Telegram bot to support the guest experience after booking.</p><p>The bot was built with Python and aiogram and connected to the reservation ecosystem.</p><h3>Personalized booking access</h3><p>An early version of the concept involved reservation-specific MA codes.</p><p>During development, this approach evolved into personalized Telegram links.</p><p>Rather than remembering and manually entering a code, guests could open a reservation-linked Telegram experience.</p><p>The flow includes phone verification and account binding, allowing booking information to be associated with an authorized Telegram account.</p><p>This reduced friction in the intended guest journey while supporting controlled access to reservation details.</p><h3>Guest assistance</h3><p>The bot provides structured access to information relevant to the guest's stay.</p><p>This includes:</p><ul><li>Reservation information.</li><li>Pre-arrival guidance.</li><li>Check-in instructions.</li><li>Apartment-specific information.</li><li>FAQ navigation.</li><li>Help resources.</li><li>Administrator contact options.</li></ul><p>The interaction structure was designed around practical guest questions rather than an unnecessarily complex conversational interface.</p><h3>Contactless check-in</h3><p>A central feature was the connection between verified reservations and physical keybox access.</p><p>The system includes operational configuration for six physical keyboxes associated with the apartment portfolio.</p><p>Authorized guests can receive relevant access instructions according to their reservation and the applicable access conditions.</p><p>This required particular attention to the boundary between public information and restricted data.</p><p>An apartment description can be available to anyone.</p><p>Its physical access code cannot.</p><p>The bot therefore separates general guest assistance from information requiring current authorization.</p>"],["Security & Physical Access Control","<p>Because the platform connects digital reservations to physical accommodation, access security was an important design and engineering consideration.</p><p>Several safeguards were implemented across different stages of the guest journey.</p><h3>Reservation verification</h3><p>Restricted instructions are linked to verified booking information.</p><p>The bot checks relevant authorization conditions instead of relying solely on information recorded during a guest's first interaction.</p><p>This matters when a booking is cancelled, modified, completed or no longer authorized for access.</p><h3>Controlled keybox code activation</h3><p>One of the more important operational decisions concerned the relationship between digital records and physical keyboxes.</p><p>Updating a code in software does not automatically change the code installed on a physical lockbox.</p><p>The administrative workflow therefore separates entering a replacement code from confirming its physical activation.</p><p>A replacement code is not made available to guests until the administrator confirms that the physical keybox has been updated.</p><p>This prevents the system from distributing access information that may not yet work in the real world.</p><h3>Conflicting administrative changes</h3><p>The keybox management system also accounts for potential conflicts between administrative actions.</p><p>Version checks help prevent an outdated administrative view from overwriting more recent code information.</p><h3>Booking and payment integrity</h3><p>The reservation system incorporates server-side safeguards, including payment verification, duplicate-processing protection and database-level measures against overlapping bookings.</p><p>These safeguards are not primarily visual design features.</p><p>They are essential parts of making the customer-facing experience dependable.</p>"],["Technical Architecture & Development","<p>My work extended beyond product strategy and interface design into the implementation and integration of the underlying digital infrastructure.</p><p>The platform was developed as several connected components with distinct responsibilities.</p><h3>Public website</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Next.js · React · TypeScript*</em></p><p>The customer-facing web application supports apartment discovery, responsive interfaces, availability information and booking interactions.</p><h3>Database</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Neon PostgreSQL*</em></p><p>The database supports structured reservation data, apartment information and the relationships required by booking and administrative workflows.</p><h3>Payment system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*WayForPay*</em></p><p>The integration handles checkout requests, payment verification, status updates and transaction processing safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative platform</h3><p>The management interface connects reservation data with operational workflows, including property availability, cleaning schedules, guest records, financial tracking and reporting.</p><h3>Telegram automation</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Python · aiogram*</em></p><p>The separately developed bot provides authenticated guest assistance and controlled access to booking-related information.</p><h3>Hosting &amp; infrastructure</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Vercel · Render · SQLite · GitHub*</em></p><p>The website was deployed through Vercel.</p><p>The Telegram bot was migrated from Railway to Render, with persistent storage used for its operational state.</p><p>GitHub supported source control and the development workflow.</p><h3>My development responsibility</h3><p>I independently directed and implemented the connected system using modern development tools and AI-assisted workflows.</p><p>My responsibilities included translating business requirements into technical structures, designing interfaces, developing application functionality, coordinating integrations, evaluating system behavior, debugging and deployment.</p><p>The project required continuity between design decisions and engineering implementation.</p><p>A booking interaction could affect the database.</p><p>A payment confirmation could affect reservation status.</p><p>A change to a reservation could affect Telegram access permissions.</p><p>Understanding these dependencies was essential to delivering the project as a coherent product.</p>"],["Iteration & Problem-Solving","<p>The project was developed iteratively rather than through a completely fixed sequence of design and development phases.</p><p>Several important decisions changed as operational requirements became clearer.</p><h3>Iteration 01: MA codes to personalized links</h3><p><strong>Initial approach:</strong> Guests would access reservation information using manually entered booking codes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> This introduced an additional step and required guests to retain and enter a code.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Personalized Telegram links connected reservations to a verification and account-binding flow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Reduce unnecessary interaction while preserving controlled access to booking information.</p><h3>Iteration 02: Payment confirmation and reservation reliability</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Payment completion involved multiple systems and potentially asynchronous responses.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Browser redirects and payment notifications could not be treated as perfectly synchronized or independently authoritative.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Server-side verification, reconciliation and duplicate-processing safeguards became part of the transaction workflow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Protect reservation and financial records against delayed or repeated payment events.</p><h3>Iteration 03: Database-level booking protection</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Availability displayed in the interface could change while a customer was completing a reservation.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Interface validation alone could not reliably prevent conflicting bookings.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Additional server-side and database-level safeguards were incorporated.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Maintain reservation integrity even when multiple actions occur close together.</p><h3>Iteration 04: Physical keybox activation</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Digital code updates and physical lockbox changes happened through different processes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> A stored code could be distributed before the physical keybox was updated.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Separate code entry from administrator-confirmed physical activation.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Keep software permissions consistent with the real-world state of the property.</p><h3>Iteration 05: Infrastructure refinement</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> The Telegram service required reliable hosting and persistence of operational data.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> The bot was migrated from Railway to Render with attention to preserving stored information.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Support the continuity of a separately hosted service connected to the wider booking ecosystem.</p><p>These iterations illustrate an important aspect of the project: decisions were evaluated against operational requirements, not only against the appearance of individual interfaces.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The transformation changed how the business presented itself and how its core processes were organized.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Business Area</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>Generic apartment rental positioning</td><td>Maria Apartments brand identity</td></tr><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>Instagram and external listings</td><td>Branded social presence and official website</td></tr><tr><td>Apartment discovery</td><td>Direct inquiries and separate listings</td><td>Structured catalog of 19 apartments</td></tr><tr><td>Availability</td><td>Manual checks and spreadsheets</td><td>Integrated availability calendars</td></tr><tr><td>Booking</td><td>Administrator-coordinated process</td><td>Structured online reservation flow</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td>Bank transfer and cash</td><td>Integrated WayForPay checkout</td></tr><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Excel-based management</td><td>Dedicated management dashboard</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning</td><td>Manual scheduling</td><td>Reservation-connected cleaning records</td></tr><tr><td>Guest communication</td><td>Direct messaging and calls</td><td>Personalized Telegram assistance</td></tr><tr><td>Check-in</td><td>Physical coordination and key handover</td><td>Controlled contactless access workflow</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Manual calculations</td><td>Centralized operational analytics</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The value of these changes lies in their connection.</p><p>A redesigned website alone would not have resolved spreadsheet-based administration.</p><p>A booking form alone would not have solved payment verification.</p><p>A Telegram bot alone would not have provided reliable authorization.</p><p>The project required these elements to function as parts of one service.</p>"],["Results & Delivered Outcomes","<p>The project produced a complete set of interconnected creative, technical and operational deliverables.</p><h3>Brand transformation</h3><p>A new name, logo, visual identity, photography direction, social media presentation and printed brand materials established a consistent identity for the accommodation business.</p><h3>Customer-facing platform</h3><p>The business gained an official bilingual website presenting all 19 apartments through structured listings, individual property information, availability and booking functionality.</p><h3>Digital reservations &amp; payment</h3><p>A connected booking experience introduced online checkout, pricing calculations, payment-status handling and server-side reservation safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative management</h3><p>A centralized dashboard introduced structured reservation management, visual calendars, guest records, cleaning coordination, deposit tracking, maintenance records and business reporting.</p><h3>Automated guest assistance</h3><p>The Telegram system provided personalized booking access, verification, structured information and controlled check-in instructions.</p><h3>Connected infrastructure</h3><p>The website, database, payment gateway, administrative system and Telegram bot were integrated into a shared digital operating environment.</p><h3>Validation &amp; measurement</h3><p>The project documentation includes automated tests and deployment checks supporting the technical implementation.</p><p>However, the available evidence does not establish a controlled long-term comparison of revenue, conversion rates, customer satisfaction or operational cost savings.</p><p>Some real-world scenarios, including comprehensive production payment validation and physical check-in success rates, require further documented evaluation.</p><p>For this reason, I distinguish between delivered functionality, expected operational benefits and independently measured business outcomes.</p><p>The most defensible result is the transformation of previously fragmented workflows into a connected digital system.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>Maria Apartments was a multidisciplinary project in which I worked across responsibilities commonly divided between several specialist roles.</p><p>My involvement began with understanding the business and continued through creative direction, interface design, technical development and deployment.</p><p>I was responsible for translating operational problems into product requirements, determining the experience across customer and administrative interfaces, implementing connected functionality and refining decisions as technical and business constraints emerged.</p><p>The project combined several professional disciplines:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Product Strategy*</em></p><p>Understanding the rental operation, defining requirements and organizing the relationship between digital tools and business processes.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI &amp; Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Designing discovery, booking, payment, administration and guest assistance experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Identity &amp; Creative Direction*</em></p><p>Establishing a recognizable identity and applying it consistently across digital and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Photography &amp; Content Production*</em></p><p>Creating and preparing property photography, branded visuals and social media materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Web &amp; Application Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive interfaces, server-side functionality, database connections and administrative features.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Integrations &amp; Automation*</em></p><p>Connecting payment processing, reservation records, Telegram assistance and physical access workflows.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Deployment*</em></p><p>Validating functionality, addressing integration challenges and managing production-oriented technical delivery.</p><p>This breadth of responsibility required an understanding of the entire service rather than a narrow focus on one design deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>Maria Apartments fundamentally expanded the scope of what began as a branding and digital presence project.</p><p>The most important challenge was not creating a visually attractive website.</p><p>It was developing a reliable relationship between customer interactions, administrative processes, financial transactions, stored data and physical accommodation.</p><p>Working directly with the business revealed how easily a small operational detail can affect the wider user experience.</p><p>A confusing payment state can create uncertainty for a guest.</p><p>An overlapping reservation can create a serious administrative problem.</p><p>An incorrectly activated keybox code can prevent someone from entering an apartment.</p><p>A disconnected cleaning schedule can affect whether accommodation is ready for the next arrival.</p><p>These are not isolated interface problems.</p><p>They are service problems that require coordinated product, design and engineering decisions.</p><p>The experience reinforced the importance of understanding a business before selecting technologies or designing screens.</p><p>It also demonstrated the value of maintaining responsibility across the complete development process.</p><p>Maria Apartments represents my ability to work independently across brand identity, product strategy, UX/UI design, software development, technical integrations and operational automation.</p><p>The final result is more than a website.</p><p>It is a connected digital foundation for an accommodation business, built around the practical needs of guests, administrators and the service itself.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*A complete hospitality experience, designed from the first impression to the apartment door.*</em></p>"]]},"de":{"name":"MARIA APARTMENTS","industry":"Hotellerie / Immobilienverwaltung","platform":"Website · Buchungsplattform · Geschäftsautomatisierung","location":"Ternopil, Ukraine","role":"UX/UI · Webdesign · Markenidentität · Operations","scope":"Website structure · Booking experience · Guest communication · Operations","strapline":"A digital product shaped by direct operational knowledge","links":[{"label":"MARIA APARTMENTS","href":"https://www.bymariaapartments.com"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>Maria Apartments is a short-term accommodation business operating a portfolio of 19 apartments across several locations in Ternopil, Ukraine.</p><p>The project began with an established rental business that had customers, properties and operational experience, but lacked a unified brand identity and an integrated digital infrastructure.</p><p>Reservations were managed through Excel spreadsheets. Guests relied on direct communication to check availability, confirm bookings and arrange their arrival. Payments involved bank transfers and cash, while key handovers required physical coordination. Instagram served as an important customer-facing channel, but there was no dedicated platform connecting apartment discovery, reservations, payments and internal operations.</p><p>My responsibility was to transform this fragmented experience into a coherent digital ecosystem.</p><p>I independently managed the project across creative direction, user experience, technical implementation and operational integration. The work included establishing a new brand, producing visual content, designing and developing a bilingual accommodation website, implementing a booking and payment system, creating an administrative management platform and developing a custom Telegram bot for guest assistance and contactless check-in.</p><p>The result was not simply a redesigned website.</p><p>It was a connected hospitality management system designed to support the relationship between guests, administrators, reservations, payments, physical properties and daily business operations.</p>"],["Understanding the Business","<h3>The original operating model</h3><p>Before the transformation, the business operated under the generic description &quot;Daily Apartment Rentals&quot; rather than a recognizable independent brand.</p><p>Individual apartments were presented primarily through existing listings and social media. Customers frequently needed to contact an administrator to obtain information that could otherwise have been available through a self-service platform.</p><p>The reservation process depended on several disconnected interactions.</p><p>A potential guest would inquire about an apartment, ask about available dates, receive pricing information and coordinate payment directly with the business.</p><p>A security deposit was typically transferred to a bank card, while the remaining accommodation cost was paid in cash upon arrival.</p><p>After booking, the administrator recorded reservation details in Excel, coordinated arrival arrangements, managed physical keys and later processed the deposit return.</p><p>Other activities, including cleaning schedules, property maintenance and financial tracking, required additional manual coordination.</p><p>These processes were functional individually, but their separation created unnecessary dependencies.</p><h3>The core business problems</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Fragmented information*</em></p><p>Reservation records, apartment availability, payments, guest communication and operational tasks were managed through separate channels.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Administrative dependency*</em></p><p>Routine guest questions and booking actions required direct staff involvement.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited self-service*</em></p><p>Customers could not complete the entire accommodation journey independently through an official website.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Payment friction*</em></p><p>Financial transactions were divided between different payment methods and stages of the stay.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Operational complexity*</em></p><p>Managing 19 properties required ongoing coordination of arrivals, departures, cleaning, maintenance and availability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Inconsistent brand presentation*</em></p><p>The absence of a unified identity made the business appear as a collection of rental listings rather than a recognizable accommodation provider.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Limited reporting*</em></p><p>Without integrated analytics, evaluating occupancy, reservation activity and property-level performance required manual work.</p><p>The challenge was to address these problems as parts of one system rather than introduce isolated solutions.</p>"],["Discovery & UX Research","<p>My research approach was strongly influenced by direct participation in the business's daily operations.</p><p>Rather than relying exclusively on a design brief, I gained firsthand insight into guest communication, booking coordination, arrival procedures and administrative responsibilities.</p><p>This allowed me to examine the complete service experience, including interactions that happened outside a conventional website.</p><p>I analyzed how guests obtained information, how availability was confirmed, how payments were coordinated and what administrators needed to prepare an apartment for the next arrival.</p><p>I also examined the relationships between reservations, cleaning schedules, security deposits and physical access.</p><p>This revealed an important distinction between the needs of different users.</p><h3>Guest needs</h3><p>Guests needed to:</p><ul><li>Find suitable accommodation without extensive messaging.</li><li>Compare apartments by location, price, capacity and amenities.</li><li>Understand availability and accommodation costs.</li><li>Complete a reservation with clear payment instructions.</li><li>Receive reliable confirmation.</li><li>Access practical arrival and accommodation information.</li><li>Obtain assistance without repeating their booking details.</li></ul><h3>Administrator needs</h3><p>Administrators needed to:</p><ul><li>View reservations across all 19 apartments.</li><li>Identify occupied and available dates.</li><li>Create and modify bookings.</li><li>Coordinate cleaning between departures and arrivals.</li><li>Track deposits and reservation payments.</li><li>Manage apartment information and operational issues.</li><li>Prepare guests for check-in.</li><li>Control physical keybox access.</li><li>Access structured business reporting.</li></ul><h3>Research conclusion</h3><p>The principal challenge was not a lack of individual digital tools.</p><p>It was the absence of a shared system connecting customer-facing experiences with operational data.</p><p>This conclusion shaped the architecture of the entire project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>Research methodology note: The discovery process was based on operational observation, firsthand experience and workflow analysis rather than a formal controlled user study.</em></p>"],["Product Strategy","<p>I defined the transformation around a connected set of digital and physical touchpoints.</p><p>The strategy had three major objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Establish a recognizable hospitality brand*</em></p><p>Create a consistent identity capable of representing all 19 apartments across digital channels and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Build an independent customer booking journey*</em></p><p>Allow guests to discover accommodation, check dates, understand pricing, book and pay without requiring continuous administrator assistance.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Centralize internal operations*</em></p><p>Provide administrators with a dedicated management environment connected to reservations, payment records, apartment availability, cleaning and guest communication.</p><p>A fourth consideration influenced the entire project: the connection between software and physical accommodation.</p><p>A successful online reservation still needed to result in a guest arriving at the correct property, receiving authorized access and finding an apartment prepared for occupancy.</p><p>This made service design and operational reliability just as important as visual interface design.</p>"],["Brand Identity & Creative Direction","<h3>From a generic rental service to Maria Apartments</h3><p>The transformation began with a new brand identity.</p><p>I developed the name <strong>Maria Apartments</strong>, replacing the original generic positioning with an identifiable hospitality brand.</p><p>The new identity needed to communicate comfort, reliability and professional management without feeling excessively corporate or disconnected from the nature of the accommodation service.</p><p>I designed the logo, established the visual direction and created a consistent system of typography, color and supporting visual elements.</p><p>A warm, light visual approach was selected to make the brand feel welcoming while maintaining clarity across functional interfaces.</p><p>The identity was designed to work in several environments: property listings, booking forms, Instagram content, printed materials and physical guest touchpoints.</p><h3>Photography &amp; property presentation</h3><p>Accommodation is a visually driven service.</p><p>Guests rely heavily on photographs to assess the condition, atmosphere and suitability of a property.</p><p>I photographed apartment interiors, selected and retouched images, and prepared visual assets for consistent presentation across the website and social media.</p><p>The objective was not to make different properties appear identical.</p><p>It was to establish a consistent standard of visual quality while preserving the individual characteristics of each apartment.</p><h3>Physical brand materials</h3><p>I also designed branded business cards and keychains, prepared them for production and coordinated ordering.</p><p>These materials extended the new identity beyond screens.</p><p>The keychains were particularly relevant because physical access was an essential part of the accommodation experience.</p><p>Connecting digital presentation with physical objects helped establish continuity throughout the guest journey.</p>"],["Instagram Rebranding & Digital Presence","<p>Instagram was already an important discovery and communication channel for the business.</p><p>However, the existing account lacked the coherence of a unified brand.</p><p>I redesigned the profile presentation, developed a consistent content direction and established a visual system aligned with the new Maria Apartments identity.</p><p>The work included updated profile information, visual content, post design, publication and organized navigation through Instagram Highlights.</p><p>I also connected the social media presence to the official website.</p><p>This reflected a deliberate strategic decision.</p><p>Instagram would remain a channel for discovery, brand communication and customer engagement, while the website would become the primary environment for property exploration, reservations and payments.</p><p>The objective was to reduce dependence on manual social media conversations as the main booking infrastructure.</p>"],["Website UX/UI Design","<h3>A website designed around the complete booking journey</h3><p>The official Maria Apartments website was developed as a responsive, bilingual platform supporting all 19 apartments.</p><p>Its primary purpose was to transform accommodation discovery into a structured and actionable experience.</p><p>The information architecture was organized around the guest's practical decision-making process:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover → Compare → Evaluate → Check Availability → Book → Pay → Receive Confirmation*</em></p><p>Each stage needed to provide enough information for the user to proceed confidently.</p><h3>Interactive building facade</h3><p>One of the distinctive visual features of the website is its interactive building facade.</p><p>Instead of functioning as a purely decorative homepage illustration, the building provides an alternative way to explore available accommodation.</p><p>The interactive windows connect the visual presentation to apartment discovery.</p><p>This approach gave the business a memorable digital identity while making the interface itself part of the experience.</p><p>However, visual originality was not allowed to replace usability.</p><p>The interactive building was supported by more conventional browsing tools, ensuring that customers could still explore properties through familiar catalog and filtering patterns.</p><p>The design decision balanced brand differentiation with practical navigation.</p><h3>Apartment catalog &amp; filtering</h3><p>The website includes a structured catalog of 19 apartments, allowing guests to explore different properties within a consistent interface.</p><p>Filtering supports practical accommodation decisions, including available dates, guest capacity, location and pricing considerations.</p><p>Because properties are distributed across different areas of Ternopil, location-based navigation was an important requirement.</p><p>The catalog was designed to help visitors narrow their options before committing to a detailed property review.</p><h3>Individual apartment pages</h3><p>Each apartment needed enough information to support an informed booking decision.</p><p>I organized property pages around consistent content categories, including:</p><ul><li>Apartment photography.</li><li>Descriptions and accommodation characteristics.</li><li>Guest capacity.</li><li>Relevant amenities.</li><li>Location information.</li><li>Pricing.</li><li>Availability.</li><li>Booking actions.</li></ul><p>Consistent structure was particularly important for a portfolio containing many different properties.</p><p>It allowed visitors to compare options without repeatedly learning a new page layout.</p><h3>Responsive design &amp; localization</h3><p>The website supports Ukrainian and English.</p><p>Both language versions were integrated into the customer-facing experience to make accommodation information accessible to local and international visitors.</p><p>The responsive design accounts for different screen sizes, including mobile browsing, where property discovery, date selection and booking actions require additional attention to hierarchy and touch interaction.</p>"],["Booking Experience & Payment Integration","<h3>Replacing a fragmented reservation process</h3><p>The original booking journey required multiple conversations, manual availability checks and separate payment arrangements.</p><p>I designed a connected booking experience that brought these actions into a structured digital flow.</p><p>The intended customer journey became:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Apartment Selection → Date Selection → Guest Details → Price Review → Online Payment → Booking Confirmation*</em></p><h3>Availability &amp; reservation validation</h3><p>Accommodation availability cannot be treated as a static piece of information.</p><p>A property may become unavailable between the moment a guest views the calendar and the moment they attempt to complete a reservation.</p><p>For this reason, availability is validated through server-side reservation logic.</p><p>The system includes safeguards against conflicting reservations and overlapping booking dates.</p><h3>Pricing logic</h3><p>The booking system was designed to account for accommodation-specific pricing conditions.</p><p>The checkout calculation can incorporate selected dates, length of stay, guest-related charges and security deposit requirements.</p><p>The aim was to present the relevant booking cost within the reservation process instead of relying on a separate conversation to establish the amount.</p><h3>Full online payment</h3><p>I integrated <strong>WayForPay</strong> to support online payment for the accommodation and security deposit.</p><p>Moving from a combination of bank transfers and cash to an integrated checkout experience was one of the project's most significant operational changes.</p><p>However, introducing digital payment also required careful handling of transaction states.</p><p>The implementation included signed payment requests, server-side verification, webhook processing, transaction status handling and reconciliation mechanisms.</p><p>A successful browser redirect alone was not treated as sufficient evidence of payment completion.</p><p>The system needed to verify the payment independently.</p><h3>Handling failure and uncertainty</h3><p>The reservation process also needed to account for situations where a payment was pending, failed, delayed or reported more than once.</p><p>These conditions informed the design of payment states and reservation safeguards.</p><p>The objective was to avoid both customer confusion and inconsistent booking records.</p><p>This work demonstrated that transactional UX involves more than a checkout interface.</p><p>It requires the visible customer journey and underlying system behavior to remain consistent under real-world conditions.</p>"],["Administrative Dashboard","<h3>Replacing Excel with an operational management system</h3><p>A major component of the project was the development of a dedicated administrative dashboard.</p><p>The original Excel-based workflow stored reservation information but did not provide a connected environment for coordinating the broader accommodation operation.</p><p>I designed and developed an interface organized around the tasks administrators actually needed to perform.</p><p>The dashboard consolidated information and actions that had previously been distributed across spreadsheets, messages and manual processes.</p><h3>Reservation management</h3><p>The system supports administrative reservation workflows, including:</p><ul><li>Reviewing existing reservations.</li><li>Creating bookings manually.</li><li>Modifying reservation details.</li><li>Extending stays.</li><li>Transferring reservations between apartments.</li><li>Managing reservation statuses.</li><li>Accessing guest and booking information.</li></ul><p>Manual booking creation was an important requirement because not every reservation originated through the public website.</p><p>The platform needed to accommodate existing business channels without forcing every customer through the same digital entry point.</p><h3>Visual reservation calendar</h3><p>I implemented a visual administrative calendar to provide a clearer view of apartment occupancy and upcoming bookings.</p><p>For a business managing 19 properties, seeing the relationships between apartments, dates and reservation statuses is more useful than examining isolated spreadsheet rows.</p><p>The calendar supports day-to-day planning and helps administrators understand the operational consequences of booking changes.</p><h3>Cleaning coordination</h3><p>Cleaning schedules are directly connected to check-outs and upcoming arrivals.</p><p>The administrative system introduced structured cleaning records and scheduling information based on reservation activity.</p><p>This helped connect apartment availability with the physical preparation required between guest stays.</p><p>Importantly, the software supports planning and coordination; the actual cleaning remains a human operational responsibility.</p><h3>Maintenance &amp; operational tasks</h3><p>The dashboard includes functionality for tracking maintenance issues and other property-related tasks.</p><p>This provides a more organized alternative to relying entirely on informal messages and separate records.</p><h3>Security deposits</h3><p>Deposit tracking was integrated into the administrative environment.</p><p>The system provides records and visibility around deposit-related responsibilities, while physical or financial actions that require administrator involvement remain controlled operational processes.</p><h3>Administrative UX principles</h3><p>The dashboard was designed around information clarity, operational context and task efficiency.</p><p>The goal was not to reproduce Excel inside a website.</p><p>It was to give administrators a purpose-built environment where reservation data could support meaningful actions.</p>"],["Business Analytics & Reporting","<p>Before the transformation, the business had limited centralized tools for examining operational performance.</p><p>Information relevant to occupancy, reservation value and individual properties existed, but evaluating it required manual calculation or reconciliation.</p><p>I introduced an analytics and reporting layer within the administrative platform.</p><p>The system organizes business information around reservation activity, occupancy, financial records and property-level performance.</p><p>This allows administrators to examine historical and upcoming activity through structured reporting rather than disconnected records.</p><p>The design also distinguishes operational information needed for immediate decisions from broader reporting used for planning.</p><p>The project established the infrastructure for better visibility into the business.</p><p>It did not, however, establish verified long-term improvements in revenue or occupancy attributable to the new platform.</p>"],["Telegram Bot & Guest Experience Automation","<h3>Designing the experience beyond checkout</h3><p>Completing a reservation is not the end of an accommodation journey.</p><p>Guests still need practical information about arrival, access, apartment rules and their stay.</p><p>Previously, much of this information was provided through direct communication with an administrator.</p><p>I developed a custom Telegram bot to support the guest experience after booking.</p><p>The bot was built with Python and aiogram and connected to the reservation ecosystem.</p><h3>Personalized booking access</h3><p>An early version of the concept involved reservation-specific MA codes.</p><p>During development, this approach evolved into personalized Telegram links.</p><p>Rather than remembering and manually entering a code, guests could open a reservation-linked Telegram experience.</p><p>The flow includes phone verification and account binding, allowing booking information to be associated with an authorized Telegram account.</p><p>This reduced friction in the intended guest journey while supporting controlled access to reservation details.</p><h3>Guest assistance</h3><p>The bot provides structured access to information relevant to the guest's stay.</p><p>This includes:</p><ul><li>Reservation information.</li><li>Pre-arrival guidance.</li><li>Check-in instructions.</li><li>Apartment-specific information.</li><li>FAQ navigation.</li><li>Help resources.</li><li>Administrator contact options.</li></ul><p>The interaction structure was designed around practical guest questions rather than an unnecessarily complex conversational interface.</p><h3>Contactless check-in</h3><p>A central feature was the connection between verified reservations and physical keybox access.</p><p>The system includes operational configuration for six physical keyboxes associated with the apartment portfolio.</p><p>Authorized guests can receive relevant access instructions according to their reservation and the applicable access conditions.</p><p>This required particular attention to the boundary between public information and restricted data.</p><p>An apartment description can be available to anyone.</p><p>Its physical access code cannot.</p><p>The bot therefore separates general guest assistance from information requiring current authorization.</p>"],["Security & Physical Access Control","<p>Because the platform connects digital reservations to physical accommodation, access security was an important design and engineering consideration.</p><p>Several safeguards were implemented across different stages of the guest journey.</p><h3>Reservation verification</h3><p>Restricted instructions are linked to verified booking information.</p><p>The bot checks relevant authorization conditions instead of relying solely on information recorded during a guest's first interaction.</p><p>This matters when a booking is cancelled, modified, completed or no longer authorized for access.</p><h3>Controlled keybox code activation</h3><p>One of the more important operational decisions concerned the relationship between digital records and physical keyboxes.</p><p>Updating a code in software does not automatically change the code installed on a physical lockbox.</p><p>The administrative workflow therefore separates entering a replacement code from confirming its physical activation.</p><p>A replacement code is not made available to guests until the administrator confirms that the physical keybox has been updated.</p><p>This prevents the system from distributing access information that may not yet work in the real world.</p><h3>Conflicting administrative changes</h3><p>The keybox management system also accounts for potential conflicts between administrative actions.</p><p>Version checks help prevent an outdated administrative view from overwriting more recent code information.</p><h3>Booking and payment integrity</h3><p>The reservation system incorporates server-side safeguards, including payment verification, duplicate-processing protection and database-level measures against overlapping bookings.</p><p>These safeguards are not primarily visual design features.</p><p>They are essential parts of making the customer-facing experience dependable.</p>"],["Technical Architecture & Development","<p>My work extended beyond product strategy and interface design into the implementation and integration of the underlying digital infrastructure.</p><p>The platform was developed as several connected components with distinct responsibilities.</p><h3>Public website</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Next.js · React · TypeScript*</em></p><p>The customer-facing web application supports apartment discovery, responsive interfaces, availability information and booking interactions.</p><h3>Database</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Neon PostgreSQL*</em></p><p>The database supports structured reservation data, apartment information and the relationships required by booking and administrative workflows.</p><h3>Payment system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*WayForPay*</em></p><p>The integration handles checkout requests, payment verification, status updates and transaction processing safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative platform</h3><p>The management interface connects reservation data with operational workflows, including property availability, cleaning schedules, guest records, financial tracking and reporting.</p><h3>Telegram automation</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Python · aiogram*</em></p><p>The separately developed bot provides authenticated guest assistance and controlled access to booking-related information.</p><h3>Hosting &amp; infrastructure</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Vercel · Render · SQLite · GitHub*</em></p><p>The website was deployed through Vercel.</p><p>The Telegram bot was migrated from Railway to Render, with persistent storage used for its operational state.</p><p>GitHub supported source control and the development workflow.</p><h3>My development responsibility</h3><p>I independently directed and implemented the connected system using modern development tools and AI-assisted workflows.</p><p>My responsibilities included translating business requirements into technical structures, designing interfaces, developing application functionality, coordinating integrations, evaluating system behavior, debugging and deployment.</p><p>The project required continuity between design decisions and engineering implementation.</p><p>A booking interaction could affect the database.</p><p>A payment confirmation could affect reservation status.</p><p>A change to a reservation could affect Telegram access permissions.</p><p>Understanding these dependencies was essential to delivering the project as a coherent product.</p>"],["Iteration & Problem-Solving","<p>The project was developed iteratively rather than through a completely fixed sequence of design and development phases.</p><p>Several important decisions changed as operational requirements became clearer.</p><h3>Iteration 01: MA codes to personalized links</h3><p><strong>Initial approach:</strong> Guests would access reservation information using manually entered booking codes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> This introduced an additional step and required guests to retain and enter a code.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Personalized Telegram links connected reservations to a verification and account-binding flow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Reduce unnecessary interaction while preserving controlled access to booking information.</p><h3>Iteration 02: Payment confirmation and reservation reliability</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Payment completion involved multiple systems and potentially asynchronous responses.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Browser redirects and payment notifications could not be treated as perfectly synchronized or independently authoritative.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Server-side verification, reconciliation and duplicate-processing safeguards became part of the transaction workflow.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Protect reservation and financial records against delayed or repeated payment events.</p><h3>Iteration 03: Database-level booking protection</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Availability displayed in the interface could change while a customer was completing a reservation.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Interface validation alone could not reliably prevent conflicting bookings.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Additional server-side and database-level safeguards were incorporated.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Maintain reservation integrity even when multiple actions occur close together.</p><h3>Iteration 04: Physical keybox activation</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> Digital code updates and physical lockbox changes happened through different processes.</p><p><strong>Problem:</strong> A stored code could be distributed before the physical keybox was updated.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> Separate code entry from administrator-confirmed physical activation.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Keep software permissions consistent with the real-world state of the property.</p><h3>Iteration 05: Infrastructure refinement</h3><p><strong>Initial challenge:</strong> The Telegram service required reliable hosting and persistence of operational data.</p><p><strong>Revised approach:</strong> The bot was migrated from Railway to Render with attention to preserving stored information.</p><p><strong>Rationale:</strong> Support the continuity of a separately hosted service connected to the wider booking ecosystem.</p><p>These iterations illustrate an important aspect of the project: decisions were evaluated against operational requirements, not only against the appearance of individual interfaces.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The transformation changed how the business presented itself and how its core processes were organized.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Business Area</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>Generic apartment rental positioning</td><td>Maria Apartments brand identity</td></tr><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>Instagram and external listings</td><td>Branded social presence and official website</td></tr><tr><td>Apartment discovery</td><td>Direct inquiries and separate listings</td><td>Structured catalog of 19 apartments</td></tr><tr><td>Availability</td><td>Manual checks and spreadsheets</td><td>Integrated availability calendars</td></tr><tr><td>Booking</td><td>Administrator-coordinated process</td><td>Structured online reservation flow</td></tr><tr><td>Payments</td><td>Bank transfer and cash</td><td>Integrated WayForPay checkout</td></tr><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Excel-based management</td><td>Dedicated management dashboard</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning</td><td>Manual scheduling</td><td>Reservation-connected cleaning records</td></tr><tr><td>Guest communication</td><td>Direct messaging and calls</td><td>Personalized Telegram assistance</td></tr><tr><td>Check-in</td><td>Physical coordination and key handover</td><td>Controlled contactless access workflow</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Manual calculations</td><td>Centralized operational analytics</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The value of these changes lies in their connection.</p><p>A redesigned website alone would not have resolved spreadsheet-based administration.</p><p>A booking form alone would not have solved payment verification.</p><p>A Telegram bot alone would not have provided reliable authorization.</p><p>The project required these elements to function as parts of one service.</p>"],["Results & Delivered Outcomes","<p>The project produced a complete set of interconnected creative, technical and operational deliverables.</p><h3>Brand transformation</h3><p>A new name, logo, visual identity, photography direction, social media presentation and printed brand materials established a consistent identity for the accommodation business.</p><h3>Customer-facing platform</h3><p>The business gained an official bilingual website presenting all 19 apartments through structured listings, individual property information, availability and booking functionality.</p><h3>Digital reservations &amp; payment</h3><p>A connected booking experience introduced online checkout, pricing calculations, payment-status handling and server-side reservation safeguards.</p><h3>Administrative management</h3><p>A centralized dashboard introduced structured reservation management, visual calendars, guest records, cleaning coordination, deposit tracking, maintenance records and business reporting.</p><h3>Automated guest assistance</h3><p>The Telegram system provided personalized booking access, verification, structured information and controlled check-in instructions.</p><h3>Connected infrastructure</h3><p>The website, database, payment gateway, administrative system and Telegram bot were integrated into a shared digital operating environment.</p><h3>Validation &amp; measurement</h3><p>The project documentation includes automated tests and deployment checks supporting the technical implementation.</p><p>However, the available evidence does not establish a controlled long-term comparison of revenue, conversion rates, customer satisfaction or operational cost savings.</p><p>Some real-world scenarios, including comprehensive production payment validation and physical check-in success rates, require further documented evaluation.</p><p>For this reason, I distinguish between delivered functionality, expected operational benefits and independently measured business outcomes.</p><p>The most defensible result is the transformation of previously fragmented workflows into a connected digital system.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>Maria Apartments was a multidisciplinary project in which I worked across responsibilities commonly divided between several specialist roles.</p><p>My involvement began with understanding the business and continued through creative direction, interface design, technical development and deployment.</p><p>I was responsible for translating operational problems into product requirements, determining the experience across customer and administrative interfaces, implementing connected functionality and refining decisions as technical and business constraints emerged.</p><p>The project combined several professional disciplines:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Product Strategy*</em></p><p>Understanding the rental operation, defining requirements and organizing the relationship between digital tools and business processes.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI &amp; Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Designing discovery, booking, payment, administration and guest assistance experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Identity &amp; Creative Direction*</em></p><p>Establishing a recognizable identity and applying it consistently across digital and physical materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Photography &amp; Content Production*</em></p><p>Creating and preparing property photography, branded visuals and social media materials.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Web &amp; Application Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive interfaces, server-side functionality, database connections and administrative features.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Integrations &amp; Automation*</em></p><p>Connecting payment processing, reservation records, Telegram assistance and physical access workflows.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Deployment*</em></p><p>Validating functionality, addressing integration challenges and managing production-oriented technical delivery.</p><p>This breadth of responsibility required an understanding of the entire service rather than a narrow focus on one design deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>Maria Apartments fundamentally expanded the scope of what began as a branding and digital presence project.</p><p>The most important challenge was not creating a visually attractive website.</p><p>It was developing a reliable relationship between customer interactions, administrative processes, financial transactions, stored data and physical accommodation.</p><p>Working directly with the business revealed how easily a small operational detail can affect the wider user experience.</p><p>A confusing payment state can create uncertainty for a guest.</p><p>An overlapping reservation can create a serious administrative problem.</p><p>An incorrectly activated keybox code can prevent someone from entering an apartment.</p><p>A disconnected cleaning schedule can affect whether accommodation is ready for the next arrival.</p><p>These are not isolated interface problems.</p><p>They are service problems that require coordinated product, design and engineering decisions.</p><p>The experience reinforced the importance of understanding a business before selecting technologies or designing screens.</p><p>It also demonstrated the value of maintaining responsibility across the complete development process.</p><p>Maria Apartments represents my ability to work independently across brand identity, product strategy, UX/UI design, software development, technical integrations and operational automation.</p><p>The final result is more than a website.</p><p>It is a connected digital foundation for an accommodation business, built around the practical needs of guests, administrators and the service itself.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*A complete hospitality experience, designed from the first impression to the apartment door.*</em></p>"]]}},"arced":{"uk":{"name":"ARCED CONSTRUCTION GROUP","industry":"Будівництво / облаштування дому","platform":"Два комерційні сайти · цифрова присутність","location":"Вінніпег, Канада","role":"UX/UI · вебдизайн · розробка · айдентика","scope":"Information architecture · Responsive UX/UI · Service presentation · Implementation","strapline":"A connected digital presence for two related businesses","links":[{"label":"ARCED Construction","href":"https://arcedconstruction.ca"},{"label":"ARCED Tile","href":"https://arcedtile.ca"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>ARCED Construction Group is a Canadian construction business operating in Winnipeg, Manitoba, with services covering concrete work and professional tile installation.</p><p>The project involved designing and developing two distinct commercial websites representing separate service directions under one business identity.</p><p>Although concrete construction and tile installation belong to the broader construction industry, they involve different customer expectations, search behavior, project requirements and decision-making processes.</p><p>A homeowner looking for concrete driveway installation does not necessarily need the same information as someone planning a bathroom renovation or kitchen backsplash.</p><p>Attempting to present both services through one undifferentiated website experience would make the business harder to navigate and reduce the clarity of its individual service offerings.</p><p>I approached ARCED as a connected digital presence rather than a single website project.</p><p>The objective was to create two independent, service-focused online experiences that could maintain a recognizable shared identity while communicating different specializations.</p><p>I independently managed the work from requirements gathering and website architecture through UX/UI design, responsive development and production delivery.</p><p>The project included developing service-oriented content structures, preparing existing brand assets for digital use, establishing conversion pathways, designing responsive interfaces, integrating customer inquiry functionality and organizing content around local search intent.</p><p>The result was a pair of commercial websites designed to help potential customers understand ARCED's services, evaluate the company and take the next step toward requesting an estimate.</p>"],["Business Context & Initial Challenge","<p>Construction websites serve a different purpose from purely editorial or experimental digital experiences.</p><p>Potential customers rarely visit a contractor's website simply to explore its visual identity.</p><p>They usually arrive with a practical problem.</p><p>They need a driveway installed, a concrete surface replaced, a bathroom tiled or a renovation completed.</p><p>Their primary concerns often include the contractor's capabilities, service area, reliability, project requirements, pricing expectations and how to initiate contact.</p><p>For ARCED, the challenge was to translate specialist construction services into an accessible digital experience.</p><p>The business needed to communicate two distinct directions:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete Construction*</em></p><p>Work involving concrete installations, foundations, driveways, patios, sidewalks, garage floors and related concrete projects.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Work involving bathroom and kitchen tiling, floors, walls, showers, backsplashes, waterproofing, tile replacement and finishing details.</p><p>The digital strategy needed to distinguish these service categories without fragmenting the business identity.</p><h3>Core project challenges</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service differentiation*</em></p><p>Present concrete and tile services clearly enough that visitors can immediately understand which website is relevant to their needs.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Credibility*</em></p><p>Establish a professional online presence appropriate for a local construction business.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information clarity*</em></p><p>Explain technical services without requiring visitors to understand specialist terminology.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Lead generation*</em></p><p>Create straightforward pathways from service discovery to an estimate request.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Organize content for customers searching for construction services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand consistency*</em></p><p>Maintain a relationship between the two websites while allowing each to develop its own service-specific structure.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Responsive usability*</em></p><p>Support visitors browsing from mobile devices, tablets and desktop computers.</p><p>The project required a balance between business communication, visual design, user experience and technical implementation.</p>"],["Discovery & Requirements Analysis","<p>I began by understanding the company's service structure, target customers and practical requirements.</p><p>The discovery process included direct communication about the business's operations, the services it offered, its intended audience, its geographical market and the information customers needed before requesting work.</p><p>I also reviewed the Canadian construction market and relevant competitor approaches to organizing services, presenting expertise and encouraging inquiries.</p><p>This work informed both the information architecture and the content hierarchy.</p><h3>Business requirements</h3><p>The websites needed to communicate:</p><ul><li>Which construction services ARCED provides.</li><li>The difference between concrete and tile specializations.</li><li>Where the company operates.</li><li>What customers can expect during a project.</li><li>Which factors affect pricing.</li><li>How to request an estimate.</li><li>How to contact the business.</li><li>What information can help the company assess a potential job.</li></ul><p>The business serves residential and commercial customer needs, which also influenced the presentation of different service types.</p><h3>Customer requirements</h3><p>From the visitor's perspective, the experience needed to answer several immediate questions.</p><p>What does this company do?</p><p>Does it provide the service I need?</p><p>Does it operate in my area?</p><p>Can I understand the process before contacting anyone?</p><p>What affects the price?</p><p>How can I request an estimate?</p><p>These questions became the foundation of the UX strategy.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>The project involved requirements gathering and competitive analysis. It did not include a documented formal usability study or controlled user interviews.</em></p>"],["UX Strategy","<h3>Designing around customer intent</h3><p>The most important UX decision was to organize the websites around what potential customers were trying to accomplish.</p><p>Instead of placing the brand story ahead of everything else, the architecture prioritizes service clarity.</p><p>A visitor should be able to recognize the relevant service category quickly, understand its practical applications and locate a clear inquiry action.</p><p>The strategy was based on four principles.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Immediate Relevance*</em></p><p>Communicate the service specialization and location early in the page.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Clear Evaluation*</em></p><p>Give visitors enough information to assess whether ARCED is suitable for their project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Reduced Contact Friction*</em></p><p>Make estimate requests and contact information accessible without unnecessary navigation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*04. Consistent Business Identity*</em></p><p>Maintain visual and structural relationships between the two sites without making them interchangeable.</p><p>This approach ensured that the design remained commercially purposeful rather than relying exclusively on visual presentation.</p>"],["Why Two Separate Websites?","<p>The decision to develop two individual websites was central to the project.</p><p>Although both service directions belong to ARCED, their audiences often begin with different problems.</p><p>Someone considering a concrete driveway may be evaluating the size of an outdoor area, surface preparation, site conditions and the nature of the installation.</p><p>Someone considering tile work may be comparing finishes, waterproofing requirements, room types, surface preparation and installation details.</p><p>These differences affect what information should appear first, how the services should be grouped and which project details deserve emphasis.</p><h3>One brand, two customer journeys</h3><p>I established a shared approach to visual identity, typography, interface consistency and contact presentation.</p><p>At the same time, I developed separate service architectures tailored to the two specializations.</p><p>This allowed:</p><ul><li>More specific service descriptions.</li><li>Clearer customer journeys.</li><li>Independent local search positioning.</li><li>More relevant project presentation.</li><li>Distinct information hierarchies.</li><li>Service-specific inquiry pathways.</li></ul><p>The objective was not to create two unrelated brands.</p><p>It was to give each specialization enough independence to communicate effectively while maintaining the connection to ARCED Construction Group.</p>"],["ARCED Construction — Concrete Services Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedconstruction.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>The construction website was designed to present ARCED's concrete services to residential and commercial customers in the Winnipeg market.</p><p>Its primary responsibility was to communicate the company's capabilities and provide a straightforward path toward a project inquiry.</p><p>Concrete construction involves practical decisions that may depend on location, surface type, dimensions, preparation requirements and the intended use of the finished structure.</p><p>The information architecture therefore needed to focus on recognizable service categories rather than abstract descriptions of construction expertise.</p><h3>Service-oriented architecture</h3><p>I structured the content around concrete-related work, with service categories covering areas such as:</p><ul><li>Concrete driveways.</li><li>Foundations.</li><li>Patios.</li><li>Sidewalks and walkways.</li><li>Garage floors.</li><li>Concrete steps.</li><li>Concrete slabs.</li><li>Concrete replacement and preparation work.</li></ul><p>These categories helped translate a broad construction specialization into specific services customers could recognize.</p><h3>User experience considerations</h3><p>The design needed to support visitors who might have limited technical knowledge.</p><p>Rather than requiring users to understand construction methods before making an inquiry, the website presents the company's work through customer-relevant applications.</p><p>The experience emphasizes:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service clarity*</em></p><p>Visitors should be able to identify whether the company handles their type of project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project context*</em></p><p>Construction imagery and project-oriented content help communicate the nature of the work.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Process transparency*</em></p><p>Explaining the steps involved in arranging construction work can reduce uncertainty before an inquiry.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Contact accessibility*</em></p><p>Estimate requests and contact actions need to remain easy to locate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Content should connect the services to the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Conversion strategy</h3><p>The website is structured to guide visitors from understanding a service toward initiating contact.</p><p>The primary journey is:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover a Service → Understand the Scope → Evaluate the Company → Request an Estimate*</em></p><p>This approach turns service information into a practical path toward a business inquiry.</p><p>The objective was to create an accessible commercial website, not a complex customer portal that would introduce unnecessary steps.</p>"],["ARCED Tile — Specialist Installation Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedtile.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>ARCED Tile focuses on professional tile installation for residential and commercial interiors.</p><p>Its website needed to explain a more visually detailed service category while supporting users at different stages of renovation planning.</p><p>Tile installation customers may already know the room they want to renovate, but not the precise service terminology.</p><p>Someone looking to improve a bathroom may need information about floor tiles, shower walls, waterproofing or existing tile removal.</p><p>The information architecture needed to accommodate both general customer intentions and specific installation requirements.</p><h3>Service architecture</h3><p>I organized the interface around recognizable categories, including:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Bathroom Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting bathroom-related installation work and finishing considerations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Kitchen Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Covering kitchen surfaces and installations that need to accommodate cabinetry, counters and daily use.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Backsplashes*</em></p><p>Highlighting decorative and practical tile installation for kitchens and other interiors.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Floor Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Communicating flooring applications involving different tile types and surface conditions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Wall Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting installation work for walls and decorative surfaces.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Shower Tile &amp; Waterproofing*</em></p><p>Explaining the relationship between installation quality, preparation and water protection.</p><p>Additional content addresses tile removal, replacement, repairs and different material requirements.</p><h3>Information hierarchy</h3><p>The website guides visitors from broad service recognition toward greater detail.</p><p>The primary page structure includes:</p><ul><li>Clear service positioning.</li><li>Service categories.</li><li>Supporting explanations.</li><li>Reasons to consider the company.</li><li>Installation process.</li><li>Project presentation.</li><li>Pricing guidance.</li><li>Frequently asked questions.</li><li>Estimate request options.</li><li>Contact information.</li></ul><p>This sequence reflects the different stages of a potential customer's decision.</p><p>A visitor who already understands the service can proceed toward an estimate.</p><p>A visitor who needs more information can explore the process, project examples and practical considerations before contacting the company.</p>"],["Designing the Customer Journey","<p>Both websites were designed around a common commercial objective: helping relevant visitors become informed prospective customers.</p><p>However, I did not approach conversion as simply placing more buttons on a page.</p><p>A successful inquiry experience requires users to understand what they are requesting.</p><h3>Stage 01. Recognition</h3><p>The visitor identifies the relevant service and confirms that the business operates in the appropriate location.</p><h3>Stage 02. Exploration</h3><p>The visitor reviews service information and understands the broad scope of the work.</p><h3>Stage 03. Evaluation</h3><p>Supporting content helps clarify the installation process, project considerations and company presentation.</p><h3>Stage 04. Inquiry</h3><p>The user is given a clear opportunity to request an estimate or contact the company.</p><h3>Stage 05. Business Follow-Up</h3><p>The website supports the transition from digital inquiry to direct communication, where project-specific requirements can be assessed.</p><p>The interfaces were designed to support this progression without forcing every visitor through an identical sequence.</p><p>Clear navigation and repeated contextual contact opportunities make the experience more flexible.</p>"],["Lead Generation & Estimate Requests","<p>For a construction business, the website's commercial value is closely connected to the quality and clarity of customer inquiries.</p><p>The design therefore needed to support users who were ready to discuss a project as well as visitors who still required more information.</p><p>I incorporated enquiry-oriented structures and contact functionality into the websites.</p><h3>Clear calls to action</h3><p>Estimate requests are presented in relation to the services being explored.</p><p>This allows a visitor to act when they have enough context rather than requiring them to return to the top of the page or search for contact details.</p><h3>Practical information collection</h3><p>A useful construction inquiry generally benefits from information about the project type, location, dimensions, requirements and contact details.</p><p>The website's estimate-oriented forms are designed to support this kind of initial communication.</p><h3>Balancing information and friction</h3><p>A form that asks too little may produce an inquiry lacking the details required for a useful response.</p><p>A form that asks too much may discourage a potential customer from completing it.</p><p>The objective was to maintain an understandable entry point to the estimate process while leaving detailed project assessment to direct follow-up.</p><p>The websites do not attempt to replace the contractor's professional evaluation.</p><p>They create a structured starting point for that conversation.</p>"],["Pricing Guidance & Cost Estimation","<p>One of the recurring challenges in construction services is pricing uncertainty.</p><p>Customers may want to understand the approximate cost of a project before contacting a contractor.</p><p>However, installation prices depend on multiple variables, including materials, project size, preparation, complexity and site conditions.</p><p>ARCED Tile includes a cost-estimation entry point as part of its customer experience.</p><h3>UX objective</h3><p>The purpose of preliminary pricing guidance is to help visitors understand the factors that influence a quote.</p><p>It is not to present an automated estimate as a guaranteed final contract price.</p><h3>Design considerations</h3><p>The experience needs to communicate:</p><ul><li>The type of work being considered.</li><li>Relevant project dimensions or scope.</li><li>Material and installation considerations.</li><li>Preparation requirements.</li><li>Why final pricing may require further assessment.</li></ul><p>By making pricing factors more understandable, the website can help visitors approach a conversation with more realistic expectations.</p><p>This supports the broader goal of transparent communication while preserving the role of an accurate project-specific estimate.</p>"],["Trust, Credibility & Service Communication","<p>Construction customers frequently evaluate a business before contacting it.</p><p>Unlike a low-cost online purchase, a renovation or concrete installation may involve significant expense, disruption and reliance on a contractor's professional workmanship.</p><p>For this reason, credibility was an important part of the content strategy.</p><h3>Communicating capabilities</h3><p>Service explanations and project-oriented imagery give visitors a clearer understanding of the work the company offers.</p><h3>Explaining the process</h3><p>Process content helps make the transition from initial inquiry to completed installation more understandable.</p><h3>Addressing uncertainty</h3><p>Frequently asked questions provide space to clarify common concerns about materials, service areas, installation requirements and estimates.</p><h3>Presenting business commitments</h3><p>The Tile website communicates company-stated information about insurance, a two-year workmanship warranty and the availability of residential and commercial services.</p><p>These details are presented as business information rather than independent certifications or guarantees of project outcomes.</p><h3>Avoiding artificial social proof</h3><p>Credibility should come from verifiable business information, actual services, relevant project materials and clear communication.</p><p>The case study does not claim a measured increase in trust or rely on invented customer testimonials.</p><p>The design objective was to give prospective customers enough relevant information to assess the business for themselves.</p>"],["Local SEO & Content Architecture","<p>ARCED operates in a geographically defined service market.</p><p>Its websites therefore needed to communicate not only what the company does, but also where those services are available.</p><p>I incorporated local search considerations into the content planning and page architecture.</p><h3>Service-specific search intent</h3><p>Concrete services and tile installation generate different search intentions.</p><p>The content strategy distinguished terms associated with the two specializations rather than treating all construction-related searches as interchangeable.</p><p>Examples of relevant search categories include:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete*</em></p><p>Concrete contractor, concrete driveways, patios, foundations, sidewalks and related installation services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile*</em></p><p>Tile installation, bathroom tile, kitchen backsplash, floor tile, shower tile and related services in Winnipeg.</p><h3>Content organization</h3><p>The websites use clear service positioning and descriptive content to support visitors searching for particular types of work.</p><p>The planning included attention to:</p><ul><li>Service-specific headings.</li><li>Descriptive page titles.</li><li>Local market relevance.</li><li>Logical content hierarchy.</li><li>Internal navigation.</li><li>Relevant service explanations.</li><li>Contact and estimate entry points.</li><li>Responsive presentation.</li></ul><h3>UX and SEO alignment</h3><p>The objective was not to fill pages with repeated keywords.</p><p>It was to make important services understandable to both visitors and search engines.</p><p>A well-structured service page should answer a recognizable customer question while clearly communicating its subject.</p><p>This approach supports search-oriented content without sacrificing readability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*The project established an SEO-oriented website structure. It does not claim verified Google rankings, organic traffic growth or search performance improvements without supporting analytics.*</em></p>"],["Visual Identity & Art Direction","<p>The two websites needed a visual language appropriate for a professional construction business.</p><p>The design direction prioritized clarity, confidence and consistency.</p><p>The objective was to communicate a dependable commercial identity without making the interfaces visually generic.</p><h3>Shared brand foundation</h3><p>ARCED Construction and ARCED Tile were designed to remain recognizable as parts of one business.</p><p>This required consistent treatment of:</p><ul><li>Brand assets.</li><li>Typography.</li><li>Layout principles.</li><li>Navigation patterns.</li><li>Calls to action.</li><li>Contact information.</li><li>Interface components.</li></ul><h3>Brand asset preparation</h3><p>I prepared and vectorized existing brand materials for more consistent digital use.</p><p>This included working with the existing ARCED identity rather than claiming authorship of the original brand concept.</p><p>The goal was to ensure that the available assets could be used clearly and consistently across different screen sizes and interface contexts.</p><h3>Service-specific presentation</h3><p>While maintaining a shared identity, the two websites allow their subject matter to influence the visual presentation.</p><p>Concrete services benefit from clear construction-oriented content and practical project information.</p><p>Tile installation benefits from detailed visual examples, material presentation and attention to finishes.</p><p>The design system needed enough flexibility to accommodate both without losing consistency.</p>"],["Responsive UX/UI Design","<p>The websites were designed for desktop, tablet and mobile experiences.</p><p>Responsive design was an important requirement because construction customers may access a contractor's website in different contexts.</p><p>Someone might browse services from a computer while planning a renovation.</p><p>Another visitor might open the website on a phone while comparing contractors or preparing to request an estimate.</p><p>The interface needed to remain understandable across those situations.</p><h3>Responsive navigation</h3><p>Navigation structures were adapted to preserve access to important content on smaller screens.</p><h3>Service presentation</h3><p>Service descriptions and supporting imagery were organized to maintain clear hierarchy without requiring desktop-sized layouts.</p><h3>Contact accessibility</h3><p>Inquiry and contact actions needed to remain accessible on mobile devices.</p><h3>Content readability</h3><p>Typography, spacing and component proportions were designed to support scanning and comprehension.</p><h3>Interaction consistency</h3><p>Interactive elements needed to behave predictably across different devices.</p><p>The objective was to preserve the same essential customer journey rather than simply shrink the desktop interface.</p>"],["Content Design & UX Writing","<p>A technically accurate service description is not automatically a useful one.</p><p>Construction terminology can be unfamiliar to customers who are planning their first renovation or concrete project.</p><p>I therefore treated content structure as part of the user experience.</p><h3>Clear service naming</h3><p>Service categories were organized around recognizable customer needs.</p><h3>Practical explanations</h3><p>Supporting descriptions communicate what the work involves and where it may be relevant.</p><h3>Action-oriented language</h3><p>Calls to action were designed to make the next step understandable.</p><h3>Frequently asked questions</h3><p>FAQ content provides additional detail without requiring every visitor to read long explanations before identifying the service they need.</p><h3>Consistent tone</h3><p>The business presentation was developed to sound professional, direct and approachable.</p><p>This helped connect the brand's visual identity to its written communication.</p><p>Content design was an important part of the project because the websites needed to function as commercial communication tools, not merely visual portfolios.</p>"],["Front-End Development & Technical Delivery","<p>My involvement continued beyond visual design into the implementation of the production websites.</p><p>I independently managed the transition from the planned information architecture and interface direction to responsive, functioning web experiences.</p><h3>Development scope</h3><p>The work included:</p><ul><li>Implementing page structures.</li><li>Developing responsive layouts.</li><li>Creating reusable interface patterns.</li><li>Integrating visual assets.</li><li>Building interactive components.</li><li>Implementing navigation behavior.</li><li>Connecting contact and inquiry functionality.</li><li>Preparing customer-facing content.</li><li>Supporting basic technical SEO requirements.</li><li>Testing different viewport sizes.</li><li>Preparing and delivering the published websites.</li></ul><h3>Two independent digital properties</h3><p>The main technical and organizational consideration was maintaining separate websites with their own content and navigation while preserving a consistent business identity.</p><p>Each site needed to function independently for its relevant audience.</p><p>At the same time, both needed to feel connected through the ARCED brand.</p><h3>Independent project ownership</h3><p>I was responsible for coordinating the work across requirements, design decisions, implementation and final delivery.</p><p>This meant evaluating the websites as complete products rather than treating design and development as unrelated stages.</p><p>Decisions about navigation affected content organization.</p><p>Decisions about service categories affected search-oriented structure.</p><p>Decisions about estimate requests affected interface hierarchy.</p><p>Maintaining responsibility across those relationships helped preserve consistency between the original objectives and the implemented websites.</p>"],["Key Design Decisions","<p>Several decisions were particularly important to the project's direction.</p><h3>Decision 01. Separate the two specializations</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Concrete construction and tile installation involve different customer needs and search intentions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Develop two independent service-focused websites under a connected brand identity.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Give each specialization enough space to communicate clearly without creating unnecessary competition between unrelated service categories.</p><h3>Decision 02. Prioritize services over generic brand messaging</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors looking for a contractor usually want to confirm practical service relevance quickly.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Place clear service positioning and recognizable service categories prominently within the experience.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Help users establish whether the company offers the work they need before asking them to explore secondary content.</p><h3>Decision 03. Connect information with inquiry actions</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors may decide to contact the company at different points in their evaluation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Integrate estimate and contact opportunities into the relevant service journeys.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Make the transition from understanding a service to requesting information more direct.</p><h3>Decision 04. Use a consistent but flexible visual system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Two independent websites can easily feel unrelated or become visually repetitive.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Retain shared brand patterns while adapting content and presentation to each specialization.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Preserve business recognition without sacrificing the clarity of individual services.</p><h3>Decision 05. Make local relevance explicit</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction services are geographically constrained, and customers need to identify appropriate providers.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Structure content around relevant services and the Winnipeg market.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Connect customer search intent with clear service and location information.</p><h3>Decision 06. Explain pricing without promising false precision</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction costs depend on conditions that may not be fully known during an initial website visit.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Support preliminary pricing understanding while maintaining a clear route toward a project-specific estimate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Improve transparency without presenting an automated calculation as a binding professional quote.</p>"],["Project Challenges & Problem-Solving","<h3>Maintaining distinction without fragmentation</h3><p>The first challenge was making the concrete and tile websites feel independent enough to serve different audiences while retaining a shared business identity.</p><p>The solution was to separate their content architecture while maintaining common visual and interaction principles.</p><h3>Balancing technical information and accessibility</h3><p>Construction services require specific explanations, but overly technical content can create unnecessary friction.</p><p>The interface therefore needed to communicate practical service applications before expanding into specialized details.</p><h3>Designing for commercial intent</h3><p>The websites were not intended as purely artistic digital experiences.</p><p>Visual choices needed to support comprehension, credibility and clear inquiry pathways.</p><p>This required evaluating design decisions against the practical goals of the business.</p><h3>Supporting different devices</h3><p>Responsive implementation required adapting information density, navigation and interaction patterns without losing access to important content.</p><h3>Coordinating design and development</h3><p>Managing the websites independently meant ensuring that the implemented result reflected the intended UX, rather than treating development as a separate final step.</p><p>These challenges reinforced the importance of approaching commercial web projects as connected systems of content, design and functionality.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The project established a dedicated digital presence for two service directions within ARCED Construction Group.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Before the Project</th><th>Delivered Direction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>No established dedicated website</td><td>Two published commercial websites</td></tr><tr><td>Service positioning</td><td>Services needed clear online separation</td><td>Distinct concrete and tile experiences</td></tr><tr><td>Information architecture</td><td>No dedicated digital service structure</td><td>Organized, service-oriented navigation</td></tr><tr><td>Visual consistency</td><td>Existing brand materials requiring digital preparation</td><td>Coordinated visual presentation</td></tr><tr><td>Customer inquiry</td><td>Direct contact without a structured website journey</td><td>Dedicated inquiry and estimate pathways</td></tr><tr><td>Local discovery</td><td>No dedicated website architecture</td><td>Winnipeg-oriented service content</td></tr><tr><td>Device support</td><td>No existing responsive website experience</td><td>Responsive desktop, tablet and mobile interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Service information</td><td>No centralized online presentation</td><td>Structured explanations and supporting content</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This comparison describes the delivered digital capabilities rather than a measured increase in sales or customer acquisition.</p>"],["Results & Deliverables","<p>The project resulted in two independently published commercial websites.</p><h3>ARCED Construction</h3><p>A dedicated online experience presenting the concrete construction specialization through service-oriented information architecture, responsive design and customer inquiry pathways.</p><h3>ARCED Tile</h3><p>A specialist tile installation website combining structured service content, project presentation, process explanations, pricing guidance, frequently asked questions and estimate request functionality.</p><h3>Shared brand presentation</h3><p>The sites maintain a recognizable relationship through consistent brand assets, visual principles and interface patterns.</p><h3>Digital customer journeys</h3><p>Both websites provide a structured route from service discovery to contacting the business.</p><h3>Local content foundation</h3><p>The content architecture supports clear communication of services within the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Responsive implementation</h3><p>The interfaces were implemented for use across different screen sizes and interaction contexts.</p><h3>Production delivery</h3><p>Both websites were developed and published as accessible commercial properties.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project outcomes should be evaluated accurately.*</em></p><p>The confirmed deliverables are two websites, their content and interface systems, their inquiry pathways and their production implementation.</p><p>The available project information does not establish verified changes in conversion rates, lead volume, search rankings or revenue.</p><p>These would require post-launch analytics and a defined measurement period.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>ARCED demonstrates my ability to independently manage commercial website projects across strategy, design and implementation.</p><p>My work included understanding business requirements, researching the relevant market, defining the information architecture, establishing visual direction, preparing brand assets and developing the production websites.</p><p>Rather than working only on isolated interface screens, I was responsible for shaping the complete digital experience around the business's services and customer needs.</p><p>The project required competency in several connected areas.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Requirements Analysis*</em></p><p>Understanding the company's service structure, customer groups, geographic market and commercial objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Strategy*</em></p><p>Designing separate customer journeys for two distinct service specializations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Organizing service content, navigation and inquiry pathways.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI Design*</em></p><p>Developing responsive interfaces that balance visual quality with practical usability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Adaptation*</em></p><p>Preparing existing identity assets for a consistent digital presentation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Writing &amp; Content Strategy*</em></p><p>Presenting construction services through clear, customer-oriented explanations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local SEO Planning*</em></p><p>Structuring content around relevant service categories and geographic search intent.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Front-End Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive layouts, interactive components and functional web experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Delivery*</em></p><p>Preparing the websites for publication and maintaining responsibility for the final implementation.</p><p>The defining aspect of my contribution was continuity across these responsibilities.</p><p>I was not simply producing visual layouts for someone else to interpret.</p><p>I managed the design and development process as a complete commercial deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>ARCED was an important exercise in translating a traditional service business into a purposeful digital experience.</p><p>The project demonstrated that effective commercial web design begins with understanding how customers make decisions.</p><p>A construction website does not need to overwhelm visitors with visual effects.</p><p>It needs to communicate relevant services, establish a professional presentation, explain important details and make the next action clear.</p><p>The most significant strategic decision was treating concrete construction and tile installation as two different customer journeys rather than forcing them into one generic interface.</p><p>This required both consistency and restraint.</p><p>The business identity needed to remain recognizable, but the content architecture had to respect the differences between the two specializations.</p><p>The project also reinforced the importance of treating content, UX, visual design and technical implementation as connected disciplines.</p><p>The value of the websites came from how those elements worked together.</p><p>My individual responsibility extended from the initial business requirements to the published digital products.</p><p>ARCED represents my ability to independently design, develop and deliver commercial websites for an international client, adapting the experience to different services, customer intentions and business objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Two specialized websites, one recognizable business identity, and a digital experience built around the way real customers search for construction services.*</em></p>"]],"images":["assets/commercial-covers/arced-construction.png","assets/commercial-covers/arced-tile.png"]},"pl":{"name":"ARCED CONSTRUCTION GROUP","industry":"Budownictwo / wykończenie wnętrz","platform":"Dwie strony komercyjne · obecność cyfrowa","location":"Winnipeg, Kanada","role":"UX/UI · web design · development · identyfikacja marki","scope":"Information architecture · Responsive UX/UI · Service presentation · Implementation","strapline":"A connected digital presence for two related businesses","links":[{"label":"ARCED Construction","href":"https://arcedconstruction.ca"},{"label":"ARCED Tile","href":"https://arcedtile.ca"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>ARCED Construction Group is a Canadian construction business operating in Winnipeg, Manitoba, with services covering concrete work and professional tile installation.</p><p>The project involved designing and developing two distinct commercial websites representing separate service directions under one business identity.</p><p>Although concrete construction and tile installation belong to the broader construction industry, they involve different customer expectations, search behavior, project requirements and decision-making processes.</p><p>A homeowner looking for concrete driveway installation does not necessarily need the same information as someone planning a bathroom renovation or kitchen backsplash.</p><p>Attempting to present both services through one undifferentiated website experience would make the business harder to navigate and reduce the clarity of its individual service offerings.</p><p>I approached ARCED as a connected digital presence rather than a single website project.</p><p>The objective was to create two independent, service-focused online experiences that could maintain a recognizable shared identity while communicating different specializations.</p><p>I independently managed the work from requirements gathering and website architecture through UX/UI design, responsive development and production delivery.</p><p>The project included developing service-oriented content structures, preparing existing brand assets for digital use, establishing conversion pathways, designing responsive interfaces, integrating customer inquiry functionality and organizing content around local search intent.</p><p>The result was a pair of commercial websites designed to help potential customers understand ARCED's services, evaluate the company and take the next step toward requesting an estimate.</p>"],["Business Context & Initial Challenge","<p>Construction websites serve a different purpose from purely editorial or experimental digital experiences.</p><p>Potential customers rarely visit a contractor's website simply to explore its visual identity.</p><p>They usually arrive with a practical problem.</p><p>They need a driveway installed, a concrete surface replaced, a bathroom tiled or a renovation completed.</p><p>Their primary concerns often include the contractor's capabilities, service area, reliability, project requirements, pricing expectations and how to initiate contact.</p><p>For ARCED, the challenge was to translate specialist construction services into an accessible digital experience.</p><p>The business needed to communicate two distinct directions:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete Construction*</em></p><p>Work involving concrete installations, foundations, driveways, patios, sidewalks, garage floors and related concrete projects.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Work involving bathroom and kitchen tiling, floors, walls, showers, backsplashes, waterproofing, tile replacement and finishing details.</p><p>The digital strategy needed to distinguish these service categories without fragmenting the business identity.</p><h3>Core project challenges</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service differentiation*</em></p><p>Present concrete and tile services clearly enough that visitors can immediately understand which website is relevant to their needs.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Credibility*</em></p><p>Establish a professional online presence appropriate for a local construction business.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information clarity*</em></p><p>Explain technical services without requiring visitors to understand specialist terminology.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Lead generation*</em></p><p>Create straightforward pathways from service discovery to an estimate request.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Organize content for customers searching for construction services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand consistency*</em></p><p>Maintain a relationship between the two websites while allowing each to develop its own service-specific structure.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Responsive usability*</em></p><p>Support visitors browsing from mobile devices, tablets and desktop computers.</p><p>The project required a balance between business communication, visual design, user experience and technical implementation.</p>"],["Discovery & Requirements Analysis","<p>I began by understanding the company's service structure, target customers and practical requirements.</p><p>The discovery process included direct communication about the business's operations, the services it offered, its intended audience, its geographical market and the information customers needed before requesting work.</p><p>I also reviewed the Canadian construction market and relevant competitor approaches to organizing services, presenting expertise and encouraging inquiries.</p><p>This work informed both the information architecture and the content hierarchy.</p><h3>Business requirements</h3><p>The websites needed to communicate:</p><ul><li>Which construction services ARCED provides.</li><li>The difference between concrete and tile specializations.</li><li>Where the company operates.</li><li>What customers can expect during a project.</li><li>Which factors affect pricing.</li><li>How to request an estimate.</li><li>How to contact the business.</li><li>What information can help the company assess a potential job.</li></ul><p>The business serves residential and commercial customer needs, which also influenced the presentation of different service types.</p><h3>Customer requirements</h3><p>From the visitor's perspective, the experience needed to answer several immediate questions.</p><p>What does this company do?</p><p>Does it provide the service I need?</p><p>Does it operate in my area?</p><p>Can I understand the process before contacting anyone?</p><p>What affects the price?</p><p>How can I request an estimate?</p><p>These questions became the foundation of the UX strategy.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>The project involved requirements gathering and competitive analysis. It did not include a documented formal usability study or controlled user interviews.</em></p>"],["UX Strategy","<h3>Designing around customer intent</h3><p>The most important UX decision was to organize the websites around what potential customers were trying to accomplish.</p><p>Instead of placing the brand story ahead of everything else, the architecture prioritizes service clarity.</p><p>A visitor should be able to recognize the relevant service category quickly, understand its practical applications and locate a clear inquiry action.</p><p>The strategy was based on four principles.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Immediate Relevance*</em></p><p>Communicate the service specialization and location early in the page.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Clear Evaluation*</em></p><p>Give visitors enough information to assess whether ARCED is suitable for their project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Reduced Contact Friction*</em></p><p>Make estimate requests and contact information accessible without unnecessary navigation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*04. Consistent Business Identity*</em></p><p>Maintain visual and structural relationships between the two sites without making them interchangeable.</p><p>This approach ensured that the design remained commercially purposeful rather than relying exclusively on visual presentation.</p>"],["Why Two Separate Websites?","<p>The decision to develop two individual websites was central to the project.</p><p>Although both service directions belong to ARCED, their audiences often begin with different problems.</p><p>Someone considering a concrete driveway may be evaluating the size of an outdoor area, surface preparation, site conditions and the nature of the installation.</p><p>Someone considering tile work may be comparing finishes, waterproofing requirements, room types, surface preparation and installation details.</p><p>These differences affect what information should appear first, how the services should be grouped and which project details deserve emphasis.</p><h3>One brand, two customer journeys</h3><p>I established a shared approach to visual identity, typography, interface consistency and contact presentation.</p><p>At the same time, I developed separate service architectures tailored to the two specializations.</p><p>This allowed:</p><ul><li>More specific service descriptions.</li><li>Clearer customer journeys.</li><li>Independent local search positioning.</li><li>More relevant project presentation.</li><li>Distinct information hierarchies.</li><li>Service-specific inquiry pathways.</li></ul><p>The objective was not to create two unrelated brands.</p><p>It was to give each specialization enough independence to communicate effectively while maintaining the connection to ARCED Construction Group.</p>"],["ARCED Construction — Concrete Services Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedconstruction.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>The construction website was designed to present ARCED's concrete services to residential and commercial customers in the Winnipeg market.</p><p>Its primary responsibility was to communicate the company's capabilities and provide a straightforward path toward a project inquiry.</p><p>Concrete construction involves practical decisions that may depend on location, surface type, dimensions, preparation requirements and the intended use of the finished structure.</p><p>The information architecture therefore needed to focus on recognizable service categories rather than abstract descriptions of construction expertise.</p><h3>Service-oriented architecture</h3><p>I structured the content around concrete-related work, with service categories covering areas such as:</p><ul><li>Concrete driveways.</li><li>Foundations.</li><li>Patios.</li><li>Sidewalks and walkways.</li><li>Garage floors.</li><li>Concrete steps.</li><li>Concrete slabs.</li><li>Concrete replacement and preparation work.</li></ul><p>These categories helped translate a broad construction specialization into specific services customers could recognize.</p><h3>User experience considerations</h3><p>The design needed to support visitors who might have limited technical knowledge.</p><p>Rather than requiring users to understand construction methods before making an inquiry, the website presents the company's work through customer-relevant applications.</p><p>The experience emphasizes:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service clarity*</em></p><p>Visitors should be able to identify whether the company handles their type of project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project context*</em></p><p>Construction imagery and project-oriented content help communicate the nature of the work.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Process transparency*</em></p><p>Explaining the steps involved in arranging construction work can reduce uncertainty before an inquiry.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Contact accessibility*</em></p><p>Estimate requests and contact actions need to remain easy to locate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Content should connect the services to the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Conversion strategy</h3><p>The website is structured to guide visitors from understanding a service toward initiating contact.</p><p>The primary journey is:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover a Service → Understand the Scope → Evaluate the Company → Request an Estimate*</em></p><p>This approach turns service information into a practical path toward a business inquiry.</p><p>The objective was to create an accessible commercial website, not a complex customer portal that would introduce unnecessary steps.</p>"],["ARCED Tile — Specialist Installation Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedtile.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>ARCED Tile focuses on professional tile installation for residential and commercial interiors.</p><p>Its website needed to explain a more visually detailed service category while supporting users at different stages of renovation planning.</p><p>Tile installation customers may already know the room they want to renovate, but not the precise service terminology.</p><p>Someone looking to improve a bathroom may need information about floor tiles, shower walls, waterproofing or existing tile removal.</p><p>The information architecture needed to accommodate both general customer intentions and specific installation requirements.</p><h3>Service architecture</h3><p>I organized the interface around recognizable categories, including:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Bathroom Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting bathroom-related installation work and finishing considerations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Kitchen Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Covering kitchen surfaces and installations that need to accommodate cabinetry, counters and daily use.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Backsplashes*</em></p><p>Highlighting decorative and practical tile installation for kitchens and other interiors.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Floor Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Communicating flooring applications involving different tile types and surface conditions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Wall Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting installation work for walls and decorative surfaces.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Shower Tile &amp; Waterproofing*</em></p><p>Explaining the relationship between installation quality, preparation and water protection.</p><p>Additional content addresses tile removal, replacement, repairs and different material requirements.</p><h3>Information hierarchy</h3><p>The website guides visitors from broad service recognition toward greater detail.</p><p>The primary page structure includes:</p><ul><li>Clear service positioning.</li><li>Service categories.</li><li>Supporting explanations.</li><li>Reasons to consider the company.</li><li>Installation process.</li><li>Project presentation.</li><li>Pricing guidance.</li><li>Frequently asked questions.</li><li>Estimate request options.</li><li>Contact information.</li></ul><p>This sequence reflects the different stages of a potential customer's decision.</p><p>A visitor who already understands the service can proceed toward an estimate.</p><p>A visitor who needs more information can explore the process, project examples and practical considerations before contacting the company.</p>"],["Designing the Customer Journey","<p>Both websites were designed around a common commercial objective: helping relevant visitors become informed prospective customers.</p><p>However, I did not approach conversion as simply placing more buttons on a page.</p><p>A successful inquiry experience requires users to understand what they are requesting.</p><h3>Stage 01. Recognition</h3><p>The visitor identifies the relevant service and confirms that the business operates in the appropriate location.</p><h3>Stage 02. Exploration</h3><p>The visitor reviews service information and understands the broad scope of the work.</p><h3>Stage 03. Evaluation</h3><p>Supporting content helps clarify the installation process, project considerations and company presentation.</p><h3>Stage 04. Inquiry</h3><p>The user is given a clear opportunity to request an estimate or contact the company.</p><h3>Stage 05. Business Follow-Up</h3><p>The website supports the transition from digital inquiry to direct communication, where project-specific requirements can be assessed.</p><p>The interfaces were designed to support this progression without forcing every visitor through an identical sequence.</p><p>Clear navigation and repeated contextual contact opportunities make the experience more flexible.</p>"],["Lead Generation & Estimate Requests","<p>For a construction business, the website's commercial value is closely connected to the quality and clarity of customer inquiries.</p><p>The design therefore needed to support users who were ready to discuss a project as well as visitors who still required more information.</p><p>I incorporated enquiry-oriented structures and contact functionality into the websites.</p><h3>Clear calls to action</h3><p>Estimate requests are presented in relation to the services being explored.</p><p>This allows a visitor to act when they have enough context rather than requiring them to return to the top of the page or search for contact details.</p><h3>Practical information collection</h3><p>A useful construction inquiry generally benefits from information about the project type, location, dimensions, requirements and contact details.</p><p>The website's estimate-oriented forms are designed to support this kind of initial communication.</p><h3>Balancing information and friction</h3><p>A form that asks too little may produce an inquiry lacking the details required for a useful response.</p><p>A form that asks too much may discourage a potential customer from completing it.</p><p>The objective was to maintain an understandable entry point to the estimate process while leaving detailed project assessment to direct follow-up.</p><p>The websites do not attempt to replace the contractor's professional evaluation.</p><p>They create a structured starting point for that conversation.</p>"],["Pricing Guidance & Cost Estimation","<p>One of the recurring challenges in construction services is pricing uncertainty.</p><p>Customers may want to understand the approximate cost of a project before contacting a contractor.</p><p>However, installation prices depend on multiple variables, including materials, project size, preparation, complexity and site conditions.</p><p>ARCED Tile includes a cost-estimation entry point as part of its customer experience.</p><h3>UX objective</h3><p>The purpose of preliminary pricing guidance is to help visitors understand the factors that influence a quote.</p><p>It is not to present an automated estimate as a guaranteed final contract price.</p><h3>Design considerations</h3><p>The experience needs to communicate:</p><ul><li>The type of work being considered.</li><li>Relevant project dimensions or scope.</li><li>Material and installation considerations.</li><li>Preparation requirements.</li><li>Why final pricing may require further assessment.</li></ul><p>By making pricing factors more understandable, the website can help visitors approach a conversation with more realistic expectations.</p><p>This supports the broader goal of transparent communication while preserving the role of an accurate project-specific estimate.</p>"],["Trust, Credibility & Service Communication","<p>Construction customers frequently evaluate a business before contacting it.</p><p>Unlike a low-cost online purchase, a renovation or concrete installation may involve significant expense, disruption and reliance on a contractor's professional workmanship.</p><p>For this reason, credibility was an important part of the content strategy.</p><h3>Communicating capabilities</h3><p>Service explanations and project-oriented imagery give visitors a clearer understanding of the work the company offers.</p><h3>Explaining the process</h3><p>Process content helps make the transition from initial inquiry to completed installation more understandable.</p><h3>Addressing uncertainty</h3><p>Frequently asked questions provide space to clarify common concerns about materials, service areas, installation requirements and estimates.</p><h3>Presenting business commitments</h3><p>The Tile website communicates company-stated information about insurance, a two-year workmanship warranty and the availability of residential and commercial services.</p><p>These details are presented as business information rather than independent certifications or guarantees of project outcomes.</p><h3>Avoiding artificial social proof</h3><p>Credibility should come from verifiable business information, actual services, relevant project materials and clear communication.</p><p>The case study does not claim a measured increase in trust or rely on invented customer testimonials.</p><p>The design objective was to give prospective customers enough relevant information to assess the business for themselves.</p>"],["Local SEO & Content Architecture","<p>ARCED operates in a geographically defined service market.</p><p>Its websites therefore needed to communicate not only what the company does, but also where those services are available.</p><p>I incorporated local search considerations into the content planning and page architecture.</p><h3>Service-specific search intent</h3><p>Concrete services and tile installation generate different search intentions.</p><p>The content strategy distinguished terms associated with the two specializations rather than treating all construction-related searches as interchangeable.</p><p>Examples of relevant search categories include:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete*</em></p><p>Concrete contractor, concrete driveways, patios, foundations, sidewalks and related installation services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile*</em></p><p>Tile installation, bathroom tile, kitchen backsplash, floor tile, shower tile and related services in Winnipeg.</p><h3>Content organization</h3><p>The websites use clear service positioning and descriptive content to support visitors searching for particular types of work.</p><p>The planning included attention to:</p><ul><li>Service-specific headings.</li><li>Descriptive page titles.</li><li>Local market relevance.</li><li>Logical content hierarchy.</li><li>Internal navigation.</li><li>Relevant service explanations.</li><li>Contact and estimate entry points.</li><li>Responsive presentation.</li></ul><h3>UX and SEO alignment</h3><p>The objective was not to fill pages with repeated keywords.</p><p>It was to make important services understandable to both visitors and search engines.</p><p>A well-structured service page should answer a recognizable customer question while clearly communicating its subject.</p><p>This approach supports search-oriented content without sacrificing readability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*The project established an SEO-oriented website structure. It does not claim verified Google rankings, organic traffic growth or search performance improvements without supporting analytics.*</em></p>"],["Visual Identity & Art Direction","<p>The two websites needed a visual language appropriate for a professional construction business.</p><p>The design direction prioritized clarity, confidence and consistency.</p><p>The objective was to communicate a dependable commercial identity without making the interfaces visually generic.</p><h3>Shared brand foundation</h3><p>ARCED Construction and ARCED Tile were designed to remain recognizable as parts of one business.</p><p>This required consistent treatment of:</p><ul><li>Brand assets.</li><li>Typography.</li><li>Layout principles.</li><li>Navigation patterns.</li><li>Calls to action.</li><li>Contact information.</li><li>Interface components.</li></ul><h3>Brand asset preparation</h3><p>I prepared and vectorized existing brand materials for more consistent digital use.</p><p>This included working with the existing ARCED identity rather than claiming authorship of the original brand concept.</p><p>The goal was to ensure that the available assets could be used clearly and consistently across different screen sizes and interface contexts.</p><h3>Service-specific presentation</h3><p>While maintaining a shared identity, the two websites allow their subject matter to influence the visual presentation.</p><p>Concrete services benefit from clear construction-oriented content and practical project information.</p><p>Tile installation benefits from detailed visual examples, material presentation and attention to finishes.</p><p>The design system needed enough flexibility to accommodate both without losing consistency.</p>"],["Responsive UX/UI Design","<p>The websites were designed for desktop, tablet and mobile experiences.</p><p>Responsive design was an important requirement because construction customers may access a contractor's website in different contexts.</p><p>Someone might browse services from a computer while planning a renovation.</p><p>Another visitor might open the website on a phone while comparing contractors or preparing to request an estimate.</p><p>The interface needed to remain understandable across those situations.</p><h3>Responsive navigation</h3><p>Navigation structures were adapted to preserve access to important content on smaller screens.</p><h3>Service presentation</h3><p>Service descriptions and supporting imagery were organized to maintain clear hierarchy without requiring desktop-sized layouts.</p><h3>Contact accessibility</h3><p>Inquiry and contact actions needed to remain accessible on mobile devices.</p><h3>Content readability</h3><p>Typography, spacing and component proportions were designed to support scanning and comprehension.</p><h3>Interaction consistency</h3><p>Interactive elements needed to behave predictably across different devices.</p><p>The objective was to preserve the same essential customer journey rather than simply shrink the desktop interface.</p>"],["Content Design & UX Writing","<p>A technically accurate service description is not automatically a useful one.</p><p>Construction terminology can be unfamiliar to customers who are planning their first renovation or concrete project.</p><p>I therefore treated content structure as part of the user experience.</p><h3>Clear service naming</h3><p>Service categories were organized around recognizable customer needs.</p><h3>Practical explanations</h3><p>Supporting descriptions communicate what the work involves and where it may be relevant.</p><h3>Action-oriented language</h3><p>Calls to action were designed to make the next step understandable.</p><h3>Frequently asked questions</h3><p>FAQ content provides additional detail without requiring every visitor to read long explanations before identifying the service they need.</p><h3>Consistent tone</h3><p>The business presentation was developed to sound professional, direct and approachable.</p><p>This helped connect the brand's visual identity to its written communication.</p><p>Content design was an important part of the project because the websites needed to function as commercial communication tools, not merely visual portfolios.</p>"],["Front-End Development & Technical Delivery","<p>My involvement continued beyond visual design into the implementation of the production websites.</p><p>I independently managed the transition from the planned information architecture and interface direction to responsive, functioning web experiences.</p><h3>Development scope</h3><p>The work included:</p><ul><li>Implementing page structures.</li><li>Developing responsive layouts.</li><li>Creating reusable interface patterns.</li><li>Integrating visual assets.</li><li>Building interactive components.</li><li>Implementing navigation behavior.</li><li>Connecting contact and inquiry functionality.</li><li>Preparing customer-facing content.</li><li>Supporting basic technical SEO requirements.</li><li>Testing different viewport sizes.</li><li>Preparing and delivering the published websites.</li></ul><h3>Two independent digital properties</h3><p>The main technical and organizational consideration was maintaining separate websites with their own content and navigation while preserving a consistent business identity.</p><p>Each site needed to function independently for its relevant audience.</p><p>At the same time, both needed to feel connected through the ARCED brand.</p><h3>Independent project ownership</h3><p>I was responsible for coordinating the work across requirements, design decisions, implementation and final delivery.</p><p>This meant evaluating the websites as complete products rather than treating design and development as unrelated stages.</p><p>Decisions about navigation affected content organization.</p><p>Decisions about service categories affected search-oriented structure.</p><p>Decisions about estimate requests affected interface hierarchy.</p><p>Maintaining responsibility across those relationships helped preserve consistency between the original objectives and the implemented websites.</p>"],["Key Design Decisions","<p>Several decisions were particularly important to the project's direction.</p><h3>Decision 01. Separate the two specializations</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Concrete construction and tile installation involve different customer needs and search intentions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Develop two independent service-focused websites under a connected brand identity.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Give each specialization enough space to communicate clearly without creating unnecessary competition between unrelated service categories.</p><h3>Decision 02. Prioritize services over generic brand messaging</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors looking for a contractor usually want to confirm practical service relevance quickly.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Place clear service positioning and recognizable service categories prominently within the experience.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Help users establish whether the company offers the work they need before asking them to explore secondary content.</p><h3>Decision 03. Connect information with inquiry actions</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors may decide to contact the company at different points in their evaluation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Integrate estimate and contact opportunities into the relevant service journeys.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Make the transition from understanding a service to requesting information more direct.</p><h3>Decision 04. Use a consistent but flexible visual system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Two independent websites can easily feel unrelated or become visually repetitive.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Retain shared brand patterns while adapting content and presentation to each specialization.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Preserve business recognition without sacrificing the clarity of individual services.</p><h3>Decision 05. Make local relevance explicit</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction services are geographically constrained, and customers need to identify appropriate providers.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Structure content around relevant services and the Winnipeg market.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Connect customer search intent with clear service and location information.</p><h3>Decision 06. Explain pricing without promising false precision</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction costs depend on conditions that may not be fully known during an initial website visit.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Support preliminary pricing understanding while maintaining a clear route toward a project-specific estimate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Improve transparency without presenting an automated calculation as a binding professional quote.</p>"],["Project Challenges & Problem-Solving","<h3>Maintaining distinction without fragmentation</h3><p>The first challenge was making the concrete and tile websites feel independent enough to serve different audiences while retaining a shared business identity.</p><p>The solution was to separate their content architecture while maintaining common visual and interaction principles.</p><h3>Balancing technical information and accessibility</h3><p>Construction services require specific explanations, but overly technical content can create unnecessary friction.</p><p>The interface therefore needed to communicate practical service applications before expanding into specialized details.</p><h3>Designing for commercial intent</h3><p>The websites were not intended as purely artistic digital experiences.</p><p>Visual choices needed to support comprehension, credibility and clear inquiry pathways.</p><p>This required evaluating design decisions against the practical goals of the business.</p><h3>Supporting different devices</h3><p>Responsive implementation required adapting information density, navigation and interaction patterns without losing access to important content.</p><h3>Coordinating design and development</h3><p>Managing the websites independently meant ensuring that the implemented result reflected the intended UX, rather than treating development as a separate final step.</p><p>These challenges reinforced the importance of approaching commercial web projects as connected systems of content, design and functionality.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The project established a dedicated digital presence for two service directions within ARCED Construction Group.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Before the Project</th><th>Delivered Direction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>No established dedicated website</td><td>Two published commercial websites</td></tr><tr><td>Service positioning</td><td>Services needed clear online separation</td><td>Distinct concrete and tile experiences</td></tr><tr><td>Information architecture</td><td>No dedicated digital service structure</td><td>Organized, service-oriented navigation</td></tr><tr><td>Visual consistency</td><td>Existing brand materials requiring digital preparation</td><td>Coordinated visual presentation</td></tr><tr><td>Customer inquiry</td><td>Direct contact without a structured website journey</td><td>Dedicated inquiry and estimate pathways</td></tr><tr><td>Local discovery</td><td>No dedicated website architecture</td><td>Winnipeg-oriented service content</td></tr><tr><td>Device support</td><td>No existing responsive website experience</td><td>Responsive desktop, tablet and mobile interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Service information</td><td>No centralized online presentation</td><td>Structured explanations and supporting content</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This comparison describes the delivered digital capabilities rather than a measured increase in sales or customer acquisition.</p>"],["Results & Deliverables","<p>The project resulted in two independently published commercial websites.</p><h3>ARCED Construction</h3><p>A dedicated online experience presenting the concrete construction specialization through service-oriented information architecture, responsive design and customer inquiry pathways.</p><h3>ARCED Tile</h3><p>A specialist tile installation website combining structured service content, project presentation, process explanations, pricing guidance, frequently asked questions and estimate request functionality.</p><h3>Shared brand presentation</h3><p>The sites maintain a recognizable relationship through consistent brand assets, visual principles and interface patterns.</p><h3>Digital customer journeys</h3><p>Both websites provide a structured route from service discovery to contacting the business.</p><h3>Local content foundation</h3><p>The content architecture supports clear communication of services within the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Responsive implementation</h3><p>The interfaces were implemented for use across different screen sizes and interaction contexts.</p><h3>Production delivery</h3><p>Both websites were developed and published as accessible commercial properties.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project outcomes should be evaluated accurately.*</em></p><p>The confirmed deliverables are two websites, their content and interface systems, their inquiry pathways and their production implementation.</p><p>The available project information does not establish verified changes in conversion rates, lead volume, search rankings or revenue.</p><p>These would require post-launch analytics and a defined measurement period.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>ARCED demonstrates my ability to independently manage commercial website projects across strategy, design and implementation.</p><p>My work included understanding business requirements, researching the relevant market, defining the information architecture, establishing visual direction, preparing brand assets and developing the production websites.</p><p>Rather than working only on isolated interface screens, I was responsible for shaping the complete digital experience around the business's services and customer needs.</p><p>The project required competency in several connected areas.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Requirements Analysis*</em></p><p>Understanding the company's service structure, customer groups, geographic market and commercial objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Strategy*</em></p><p>Designing separate customer journeys for two distinct service specializations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Organizing service content, navigation and inquiry pathways.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI Design*</em></p><p>Developing responsive interfaces that balance visual quality with practical usability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Adaptation*</em></p><p>Preparing existing identity assets for a consistent digital presentation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Writing &amp; Content Strategy*</em></p><p>Presenting construction services through clear, customer-oriented explanations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local SEO Planning*</em></p><p>Structuring content around relevant service categories and geographic search intent.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Front-End Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive layouts, interactive components and functional web experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Delivery*</em></p><p>Preparing the websites for publication and maintaining responsibility for the final implementation.</p><p>The defining aspect of my contribution was continuity across these responsibilities.</p><p>I was not simply producing visual layouts for someone else to interpret.</p><p>I managed the design and development process as a complete commercial deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>ARCED was an important exercise in translating a traditional service business into a purposeful digital experience.</p><p>The project demonstrated that effective commercial web design begins with understanding how customers make decisions.</p><p>A construction website does not need to overwhelm visitors with visual effects.</p><p>It needs to communicate relevant services, establish a professional presentation, explain important details and make the next action clear.</p><p>The most significant strategic decision was treating concrete construction and tile installation as two different customer journeys rather than forcing them into one generic interface.</p><p>This required both consistency and restraint.</p><p>The business identity needed to remain recognizable, but the content architecture had to respect the differences between the two specializations.</p><p>The project also reinforced the importance of treating content, UX, visual design and technical implementation as connected disciplines.</p><p>The value of the websites came from how those elements worked together.</p><p>My individual responsibility extended from the initial business requirements to the published digital products.</p><p>ARCED represents my ability to independently design, develop and deliver commercial websites for an international client, adapting the experience to different services, customer intentions and business objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Two specialized websites, one recognizable business identity, and a digital experience built around the way real customers search for construction services.*</em></p>"]],"images":["assets/commercial-covers/arced-construction.png","assets/commercial-covers/arced-tile.png"]},"ru":{"name":"ARCED CONSTRUCTION GROUP","industry":"Строительство / улучшение дома","platform":"Два коммерческих сайта · цифровое присутствие","location":"Виннипег, Канада","role":"UX/UI · веб-дизайн · разработка · айдентика","scope":"Information architecture · Responsive UX/UI · Service presentation · Implementation","strapline":"A connected digital presence for two related businesses","links":[{"label":"ARCED Construction","href":"https://arcedconstruction.ca"},{"label":"ARCED Tile","href":"https://arcedtile.ca"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>ARCED Construction Group is a Canadian construction business operating in Winnipeg, Manitoba, with services covering concrete work and professional tile installation.</p><p>The project involved designing and developing two distinct commercial websites representing separate service directions under one business identity.</p><p>Although concrete construction and tile installation belong to the broader construction industry, they involve different customer expectations, search behavior, project requirements and decision-making processes.</p><p>A homeowner looking for concrete driveway installation does not necessarily need the same information as someone planning a bathroom renovation or kitchen backsplash.</p><p>Attempting to present both services through one undifferentiated website experience would make the business harder to navigate and reduce the clarity of its individual service offerings.</p><p>I approached ARCED as a connected digital presence rather than a single website project.</p><p>The objective was to create two independent, service-focused online experiences that could maintain a recognizable shared identity while communicating different specializations.</p><p>I independently managed the work from requirements gathering and website architecture through UX/UI design, responsive development and production delivery.</p><p>The project included developing service-oriented content structures, preparing existing brand assets for digital use, establishing conversion pathways, designing responsive interfaces, integrating customer inquiry functionality and organizing content around local search intent.</p><p>The result was a pair of commercial websites designed to help potential customers understand ARCED's services, evaluate the company and take the next step toward requesting an estimate.</p>"],["Business Context & Initial Challenge","<p>Construction websites serve a different purpose from purely editorial or experimental digital experiences.</p><p>Potential customers rarely visit a contractor's website simply to explore its visual identity.</p><p>They usually arrive with a practical problem.</p><p>They need a driveway installed, a concrete surface replaced, a bathroom tiled or a renovation completed.</p><p>Their primary concerns often include the contractor's capabilities, service area, reliability, project requirements, pricing expectations and how to initiate contact.</p><p>For ARCED, the challenge was to translate specialist construction services into an accessible digital experience.</p><p>The business needed to communicate two distinct directions:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete Construction*</em></p><p>Work involving concrete installations, foundations, driveways, patios, sidewalks, garage floors and related concrete projects.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Work involving bathroom and kitchen tiling, floors, walls, showers, backsplashes, waterproofing, tile replacement and finishing details.</p><p>The digital strategy needed to distinguish these service categories without fragmenting the business identity.</p><h3>Core project challenges</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service differentiation*</em></p><p>Present concrete and tile services clearly enough that visitors can immediately understand which website is relevant to their needs.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Credibility*</em></p><p>Establish a professional online presence appropriate for a local construction business.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information clarity*</em></p><p>Explain technical services without requiring visitors to understand specialist terminology.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Lead generation*</em></p><p>Create straightforward pathways from service discovery to an estimate request.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Organize content for customers searching for construction services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand consistency*</em></p><p>Maintain a relationship between the two websites while allowing each to develop its own service-specific structure.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Responsive usability*</em></p><p>Support visitors browsing from mobile devices, tablets and desktop computers.</p><p>The project required a balance between business communication, visual design, user experience and technical implementation.</p>"],["Discovery & Requirements Analysis","<p>I began by understanding the company's service structure, target customers and practical requirements.</p><p>The discovery process included direct communication about the business's operations, the services it offered, its intended audience, its geographical market and the information customers needed before requesting work.</p><p>I also reviewed the Canadian construction market and relevant competitor approaches to organizing services, presenting expertise and encouraging inquiries.</p><p>This work informed both the information architecture and the content hierarchy.</p><h3>Business requirements</h3><p>The websites needed to communicate:</p><ul><li>Which construction services ARCED provides.</li><li>The difference between concrete and tile specializations.</li><li>Where the company operates.</li><li>What customers can expect during a project.</li><li>Which factors affect pricing.</li><li>How to request an estimate.</li><li>How to contact the business.</li><li>What information can help the company assess a potential job.</li></ul><p>The business serves residential and commercial customer needs, which also influenced the presentation of different service types.</p><h3>Customer requirements</h3><p>From the visitor's perspective, the experience needed to answer several immediate questions.</p><p>What does this company do?</p><p>Does it provide the service I need?</p><p>Does it operate in my area?</p><p>Can I understand the process before contacting anyone?</p><p>What affects the price?</p><p>How can I request an estimate?</p><p>These questions became the foundation of the UX strategy.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>The project involved requirements gathering and competitive analysis. It did not include a documented formal usability study or controlled user interviews.</em></p>"],["UX Strategy","<h3>Designing around customer intent</h3><p>The most important UX decision was to organize the websites around what potential customers were trying to accomplish.</p><p>Instead of placing the brand story ahead of everything else, the architecture prioritizes service clarity.</p><p>A visitor should be able to recognize the relevant service category quickly, understand its practical applications and locate a clear inquiry action.</p><p>The strategy was based on four principles.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Immediate Relevance*</em></p><p>Communicate the service specialization and location early in the page.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Clear Evaluation*</em></p><p>Give visitors enough information to assess whether ARCED is suitable for their project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Reduced Contact Friction*</em></p><p>Make estimate requests and contact information accessible without unnecessary navigation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*04. Consistent Business Identity*</em></p><p>Maintain visual and structural relationships between the two sites without making them interchangeable.</p><p>This approach ensured that the design remained commercially purposeful rather than relying exclusively on visual presentation.</p>"],["Why Two Separate Websites?","<p>The decision to develop two individual websites was central to the project.</p><p>Although both service directions belong to ARCED, their audiences often begin with different problems.</p><p>Someone considering a concrete driveway may be evaluating the size of an outdoor area, surface preparation, site conditions and the nature of the installation.</p><p>Someone considering tile work may be comparing finishes, waterproofing requirements, room types, surface preparation and installation details.</p><p>These differences affect what information should appear first, how the services should be grouped and which project details deserve emphasis.</p><h3>One brand, two customer journeys</h3><p>I established a shared approach to visual identity, typography, interface consistency and contact presentation.</p><p>At the same time, I developed separate service architectures tailored to the two specializations.</p><p>This allowed:</p><ul><li>More specific service descriptions.</li><li>Clearer customer journeys.</li><li>Independent local search positioning.</li><li>More relevant project presentation.</li><li>Distinct information hierarchies.</li><li>Service-specific inquiry pathways.</li></ul><p>The objective was not to create two unrelated brands.</p><p>It was to give each specialization enough independence to communicate effectively while maintaining the connection to ARCED Construction Group.</p>"],["ARCED Construction — Concrete Services Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedconstruction.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>The construction website was designed to present ARCED's concrete services to residential and commercial customers in the Winnipeg market.</p><p>Its primary responsibility was to communicate the company's capabilities and provide a straightforward path toward a project inquiry.</p><p>Concrete construction involves practical decisions that may depend on location, surface type, dimensions, preparation requirements and the intended use of the finished structure.</p><p>The information architecture therefore needed to focus on recognizable service categories rather than abstract descriptions of construction expertise.</p><h3>Service-oriented architecture</h3><p>I structured the content around concrete-related work, with service categories covering areas such as:</p><ul><li>Concrete driveways.</li><li>Foundations.</li><li>Patios.</li><li>Sidewalks and walkways.</li><li>Garage floors.</li><li>Concrete steps.</li><li>Concrete slabs.</li><li>Concrete replacement and preparation work.</li></ul><p>These categories helped translate a broad construction specialization into specific services customers could recognize.</p><h3>User experience considerations</h3><p>The design needed to support visitors who might have limited technical knowledge.</p><p>Rather than requiring users to understand construction methods before making an inquiry, the website presents the company's work through customer-relevant applications.</p><p>The experience emphasizes:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service clarity*</em></p><p>Visitors should be able to identify whether the company handles their type of project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project context*</em></p><p>Construction imagery and project-oriented content help communicate the nature of the work.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Process transparency*</em></p><p>Explaining the steps involved in arranging construction work can reduce uncertainty before an inquiry.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Contact accessibility*</em></p><p>Estimate requests and contact actions need to remain easy to locate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Content should connect the services to the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Conversion strategy</h3><p>The website is structured to guide visitors from understanding a service toward initiating contact.</p><p>The primary journey is:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover a Service → Understand the Scope → Evaluate the Company → Request an Estimate*</em></p><p>This approach turns service information into a practical path toward a business inquiry.</p><p>The objective was to create an accessible commercial website, not a complex customer portal that would introduce unnecessary steps.</p>"],["ARCED Tile — Specialist Installation Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedtile.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>ARCED Tile focuses on professional tile installation for residential and commercial interiors.</p><p>Its website needed to explain a more visually detailed service category while supporting users at different stages of renovation planning.</p><p>Tile installation customers may already know the room they want to renovate, but not the precise service terminology.</p><p>Someone looking to improve a bathroom may need information about floor tiles, shower walls, waterproofing or existing tile removal.</p><p>The information architecture needed to accommodate both general customer intentions and specific installation requirements.</p><h3>Service architecture</h3><p>I organized the interface around recognizable categories, including:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Bathroom Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting bathroom-related installation work and finishing considerations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Kitchen Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Covering kitchen surfaces and installations that need to accommodate cabinetry, counters and daily use.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Backsplashes*</em></p><p>Highlighting decorative and practical tile installation for kitchens and other interiors.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Floor Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Communicating flooring applications involving different tile types and surface conditions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Wall Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting installation work for walls and decorative surfaces.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Shower Tile &amp; Waterproofing*</em></p><p>Explaining the relationship between installation quality, preparation and water protection.</p><p>Additional content addresses tile removal, replacement, repairs and different material requirements.</p><h3>Information hierarchy</h3><p>The website guides visitors from broad service recognition toward greater detail.</p><p>The primary page structure includes:</p><ul><li>Clear service positioning.</li><li>Service categories.</li><li>Supporting explanations.</li><li>Reasons to consider the company.</li><li>Installation process.</li><li>Project presentation.</li><li>Pricing guidance.</li><li>Frequently asked questions.</li><li>Estimate request options.</li><li>Contact information.</li></ul><p>This sequence reflects the different stages of a potential customer's decision.</p><p>A visitor who already understands the service can proceed toward an estimate.</p><p>A visitor who needs more information can explore the process, project examples and practical considerations before contacting the company.</p>"],["Designing the Customer Journey","<p>Both websites were designed around a common commercial objective: helping relevant visitors become informed prospective customers.</p><p>However, I did not approach conversion as simply placing more buttons on a page.</p><p>A successful inquiry experience requires users to understand what they are requesting.</p><h3>Stage 01. Recognition</h3><p>The visitor identifies the relevant service and confirms that the business operates in the appropriate location.</p><h3>Stage 02. Exploration</h3><p>The visitor reviews service information and understands the broad scope of the work.</p><h3>Stage 03. Evaluation</h3><p>Supporting content helps clarify the installation process, project considerations and company presentation.</p><h3>Stage 04. Inquiry</h3><p>The user is given a clear opportunity to request an estimate or contact the company.</p><h3>Stage 05. Business Follow-Up</h3><p>The website supports the transition from digital inquiry to direct communication, where project-specific requirements can be assessed.</p><p>The interfaces were designed to support this progression without forcing every visitor through an identical sequence.</p><p>Clear navigation and repeated contextual contact opportunities make the experience more flexible.</p>"],["Lead Generation & Estimate Requests","<p>For a construction business, the website's commercial value is closely connected to the quality and clarity of customer inquiries.</p><p>The design therefore needed to support users who were ready to discuss a project as well as visitors who still required more information.</p><p>I incorporated enquiry-oriented structures and contact functionality into the websites.</p><h3>Clear calls to action</h3><p>Estimate requests are presented in relation to the services being explored.</p><p>This allows a visitor to act when they have enough context rather than requiring them to return to the top of the page or search for contact details.</p><h3>Practical information collection</h3><p>A useful construction inquiry generally benefits from information about the project type, location, dimensions, requirements and contact details.</p><p>The website's estimate-oriented forms are designed to support this kind of initial communication.</p><h3>Balancing information and friction</h3><p>A form that asks too little may produce an inquiry lacking the details required for a useful response.</p><p>A form that asks too much may discourage a potential customer from completing it.</p><p>The objective was to maintain an understandable entry point to the estimate process while leaving detailed project assessment to direct follow-up.</p><p>The websites do not attempt to replace the contractor's professional evaluation.</p><p>They create a structured starting point for that conversation.</p>"],["Pricing Guidance & Cost Estimation","<p>One of the recurring challenges in construction services is pricing uncertainty.</p><p>Customers may want to understand the approximate cost of a project before contacting a contractor.</p><p>However, installation prices depend on multiple variables, including materials, project size, preparation, complexity and site conditions.</p><p>ARCED Tile includes a cost-estimation entry point as part of its customer experience.</p><h3>UX objective</h3><p>The purpose of preliminary pricing guidance is to help visitors understand the factors that influence a quote.</p><p>It is not to present an automated estimate as a guaranteed final contract price.</p><h3>Design considerations</h3><p>The experience needs to communicate:</p><ul><li>The type of work being considered.</li><li>Relevant project dimensions or scope.</li><li>Material and installation considerations.</li><li>Preparation requirements.</li><li>Why final pricing may require further assessment.</li></ul><p>By making pricing factors more understandable, the website can help visitors approach a conversation with more realistic expectations.</p><p>This supports the broader goal of transparent communication while preserving the role of an accurate project-specific estimate.</p>"],["Trust, Credibility & Service Communication","<p>Construction customers frequently evaluate a business before contacting it.</p><p>Unlike a low-cost online purchase, a renovation or concrete installation may involve significant expense, disruption and reliance on a contractor's professional workmanship.</p><p>For this reason, credibility was an important part of the content strategy.</p><h3>Communicating capabilities</h3><p>Service explanations and project-oriented imagery give visitors a clearer understanding of the work the company offers.</p><h3>Explaining the process</h3><p>Process content helps make the transition from initial inquiry to completed installation more understandable.</p><h3>Addressing uncertainty</h3><p>Frequently asked questions provide space to clarify common concerns about materials, service areas, installation requirements and estimates.</p><h3>Presenting business commitments</h3><p>The Tile website communicates company-stated information about insurance, a two-year workmanship warranty and the availability of residential and commercial services.</p><p>These details are presented as business information rather than independent certifications or guarantees of project outcomes.</p><h3>Avoiding artificial social proof</h3><p>Credibility should come from verifiable business information, actual services, relevant project materials and clear communication.</p><p>The case study does not claim a measured increase in trust or rely on invented customer testimonials.</p><p>The design objective was to give prospective customers enough relevant information to assess the business for themselves.</p>"],["Local SEO & Content Architecture","<p>ARCED operates in a geographically defined service market.</p><p>Its websites therefore needed to communicate not only what the company does, but also where those services are available.</p><p>I incorporated local search considerations into the content planning and page architecture.</p><h3>Service-specific search intent</h3><p>Concrete services and tile installation generate different search intentions.</p><p>The content strategy distinguished terms associated with the two specializations rather than treating all construction-related searches as interchangeable.</p><p>Examples of relevant search categories include:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete*</em></p><p>Concrete contractor, concrete driveways, patios, foundations, sidewalks and related installation services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile*</em></p><p>Tile installation, bathroom tile, kitchen backsplash, floor tile, shower tile and related services in Winnipeg.</p><h3>Content organization</h3><p>The websites use clear service positioning and descriptive content to support visitors searching for particular types of work.</p><p>The planning included attention to:</p><ul><li>Service-specific headings.</li><li>Descriptive page titles.</li><li>Local market relevance.</li><li>Logical content hierarchy.</li><li>Internal navigation.</li><li>Relevant service explanations.</li><li>Contact and estimate entry points.</li><li>Responsive presentation.</li></ul><h3>UX and SEO alignment</h3><p>The objective was not to fill pages with repeated keywords.</p><p>It was to make important services understandable to both visitors and search engines.</p><p>A well-structured service page should answer a recognizable customer question while clearly communicating its subject.</p><p>This approach supports search-oriented content without sacrificing readability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*The project established an SEO-oriented website structure. It does not claim verified Google rankings, organic traffic growth or search performance improvements without supporting analytics.*</em></p>"],["Visual Identity & Art Direction","<p>The two websites needed a visual language appropriate for a professional construction business.</p><p>The design direction prioritized clarity, confidence and consistency.</p><p>The objective was to communicate a dependable commercial identity without making the interfaces visually generic.</p><h3>Shared brand foundation</h3><p>ARCED Construction and ARCED Tile were designed to remain recognizable as parts of one business.</p><p>This required consistent treatment of:</p><ul><li>Brand assets.</li><li>Typography.</li><li>Layout principles.</li><li>Navigation patterns.</li><li>Calls to action.</li><li>Contact information.</li><li>Interface components.</li></ul><h3>Brand asset preparation</h3><p>I prepared and vectorized existing brand materials for more consistent digital use.</p><p>This included working with the existing ARCED identity rather than claiming authorship of the original brand concept.</p><p>The goal was to ensure that the available assets could be used clearly and consistently across different screen sizes and interface contexts.</p><h3>Service-specific presentation</h3><p>While maintaining a shared identity, the two websites allow their subject matter to influence the visual presentation.</p><p>Concrete services benefit from clear construction-oriented content and practical project information.</p><p>Tile installation benefits from detailed visual examples, material presentation and attention to finishes.</p><p>The design system needed enough flexibility to accommodate both without losing consistency.</p>"],["Responsive UX/UI Design","<p>The websites were designed for desktop, tablet and mobile experiences.</p><p>Responsive design was an important requirement because construction customers may access a contractor's website in different contexts.</p><p>Someone might browse services from a computer while planning a renovation.</p><p>Another visitor might open the website on a phone while comparing contractors or preparing to request an estimate.</p><p>The interface needed to remain understandable across those situations.</p><h3>Responsive navigation</h3><p>Navigation structures were adapted to preserve access to important content on smaller screens.</p><h3>Service presentation</h3><p>Service descriptions and supporting imagery were organized to maintain clear hierarchy without requiring desktop-sized layouts.</p><h3>Contact accessibility</h3><p>Inquiry and contact actions needed to remain accessible on mobile devices.</p><h3>Content readability</h3><p>Typography, spacing and component proportions were designed to support scanning and comprehension.</p><h3>Interaction consistency</h3><p>Interactive elements needed to behave predictably across different devices.</p><p>The objective was to preserve the same essential customer journey rather than simply shrink the desktop interface.</p>"],["Content Design & UX Writing","<p>A technically accurate service description is not automatically a useful one.</p><p>Construction terminology can be unfamiliar to customers who are planning their first renovation or concrete project.</p><p>I therefore treated content structure as part of the user experience.</p><h3>Clear service naming</h3><p>Service categories were organized around recognizable customer needs.</p><h3>Practical explanations</h3><p>Supporting descriptions communicate what the work involves and where it may be relevant.</p><h3>Action-oriented language</h3><p>Calls to action were designed to make the next step understandable.</p><h3>Frequently asked questions</h3><p>FAQ content provides additional detail without requiring every visitor to read long explanations before identifying the service they need.</p><h3>Consistent tone</h3><p>The business presentation was developed to sound professional, direct and approachable.</p><p>This helped connect the brand's visual identity to its written communication.</p><p>Content design was an important part of the project because the websites needed to function as commercial communication tools, not merely visual portfolios.</p>"],["Front-End Development & Technical Delivery","<p>My involvement continued beyond visual design into the implementation of the production websites.</p><p>I independently managed the transition from the planned information architecture and interface direction to responsive, functioning web experiences.</p><h3>Development scope</h3><p>The work included:</p><ul><li>Implementing page structures.</li><li>Developing responsive layouts.</li><li>Creating reusable interface patterns.</li><li>Integrating visual assets.</li><li>Building interactive components.</li><li>Implementing navigation behavior.</li><li>Connecting contact and inquiry functionality.</li><li>Preparing customer-facing content.</li><li>Supporting basic technical SEO requirements.</li><li>Testing different viewport sizes.</li><li>Preparing and delivering the published websites.</li></ul><h3>Two independent digital properties</h3><p>The main technical and organizational consideration was maintaining separate websites with their own content and navigation while preserving a consistent business identity.</p><p>Each site needed to function independently for its relevant audience.</p><p>At the same time, both needed to feel connected through the ARCED brand.</p><h3>Independent project ownership</h3><p>I was responsible for coordinating the work across requirements, design decisions, implementation and final delivery.</p><p>This meant evaluating the websites as complete products rather than treating design and development as unrelated stages.</p><p>Decisions about navigation affected content organization.</p><p>Decisions about service categories affected search-oriented structure.</p><p>Decisions about estimate requests affected interface hierarchy.</p><p>Maintaining responsibility across those relationships helped preserve consistency between the original objectives and the implemented websites.</p>"],["Key Design Decisions","<p>Several decisions were particularly important to the project's direction.</p><h3>Decision 01. Separate the two specializations</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Concrete construction and tile installation involve different customer needs and search intentions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Develop two independent service-focused websites under a connected brand identity.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Give each specialization enough space to communicate clearly without creating unnecessary competition between unrelated service categories.</p><h3>Decision 02. Prioritize services over generic brand messaging</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors looking for a contractor usually want to confirm practical service relevance quickly.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Place clear service positioning and recognizable service categories prominently within the experience.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Help users establish whether the company offers the work they need before asking them to explore secondary content.</p><h3>Decision 03. Connect information with inquiry actions</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors may decide to contact the company at different points in their evaluation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Integrate estimate and contact opportunities into the relevant service journeys.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Make the transition from understanding a service to requesting information more direct.</p><h3>Decision 04. Use a consistent but flexible visual system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Two independent websites can easily feel unrelated or become visually repetitive.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Retain shared brand patterns while adapting content and presentation to each specialization.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Preserve business recognition without sacrificing the clarity of individual services.</p><h3>Decision 05. Make local relevance explicit</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction services are geographically constrained, and customers need to identify appropriate providers.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Structure content around relevant services and the Winnipeg market.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Connect customer search intent with clear service and location information.</p><h3>Decision 06. Explain pricing without promising false precision</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction costs depend on conditions that may not be fully known during an initial website visit.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Support preliminary pricing understanding while maintaining a clear route toward a project-specific estimate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Improve transparency without presenting an automated calculation as a binding professional quote.</p>"],["Project Challenges & Problem-Solving","<h3>Maintaining distinction without fragmentation</h3><p>The first challenge was making the concrete and tile websites feel independent enough to serve different audiences while retaining a shared business identity.</p><p>The solution was to separate their content architecture while maintaining common visual and interaction principles.</p><h3>Balancing technical information and accessibility</h3><p>Construction services require specific explanations, but overly technical content can create unnecessary friction.</p><p>The interface therefore needed to communicate practical service applications before expanding into specialized details.</p><h3>Designing for commercial intent</h3><p>The websites were not intended as purely artistic digital experiences.</p><p>Visual choices needed to support comprehension, credibility and clear inquiry pathways.</p><p>This required evaluating design decisions against the practical goals of the business.</p><h3>Supporting different devices</h3><p>Responsive implementation required adapting information density, navigation and interaction patterns without losing access to important content.</p><h3>Coordinating design and development</h3><p>Managing the websites independently meant ensuring that the implemented result reflected the intended UX, rather than treating development as a separate final step.</p><p>These challenges reinforced the importance of approaching commercial web projects as connected systems of content, design and functionality.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The project established a dedicated digital presence for two service directions within ARCED Construction Group.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Before the Project</th><th>Delivered Direction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>No established dedicated website</td><td>Two published commercial websites</td></tr><tr><td>Service positioning</td><td>Services needed clear online separation</td><td>Distinct concrete and tile experiences</td></tr><tr><td>Information architecture</td><td>No dedicated digital service structure</td><td>Organized, service-oriented navigation</td></tr><tr><td>Visual consistency</td><td>Existing brand materials requiring digital preparation</td><td>Coordinated visual presentation</td></tr><tr><td>Customer inquiry</td><td>Direct contact without a structured website journey</td><td>Dedicated inquiry and estimate pathways</td></tr><tr><td>Local discovery</td><td>No dedicated website architecture</td><td>Winnipeg-oriented service content</td></tr><tr><td>Device support</td><td>No existing responsive website experience</td><td>Responsive desktop, tablet and mobile interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Service information</td><td>No centralized online presentation</td><td>Structured explanations and supporting content</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This comparison describes the delivered digital capabilities rather than a measured increase in sales or customer acquisition.</p>"],["Results & Deliverables","<p>The project resulted in two independently published commercial websites.</p><h3>ARCED Construction</h3><p>A dedicated online experience presenting the concrete construction specialization through service-oriented information architecture, responsive design and customer inquiry pathways.</p><h3>ARCED Tile</h3><p>A specialist tile installation website combining structured service content, project presentation, process explanations, pricing guidance, frequently asked questions and estimate request functionality.</p><h3>Shared brand presentation</h3><p>The sites maintain a recognizable relationship through consistent brand assets, visual principles and interface patterns.</p><h3>Digital customer journeys</h3><p>Both websites provide a structured route from service discovery to contacting the business.</p><h3>Local content foundation</h3><p>The content architecture supports clear communication of services within the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Responsive implementation</h3><p>The interfaces were implemented for use across different screen sizes and interaction contexts.</p><h3>Production delivery</h3><p>Both websites were developed and published as accessible commercial properties.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project outcomes should be evaluated accurately.*</em></p><p>The confirmed deliverables are two websites, their content and interface systems, their inquiry pathways and their production implementation.</p><p>The available project information does not establish verified changes in conversion rates, lead volume, search rankings or revenue.</p><p>These would require post-launch analytics and a defined measurement period.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>ARCED demonstrates my ability to independently manage commercial website projects across strategy, design and implementation.</p><p>My work included understanding business requirements, researching the relevant market, defining the information architecture, establishing visual direction, preparing brand assets and developing the production websites.</p><p>Rather than working only on isolated interface screens, I was responsible for shaping the complete digital experience around the business's services and customer needs.</p><p>The project required competency in several connected areas.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Requirements Analysis*</em></p><p>Understanding the company's service structure, customer groups, geographic market and commercial objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Strategy*</em></p><p>Designing separate customer journeys for two distinct service specializations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Organizing service content, navigation and inquiry pathways.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI Design*</em></p><p>Developing responsive interfaces that balance visual quality with practical usability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Adaptation*</em></p><p>Preparing existing identity assets for a consistent digital presentation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Writing &amp; Content Strategy*</em></p><p>Presenting construction services through clear, customer-oriented explanations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local SEO Planning*</em></p><p>Structuring content around relevant service categories and geographic search intent.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Front-End Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive layouts, interactive components and functional web experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Delivery*</em></p><p>Preparing the websites for publication and maintaining responsibility for the final implementation.</p><p>The defining aspect of my contribution was continuity across these responsibilities.</p><p>I was not simply producing visual layouts for someone else to interpret.</p><p>I managed the design and development process as a complete commercial deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>ARCED was an important exercise in translating a traditional service business into a purposeful digital experience.</p><p>The project demonstrated that effective commercial web design begins with understanding how customers make decisions.</p><p>A construction website does not need to overwhelm visitors with visual effects.</p><p>It needs to communicate relevant services, establish a professional presentation, explain important details and make the next action clear.</p><p>The most significant strategic decision was treating concrete construction and tile installation as two different customer journeys rather than forcing them into one generic interface.</p><p>This required both consistency and restraint.</p><p>The business identity needed to remain recognizable, but the content architecture had to respect the differences between the two specializations.</p><p>The project also reinforced the importance of treating content, UX, visual design and technical implementation as connected disciplines.</p><p>The value of the websites came from how those elements worked together.</p><p>My individual responsibility extended from the initial business requirements to the published digital products.</p><p>ARCED represents my ability to independently design, develop and deliver commercial websites for an international client, adapting the experience to different services, customer intentions and business objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Two specialized websites, one recognizable business identity, and a digital experience built around the way real customers search for construction services.*</em></p>"]],"images":["assets/commercial-covers/arced-construction.png","assets/commercial-covers/arced-tile.png"]},"de":{"name":"ARCED CONSTRUCTION GROUP","industry":"Bauwesen / Heimverbesserung","platform":"Zwei kommerzielle Websites · digitale Präsenz","location":"Winnipeg, Kanada","role":"UX/UI · Webdesign · Entwicklung · Markenidentität","scope":"Information architecture · Responsive UX/UI · Service presentation · Implementation","strapline":"A connected digital presence for two related businesses","links":[{"label":"ARCED Construction","href":"https://arcedconstruction.ca"},{"label":"ARCED Tile","href":"https://arcedtile.ca"}],"sections":[["Project Overview","<p>ARCED Construction Group is a Canadian construction business operating in Winnipeg, Manitoba, with services covering concrete work and professional tile installation.</p><p>The project involved designing and developing two distinct commercial websites representing separate service directions under one business identity.</p><p>Although concrete construction and tile installation belong to the broader construction industry, they involve different customer expectations, search behavior, project requirements and decision-making processes.</p><p>A homeowner looking for concrete driveway installation does not necessarily need the same information as someone planning a bathroom renovation or kitchen backsplash.</p><p>Attempting to present both services through one undifferentiated website experience would make the business harder to navigate and reduce the clarity of its individual service offerings.</p><p>I approached ARCED as a connected digital presence rather than a single website project.</p><p>The objective was to create two independent, service-focused online experiences that could maintain a recognizable shared identity while communicating different specializations.</p><p>I independently managed the work from requirements gathering and website architecture through UX/UI design, responsive development and production delivery.</p><p>The project included developing service-oriented content structures, preparing existing brand assets for digital use, establishing conversion pathways, designing responsive interfaces, integrating customer inquiry functionality and organizing content around local search intent.</p><p>The result was a pair of commercial websites designed to help potential customers understand ARCED's services, evaluate the company and take the next step toward requesting an estimate.</p>"],["Business Context & Initial Challenge","<p>Construction websites serve a different purpose from purely editorial or experimental digital experiences.</p><p>Potential customers rarely visit a contractor's website simply to explore its visual identity.</p><p>They usually arrive with a practical problem.</p><p>They need a driveway installed, a concrete surface replaced, a bathroom tiled or a renovation completed.</p><p>Their primary concerns often include the contractor's capabilities, service area, reliability, project requirements, pricing expectations and how to initiate contact.</p><p>For ARCED, the challenge was to translate specialist construction services into an accessible digital experience.</p><p>The business needed to communicate two distinct directions:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete Construction*</em></p><p>Work involving concrete installations, foundations, driveways, patios, sidewalks, garage floors and related concrete projects.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Work involving bathroom and kitchen tiling, floors, walls, showers, backsplashes, waterproofing, tile replacement and finishing details.</p><p>The digital strategy needed to distinguish these service categories without fragmenting the business identity.</p><h3>Core project challenges</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service differentiation*</em></p><p>Present concrete and tile services clearly enough that visitors can immediately understand which website is relevant to their needs.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Credibility*</em></p><p>Establish a professional online presence appropriate for a local construction business.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information clarity*</em></p><p>Explain technical services without requiring visitors to understand specialist terminology.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Lead generation*</em></p><p>Create straightforward pathways from service discovery to an estimate request.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Organize content for customers searching for construction services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand consistency*</em></p><p>Maintain a relationship between the two websites while allowing each to develop its own service-specific structure.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Responsive usability*</em></p><p>Support visitors browsing from mobile devices, tablets and desktop computers.</p><p>The project required a balance between business communication, visual design, user experience and technical implementation.</p>"],["Discovery & Requirements Analysis","<p>I began by understanding the company's service structure, target customers and practical requirements.</p><p>The discovery process included direct communication about the business's operations, the services it offered, its intended audience, its geographical market and the information customers needed before requesting work.</p><p>I also reviewed the Canadian construction market and relevant competitor approaches to organizing services, presenting expertise and encouraging inquiries.</p><p>This work informed both the information architecture and the content hierarchy.</p><h3>Business requirements</h3><p>The websites needed to communicate:</p><ul><li>Which construction services ARCED provides.</li><li>The difference between concrete and tile specializations.</li><li>Where the company operates.</li><li>What customers can expect during a project.</li><li>Which factors affect pricing.</li><li>How to request an estimate.</li><li>How to contact the business.</li><li>What information can help the company assess a potential job.</li></ul><p>The business serves residential and commercial customer needs, which also influenced the presentation of different service types.</p><h3>Customer requirements</h3><p>From the visitor's perspective, the experience needed to answer several immediate questions.</p><p>What does this company do?</p><p>Does it provide the service I need?</p><p>Does it operate in my area?</p><p>Can I understand the process before contacting anyone?</p><p>What affects the price?</p><p>How can I request an estimate?</p><p>These questions became the foundation of the UX strategy.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>The project involved requirements gathering and competitive analysis. It did not include a documented formal usability study or controlled user interviews.</em></p>"],["UX Strategy","<h3>Designing around customer intent</h3><p>The most important UX decision was to organize the websites around what potential customers were trying to accomplish.</p><p>Instead of placing the brand story ahead of everything else, the architecture prioritizes service clarity.</p><p>A visitor should be able to recognize the relevant service category quickly, understand its practical applications and locate a clear inquiry action.</p><p>The strategy was based on four principles.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*01. Immediate Relevance*</em></p><p>Communicate the service specialization and location early in the page.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*02. Clear Evaluation*</em></p><p>Give visitors enough information to assess whether ARCED is suitable for their project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*03. Reduced Contact Friction*</em></p><p>Make estimate requests and contact information accessible without unnecessary navigation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*04. Consistent Business Identity*</em></p><p>Maintain visual and structural relationships between the two sites without making them interchangeable.</p><p>This approach ensured that the design remained commercially purposeful rather than relying exclusively on visual presentation.</p>"],["Why Two Separate Websites?","<p>The decision to develop two individual websites was central to the project.</p><p>Although both service directions belong to ARCED, their audiences often begin with different problems.</p><p>Someone considering a concrete driveway may be evaluating the size of an outdoor area, surface preparation, site conditions and the nature of the installation.</p><p>Someone considering tile work may be comparing finishes, waterproofing requirements, room types, surface preparation and installation details.</p><p>These differences affect what information should appear first, how the services should be grouped and which project details deserve emphasis.</p><h3>One brand, two customer journeys</h3><p>I established a shared approach to visual identity, typography, interface consistency and contact presentation.</p><p>At the same time, I developed separate service architectures tailored to the two specializations.</p><p>This allowed:</p><ul><li>More specific service descriptions.</li><li>Clearer customer journeys.</li><li>Independent local search positioning.</li><li>More relevant project presentation.</li><li>Distinct information hierarchies.</li><li>Service-specific inquiry pathways.</li></ul><p>The objective was not to create two unrelated brands.</p><p>It was to give each specialization enough independence to communicate effectively while maintaining the connection to ARCED Construction Group.</p>"],["ARCED Construction — Concrete Services Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedconstruction.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>The construction website was designed to present ARCED's concrete services to residential and commercial customers in the Winnipeg market.</p><p>Its primary responsibility was to communicate the company's capabilities and provide a straightforward path toward a project inquiry.</p><p>Concrete construction involves practical decisions that may depend on location, surface type, dimensions, preparation requirements and the intended use of the finished structure.</p><p>The information architecture therefore needed to focus on recognizable service categories rather than abstract descriptions of construction expertise.</p><h3>Service-oriented architecture</h3><p>I structured the content around concrete-related work, with service categories covering areas such as:</p><ul><li>Concrete driveways.</li><li>Foundations.</li><li>Patios.</li><li>Sidewalks and walkways.</li><li>Garage floors.</li><li>Concrete steps.</li><li>Concrete slabs.</li><li>Concrete replacement and preparation work.</li></ul><p>These categories helped translate a broad construction specialization into specific services customers could recognize.</p><h3>User experience considerations</h3><p>The design needed to support visitors who might have limited technical knowledge.</p><p>Rather than requiring users to understand construction methods before making an inquiry, the website presents the company's work through customer-relevant applications.</p><p>The experience emphasizes:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Service clarity*</em></p><p>Visitors should be able to identify whether the company handles their type of project.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project context*</em></p><p>Construction imagery and project-oriented content help communicate the nature of the work.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Process transparency*</em></p><p>Explaining the steps involved in arranging construction work can reduce uncertainty before an inquiry.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Contact accessibility*</em></p><p>Estimate requests and contact actions need to remain easy to locate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local relevance*</em></p><p>Content should connect the services to the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Conversion strategy</h3><p>The website is structured to guide visitors from understanding a service toward initiating contact.</p><p>The primary journey is:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Discover a Service → Understand the Scope → Evaluate the Company → Request an Estimate*</em></p><p>This approach turns service information into a practical path toward a business inquiry.</p><p>The objective was to create an accessible commercial website, not a complex customer portal that would introduce unnecessary steps.</p>"],["ARCED Tile — Specialist Installation Website","<p><strong>Website:</strong> https://arcedtile.ca</p><h3>Product objective</h3><p>ARCED Tile focuses on professional tile installation for residential and commercial interiors.</p><p>Its website needed to explain a more visually detailed service category while supporting users at different stages of renovation planning.</p><p>Tile installation customers may already know the room they want to renovate, but not the precise service terminology.</p><p>Someone looking to improve a bathroom may need information about floor tiles, shower walls, waterproofing or existing tile removal.</p><p>The information architecture needed to accommodate both general customer intentions and specific installation requirements.</p><h3>Service architecture</h3><p>I organized the interface around recognizable categories, including:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Bathroom Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting bathroom-related installation work and finishing considerations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Kitchen Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Covering kitchen surfaces and installations that need to accommodate cabinetry, counters and daily use.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Backsplashes*</em></p><p>Highlighting decorative and practical tile installation for kitchens and other interiors.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Floor Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Communicating flooring applications involving different tile types and surface conditions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Wall Tile Installation*</em></p><p>Presenting installation work for walls and decorative surfaces.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Shower Tile &amp; Waterproofing*</em></p><p>Explaining the relationship between installation quality, preparation and water protection.</p><p>Additional content addresses tile removal, replacement, repairs and different material requirements.</p><h3>Information hierarchy</h3><p>The website guides visitors from broad service recognition toward greater detail.</p><p>The primary page structure includes:</p><ul><li>Clear service positioning.</li><li>Service categories.</li><li>Supporting explanations.</li><li>Reasons to consider the company.</li><li>Installation process.</li><li>Project presentation.</li><li>Pricing guidance.</li><li>Frequently asked questions.</li><li>Estimate request options.</li><li>Contact information.</li></ul><p>This sequence reflects the different stages of a potential customer's decision.</p><p>A visitor who already understands the service can proceed toward an estimate.</p><p>A visitor who needs more information can explore the process, project examples and practical considerations before contacting the company.</p>"],["Designing the Customer Journey","<p>Both websites were designed around a common commercial objective: helping relevant visitors become informed prospective customers.</p><p>However, I did not approach conversion as simply placing more buttons on a page.</p><p>A successful inquiry experience requires users to understand what they are requesting.</p><h3>Stage 01. Recognition</h3><p>The visitor identifies the relevant service and confirms that the business operates in the appropriate location.</p><h3>Stage 02. Exploration</h3><p>The visitor reviews service information and understands the broad scope of the work.</p><h3>Stage 03. Evaluation</h3><p>Supporting content helps clarify the installation process, project considerations and company presentation.</p><h3>Stage 04. Inquiry</h3><p>The user is given a clear opportunity to request an estimate or contact the company.</p><h3>Stage 05. Business Follow-Up</h3><p>The website supports the transition from digital inquiry to direct communication, where project-specific requirements can be assessed.</p><p>The interfaces were designed to support this progression without forcing every visitor through an identical sequence.</p><p>Clear navigation and repeated contextual contact opportunities make the experience more flexible.</p>"],["Lead Generation & Estimate Requests","<p>For a construction business, the website's commercial value is closely connected to the quality and clarity of customer inquiries.</p><p>The design therefore needed to support users who were ready to discuss a project as well as visitors who still required more information.</p><p>I incorporated enquiry-oriented structures and contact functionality into the websites.</p><h3>Clear calls to action</h3><p>Estimate requests are presented in relation to the services being explored.</p><p>This allows a visitor to act when they have enough context rather than requiring them to return to the top of the page or search for contact details.</p><h3>Practical information collection</h3><p>A useful construction inquiry generally benefits from information about the project type, location, dimensions, requirements and contact details.</p><p>The website's estimate-oriented forms are designed to support this kind of initial communication.</p><h3>Balancing information and friction</h3><p>A form that asks too little may produce an inquiry lacking the details required for a useful response.</p><p>A form that asks too much may discourage a potential customer from completing it.</p><p>The objective was to maintain an understandable entry point to the estimate process while leaving detailed project assessment to direct follow-up.</p><p>The websites do not attempt to replace the contractor's professional evaluation.</p><p>They create a structured starting point for that conversation.</p>"],["Pricing Guidance & Cost Estimation","<p>One of the recurring challenges in construction services is pricing uncertainty.</p><p>Customers may want to understand the approximate cost of a project before contacting a contractor.</p><p>However, installation prices depend on multiple variables, including materials, project size, preparation, complexity and site conditions.</p><p>ARCED Tile includes a cost-estimation entry point as part of its customer experience.</p><h3>UX objective</h3><p>The purpose of preliminary pricing guidance is to help visitors understand the factors that influence a quote.</p><p>It is not to present an automated estimate as a guaranteed final contract price.</p><h3>Design considerations</h3><p>The experience needs to communicate:</p><ul><li>The type of work being considered.</li><li>Relevant project dimensions or scope.</li><li>Material and installation considerations.</li><li>Preparation requirements.</li><li>Why final pricing may require further assessment.</li></ul><p>By making pricing factors more understandable, the website can help visitors approach a conversation with more realistic expectations.</p><p>This supports the broader goal of transparent communication while preserving the role of an accurate project-specific estimate.</p>"],["Trust, Credibility & Service Communication","<p>Construction customers frequently evaluate a business before contacting it.</p><p>Unlike a low-cost online purchase, a renovation or concrete installation may involve significant expense, disruption and reliance on a contractor's professional workmanship.</p><p>For this reason, credibility was an important part of the content strategy.</p><h3>Communicating capabilities</h3><p>Service explanations and project-oriented imagery give visitors a clearer understanding of the work the company offers.</p><h3>Explaining the process</h3><p>Process content helps make the transition from initial inquiry to completed installation more understandable.</p><h3>Addressing uncertainty</h3><p>Frequently asked questions provide space to clarify common concerns about materials, service areas, installation requirements and estimates.</p><h3>Presenting business commitments</h3><p>The Tile website communicates company-stated information about insurance, a two-year workmanship warranty and the availability of residential and commercial services.</p><p>These details are presented as business information rather than independent certifications or guarantees of project outcomes.</p><h3>Avoiding artificial social proof</h3><p>Credibility should come from verifiable business information, actual services, relevant project materials and clear communication.</p><p>The case study does not claim a measured increase in trust or rely on invented customer testimonials.</p><p>The design objective was to give prospective customers enough relevant information to assess the business for themselves.</p>"],["Local SEO & Content Architecture","<p>ARCED operates in a geographically defined service market.</p><p>Its websites therefore needed to communicate not only what the company does, but also where those services are available.</p><p>I incorporated local search considerations into the content planning and page architecture.</p><h3>Service-specific search intent</h3><p>Concrete services and tile installation generate different search intentions.</p><p>The content strategy distinguished terms associated with the two specializations rather than treating all construction-related searches as interchangeable.</p><p>Examples of relevant search categories include:</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Concrete*</em></p><p>Concrete contractor, concrete driveways, patios, foundations, sidewalks and related installation services in Winnipeg.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Tile*</em></p><p>Tile installation, bathroom tile, kitchen backsplash, floor tile, shower tile and related services in Winnipeg.</p><h3>Content organization</h3><p>The websites use clear service positioning and descriptive content to support visitors searching for particular types of work.</p><p>The planning included attention to:</p><ul><li>Service-specific headings.</li><li>Descriptive page titles.</li><li>Local market relevance.</li><li>Logical content hierarchy.</li><li>Internal navigation.</li><li>Relevant service explanations.</li><li>Contact and estimate entry points.</li><li>Responsive presentation.</li></ul><h3>UX and SEO alignment</h3><p>The objective was not to fill pages with repeated keywords.</p><p>It was to make important services understandable to both visitors and search engines.</p><p>A well-structured service page should answer a recognizable customer question while clearly communicating its subject.</p><p>This approach supports search-oriented content without sacrificing readability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*The project established an SEO-oriented website structure. It does not claim verified Google rankings, organic traffic growth or search performance improvements without supporting analytics.*</em></p>"],["Visual Identity & Art Direction","<p>The two websites needed a visual language appropriate for a professional construction business.</p><p>The design direction prioritized clarity, confidence and consistency.</p><p>The objective was to communicate a dependable commercial identity without making the interfaces visually generic.</p><h3>Shared brand foundation</h3><p>ARCED Construction and ARCED Tile were designed to remain recognizable as parts of one business.</p><p>This required consistent treatment of:</p><ul><li>Brand assets.</li><li>Typography.</li><li>Layout principles.</li><li>Navigation patterns.</li><li>Calls to action.</li><li>Contact information.</li><li>Interface components.</li></ul><h3>Brand asset preparation</h3><p>I prepared and vectorized existing brand materials for more consistent digital use.</p><p>This included working with the existing ARCED identity rather than claiming authorship of the original brand concept.</p><p>The goal was to ensure that the available assets could be used clearly and consistently across different screen sizes and interface contexts.</p><h3>Service-specific presentation</h3><p>While maintaining a shared identity, the two websites allow their subject matter to influence the visual presentation.</p><p>Concrete services benefit from clear construction-oriented content and practical project information.</p><p>Tile installation benefits from detailed visual examples, material presentation and attention to finishes.</p><p>The design system needed enough flexibility to accommodate both without losing consistency.</p>"],["Responsive UX/UI Design","<p>The websites were designed for desktop, tablet and mobile experiences.</p><p>Responsive design was an important requirement because construction customers may access a contractor's website in different contexts.</p><p>Someone might browse services from a computer while planning a renovation.</p><p>Another visitor might open the website on a phone while comparing contractors or preparing to request an estimate.</p><p>The interface needed to remain understandable across those situations.</p><h3>Responsive navigation</h3><p>Navigation structures were adapted to preserve access to important content on smaller screens.</p><h3>Service presentation</h3><p>Service descriptions and supporting imagery were organized to maintain clear hierarchy without requiring desktop-sized layouts.</p><h3>Contact accessibility</h3><p>Inquiry and contact actions needed to remain accessible on mobile devices.</p><h3>Content readability</h3><p>Typography, spacing and component proportions were designed to support scanning and comprehension.</p><h3>Interaction consistency</h3><p>Interactive elements needed to behave predictably across different devices.</p><p>The objective was to preserve the same essential customer journey rather than simply shrink the desktop interface.</p>"],["Content Design & UX Writing","<p>A technically accurate service description is not automatically a useful one.</p><p>Construction terminology can be unfamiliar to customers who are planning their first renovation or concrete project.</p><p>I therefore treated content structure as part of the user experience.</p><h3>Clear service naming</h3><p>Service categories were organized around recognizable customer needs.</p><h3>Practical explanations</h3><p>Supporting descriptions communicate what the work involves and where it may be relevant.</p><h3>Action-oriented language</h3><p>Calls to action were designed to make the next step understandable.</p><h3>Frequently asked questions</h3><p>FAQ content provides additional detail without requiring every visitor to read long explanations before identifying the service they need.</p><h3>Consistent tone</h3><p>The business presentation was developed to sound professional, direct and approachable.</p><p>This helped connect the brand's visual identity to its written communication.</p><p>Content design was an important part of the project because the websites needed to function as commercial communication tools, not merely visual portfolios.</p>"],["Front-End Development & Technical Delivery","<p>My involvement continued beyond visual design into the implementation of the production websites.</p><p>I independently managed the transition from the planned information architecture and interface direction to responsive, functioning web experiences.</p><h3>Development scope</h3><p>The work included:</p><ul><li>Implementing page structures.</li><li>Developing responsive layouts.</li><li>Creating reusable interface patterns.</li><li>Integrating visual assets.</li><li>Building interactive components.</li><li>Implementing navigation behavior.</li><li>Connecting contact and inquiry functionality.</li><li>Preparing customer-facing content.</li><li>Supporting basic technical SEO requirements.</li><li>Testing different viewport sizes.</li><li>Preparing and delivering the published websites.</li></ul><h3>Two independent digital properties</h3><p>The main technical and organizational consideration was maintaining separate websites with their own content and navigation while preserving a consistent business identity.</p><p>Each site needed to function independently for its relevant audience.</p><p>At the same time, both needed to feel connected through the ARCED brand.</p><h3>Independent project ownership</h3><p>I was responsible for coordinating the work across requirements, design decisions, implementation and final delivery.</p><p>This meant evaluating the websites as complete products rather than treating design and development as unrelated stages.</p><p>Decisions about navigation affected content organization.</p><p>Decisions about service categories affected search-oriented structure.</p><p>Decisions about estimate requests affected interface hierarchy.</p><p>Maintaining responsibility across those relationships helped preserve consistency between the original objectives and the implemented websites.</p>"],["Key Design Decisions","<p>Several decisions were particularly important to the project's direction.</p><h3>Decision 01. Separate the two specializations</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Concrete construction and tile installation involve different customer needs and search intentions.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Develop two independent service-focused websites under a connected brand identity.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Give each specialization enough space to communicate clearly without creating unnecessary competition between unrelated service categories.</p><h3>Decision 02. Prioritize services over generic brand messaging</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors looking for a contractor usually want to confirm practical service relevance quickly.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Place clear service positioning and recognizable service categories prominently within the experience.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Help users establish whether the company offers the work they need before asking them to explore secondary content.</p><h3>Decision 03. Connect information with inquiry actions</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Visitors may decide to contact the company at different points in their evaluation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Integrate estimate and contact opportunities into the relevant service journeys.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Make the transition from understanding a service to requesting information more direct.</p><h3>Decision 04. Use a consistent but flexible visual system</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Two independent websites can easily feel unrelated or become visually repetitive.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Retain shared brand patterns while adapting content and presentation to each specialization.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Preserve business recognition without sacrificing the clarity of individual services.</p><h3>Decision 05. Make local relevance explicit</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction services are geographically constrained, and customers need to identify appropriate providers.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Structure content around relevant services and the Winnipeg market.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Connect customer search intent with clear service and location information.</p><h3>Decision 06. Explain pricing without promising false precision</h3><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Problem*</em></p><p>Construction costs depend on conditions that may not be fully known during an initial website visit.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Design Decision*</em></p><p>Support preliminary pricing understanding while maintaining a clear route toward a project-specific estimate.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Rationale*</em></p><p>Improve transparency without presenting an automated calculation as a binding professional quote.</p>"],["Project Challenges & Problem-Solving","<h3>Maintaining distinction without fragmentation</h3><p>The first challenge was making the concrete and tile websites feel independent enough to serve different audiences while retaining a shared business identity.</p><p>The solution was to separate their content architecture while maintaining common visual and interaction principles.</p><h3>Balancing technical information and accessibility</h3><p>Construction services require specific explanations, but overly technical content can create unnecessary friction.</p><p>The interface therefore needed to communicate practical service applications before expanding into specialized details.</p><h3>Designing for commercial intent</h3><p>The websites were not intended as purely artistic digital experiences.</p><p>Visual choices needed to support comprehension, credibility and clear inquiry pathways.</p><p>This required evaluating design decisions against the practical goals of the business.</p><h3>Supporting different devices</h3><p>Responsive implementation required adapting information density, navigation and interaction patterns without losing access to important content.</p><h3>Coordinating design and development</h3><p>Managing the websites independently meant ensuring that the implemented result reflected the intended UX, rather than treating development as a separate final step.</p><p>These challenges reinforced the importance of approaching commercial web projects as connected systems of content, design and functionality.</p>"],["Before & After","<p>The project established a dedicated digital presence for two service directions within ARCED Construction Group.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Before the Project</th><th>Delivered Direction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Digital presence</td><td>No established dedicated website</td><td>Two published commercial websites</td></tr><tr><td>Service positioning</td><td>Services needed clear online separation</td><td>Distinct concrete and tile experiences</td></tr><tr><td>Information architecture</td><td>No dedicated digital service structure</td><td>Organized, service-oriented navigation</td></tr><tr><td>Visual consistency</td><td>Existing brand materials requiring digital preparation</td><td>Coordinated visual presentation</td></tr><tr><td>Customer inquiry</td><td>Direct contact without a structured website journey</td><td>Dedicated inquiry and estimate pathways</td></tr><tr><td>Local discovery</td><td>No dedicated website architecture</td><td>Winnipeg-oriented service content</td></tr><tr><td>Device support</td><td>No existing responsive website experience</td><td>Responsive desktop, tablet and mobile interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Service information</td><td>No centralized online presentation</td><td>Structured explanations and supporting content</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This comparison describes the delivered digital capabilities rather than a measured increase in sales or customer acquisition.</p>"],["Results & Deliverables","<p>The project resulted in two independently published commercial websites.</p><h3>ARCED Construction</h3><p>A dedicated online experience presenting the concrete construction specialization through service-oriented information architecture, responsive design and customer inquiry pathways.</p><h3>ARCED Tile</h3><p>A specialist tile installation website combining structured service content, project presentation, process explanations, pricing guidance, frequently asked questions and estimate request functionality.</p><h3>Shared brand presentation</h3><p>The sites maintain a recognizable relationship through consistent brand assets, visual principles and interface patterns.</p><h3>Digital customer journeys</h3><p>Both websites provide a structured route from service discovery to contacting the business.</p><h3>Local content foundation</h3><p>The content architecture supports clear communication of services within the Winnipeg market.</p><h3>Responsive implementation</h3><p>The interfaces were implemented for use across different screen sizes and interaction contexts.</p><h3>Production delivery</h3><p>Both websites were developed and published as accessible commercial properties.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Project outcomes should be evaluated accurately.*</em></p><p>The confirmed deliverables are two websites, their content and interface systems, their inquiry pathways and their production implementation.</p><p>The available project information does not establish verified changes in conversion rates, lead volume, search rankings or revenue.</p><p>These would require post-launch analytics and a defined measurement period.</p>"],["My Individual Contribution","<p>ARCED demonstrates my ability to independently manage commercial website projects across strategy, design and implementation.</p><p>My work included understanding business requirements, researching the relevant market, defining the information architecture, establishing visual direction, preparing brand assets and developing the production websites.</p><p>Rather than working only on isolated interface screens, I was responsible for shaping the complete digital experience around the business's services and customer needs.</p><p>The project required competency in several connected areas.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Business &amp; Requirements Analysis*</em></p><p>Understanding the company's service structure, customer groups, geographic market and commercial objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Strategy*</em></p><p>Designing separate customer journeys for two distinct service specializations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Information Architecture*</em></p><p>Organizing service content, navigation and inquiry pathways.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX/UI Design*</em></p><p>Developing responsive interfaces that balance visual quality with practical usability.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Brand Adaptation*</em></p><p>Preparing existing identity assets for a consistent digital presentation.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*UX Writing &amp; Content Strategy*</em></p><p>Presenting construction services through clear, customer-oriented explanations.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Local SEO Planning*</em></p><p>Structuring content around relevant service categories and geographic search intent.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Front-End Development*</em></p><p>Implementing responsive layouts, interactive components and functional web experiences.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Testing &amp; Delivery*</em></p><p>Preparing the websites for publication and maintaining responsibility for the final implementation.</p><p>The defining aspect of my contribution was continuity across these responsibilities.</p><p>I was not simply producing visual layouts for someone else to interpret.</p><p>I managed the design and development process as a complete commercial deliverable.</p>"],["Reflection","<p>ARCED was an important exercise in translating a traditional service business into a purposeful digital experience.</p><p>The project demonstrated that effective commercial web design begins with understanding how customers make decisions.</p><p>A construction website does not need to overwhelm visitors with visual effects.</p><p>It needs to communicate relevant services, establish a professional presentation, explain important details and make the next action clear.</p><p>The most significant strategic decision was treating concrete construction and tile installation as two different customer journeys rather than forcing them into one generic interface.</p><p>This required both consistency and restraint.</p><p>The business identity needed to remain recognizable, but the content architecture had to respect the differences between the two specializations.</p><p>The project also reinforced the importance of treating content, UX, visual design and technical implementation as connected disciplines.</p><p>The value of the websites came from how those elements worked together.</p><p>My individual responsibility extended from the initial business requirements to the published digital products.</p><p>ARCED represents my ability to independently design, develop and deliver commercial websites for an international client, adapting the experience to different services, customer intentions and business objectives.</p><p class=\"case-note\"><em>*Two specialized websites, one recognizable business identity, and a digital experience built around the way real customers search for construction services.*</em></p>"]],"images":["assets/commercial-covers/arced-construction.png","assets/commercial-covers/arced-tile.png"]}}};